window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol2/06_distributed_training"] = {"key": "vol2/06_distributed_training", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Why Distribution Is Necessary"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "The Parallelism Design Space"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Parallelism strategy selection and engineering trade-offs"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 2, "title": "Data Parallelism"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Data parallelism implementation"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 3, "title": "Data parallelism trade-offs"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "Memory-efficient data parallelism with ZeRO and FSDP"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 2, "title": "Scaling Efficiency and Convergence"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "The mathematics of scaling efficiency"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 3, "title": "Scaling physics and Amdahl's Law with communication"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 3, "title": "Convergence rate for synchronous data parallel SGD"}, {"id": "sec-13", "level": 3, "title": "Staleness impact across synchronization models"}, {"id": "sec-14", "level": 3, "title": "Learning rate scaling rules"}, {"id": "sec-15", "level": 3, "title": "Critical batch size and diminishing returns"}, {"id": "sec-16", "level": 3, "title": "Communication cost versus convergence speed"}, {"id": "sec-17", "level": 3, "title": "FP8 for distributed training"}, {"id": "sec-18", "level": 2, "title": "Model Parallelism"}, {"id": "sec-19", "level": 3, "title": "Layer-wise partitioning"}, {"id": "sec-20", "level": 3, "title": "Pipeline parallelism"}, {"id": "sec-21", "level": 3, "title": "Tensor parallelism"}, {"id": "sec-22", "level": 3, "title": "Sequence and context parallelism"}, {"id": "sec-23", "level": 3, "title": "Expert parallelism (mixture of experts)"}, {"id": "sec-24", "level": 3, "title": "Sparse embedding sharding"}, {"id": "sec-25", "level": 2, "title": "Hybrid Parallelism"}, {"id": "sec-26", "level": 3, "title": "The 3D training loop"}, {"id": "sec-27", "level": 3, "title": "Hybrid-parallelism worked example"}, {"id": "sec-28", "level": 2, "title": "Multi-Model Training for Alignment and RLHF"}, {"id": "sec-29", "level": 3, "title": "The multi-model coordination problem"}, {"id": "sec-30", "level": 3, "title": "Infrastructure asymmetry between training and inference models"}, {"id": "sec-31", "level": 3, "title": "Resource requirements for PPO versus DPO"}, {"id": "sec-32", "level": 3, "title": "Sequence length variance and batching challenges"}, {"id": "sec-33", "level": 2, "title": "Parallelism Strategy Comparison"}, {"id": "sec-34", "level": 2, "title": "Frameworks and Runtime Contracts"}, {"id": "sec-35", "level": 2, "title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls"}, {"id": "sec-36", "level": 2, "title": "Summary"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Distributed Training</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can adding accelerators make training slower?</em></p>\n<p>Splitting a training job across machines creates more compute capacity, but it also requires those machines to exchange state and agree on updates. Each accelerator may finish its local work sooner while spending more of the step waiting for other workers. Once communication and waiting consume the time saved by parallel execution, adding accelerators raises the bill without shortening the run. Single-node optimization remains necessary, yet faster local arithmetic cannot remove a dependency on data that another machine has not delivered. Memory pressure complicates the decision, because a model may require distribution simply to fit, even when the resulting exchanges are expensive. Dividing its state across more devices can relieve that limit while exposing another in network bandwidth or scheduling. Conversely, replicating a model can process more examples at once without reducing the memory needed for each copy. Neither improvement alone establishes that training will finish sooner. Changing how examples are grouped or when updates become available can change the work required to reach the target quality. Engineers must therefore compare complete training configurations, accounting for memory, communication, update behavior, and elapsed time together. The practical question is where additional hardware buys useful progress and where it merely creates more coordination. That judgment determines which models and experiments fit the available schedule and budget. In C³ terms, each partitioning strategy trades parallelized compute against network-bound communication and coordination across workers.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Apply the Fleet Law to diagnose compute-, memory-, communication-, or coordination-bound distributed training regimes</li>\n<li>Calculate scaling efficiency, estimate useful batch size, and budget synchronization overhead for data-parallel training</li>\n<li>Design memory-sharding plans that trade accelerator capacity for additional communication</li>\n<li>Map tensor, pipeline, sequence, expert, and data parallelism onto hardware bandwidth tiers and model structure</li>\n<li>Analyze microbatch pipeline schedules for bubble overhead, activation residency, and synchronous update ordering</li>\n<li>Evaluate synchronization and low-precision policies under SGD equivalence, staleness, straggler, and numerical-stability constraints</li>\n<li>Synthesize hybrid parallelism configurations for frontier, recommendation, and alignment training constraints</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Why Distribution Is Necessary</h2>\n<p>A GPT-3-class 175B-parameter reference model cannot load onto a single accelerator, because its FP16 weights alone exceed the memory of the device that must hold them. The empirical scaling law (principle \\ref{pri-universal-scaling}) keeps pushing frontier models further past what one machine can store or finish in calendar time. The accelerator hierarchy, network fabric, and storage pipeline (@sec-data-storage) form the physical foundation of the fleet, and @sec-collective-communication priced the collective operations that move state across it. The remaining challenge is deciding how to partition the training job: which intermediate results and gradients must cross that fabric, and when workers must synchronize, while preserving the optimization process a single device would have followed.</p>\n<p>↩ The power-law relationship between quality and compute, data, and parameters, and the compute-optimal allocation it implies, are established in @sec-vol2-intro-ai-scaling-laws-a043.</p>\n<p>In the fleet stack (@fig-fleet-stack), distributed training occupies the Distributed Execution row of the Distribution band, the logic that partitions the <em>mathematical</em> workload across the <em>physical</em> fleet. Data, tensor, pipeline, and hybrid parallelism create different traffic patterns for the interconnect to carry, and selecting among them requires evaluating the reference workload against finite high-bandwidth memory (HBM) capacity, compute throughput, and input bandwidth.</p>\n<p>Three distinct limits push a training workload past a single accelerator. Memory exhaustion hits first when model parameters, optimizer states, and activation storage exceed single-device capacity. For full mixed-precision training with Adaptive Moment Estimation (Adam), the convention of <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.bytes_per_param_str]</code> bytes per parameter (FP16 weights and gradients, an FP32 master weight, and two FP32 Adam moments) can exceed an <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.h100_mem_gb_str]</code> accelerator at roughly <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.crossover_params_str]</code> parameters before activations. Larger 10--20B models therefore need sharding, offload to a slower memory tier, or another memory-saving approach [@rajbhandari2020]. The capacity limit alone does not choose among them.</p>\n<p>↪ Accelerator HBM capacity, the ceiling these per-parameter budgets are measured against, is developed in @sec-appdx-dam-taxonomy-memory-hierarchy.</p>\n<p>Even when a model fits in memory, unacceptable training duration can still force distribution because a single device may require weeks or months to converge, turning wall-clock time itself into a systems constraint. GPT-3's 175B-parameter training run used a cluster of V100 GPUs [@brown2020language], illustrating why the calendar, alongside HBM capacity, drives scale-out.</p>\n<p>Input demand at dataset scale adds a third pressure. Dataset bytes govern storage capacity, whereas the training schedule governs the required delivery rate. As @sec-storage-pipeline-equation showed, either can motivate distributed data services, although a large dataset alone does not require distributing model computation. Together, capacity, calendar, and input delivery define the constraints a training configuration must satisfy.</p>\n<p>The first signal, memory exhaustion, has a long history, traced in @fig-memory-wall-trend. Over the plotted span, single-accelerator memory grew about <code>[MemoryWallTrend.gpu_growth_mult_str]</code> from the <code>[MemoryWallTrend.first_gpu_str]</code> to the <code>[MemoryWallTrend.last_gpu_str]</code>, while the memory needed to train a state-of-the-art model grew by roughly <code>[MemoryWallTrend.demand_orders_str]</code> orders of magnitude. Among the plotted models, the curves cross in <code>[MemoryWallTrend.crossover_year_str]</code> at GPT-3, whose training state exceeded the largest accelerator then available by about <code>[MemoryWallTrend.crossover_gap_mult_str]</code>. GPT-3 marks where the gap passed an order of magnitude, and every later plotted frontier model also sits far above the capacity curve. For those models, spreading state beyond one device's memory is a precondition for training at all rather than a way to train faster.</p>\n<p>Whichever signal motivates distribution, the system must state which optimization update it implements. The synchronous baseline is developed first. In it, the <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_gpus_label_str]</code> of the volume's Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) reference cluster combine their contributions before advancing to the next parameter version. The archetype names a class of workload, and the running instance of it in this chapter is a GPT-3-class <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.params_b_str]</code>-parameter dense transformer. Matching the global batch, loss normalization, and optimizer state preserves the corresponding single-device update mathematically. Later synchronization models deliberately relax that update ordering and must be evaluated under their own convergence assumptions.</p>\n<p>↩ The three systems archetypes and their limiting constraints are established in @sec-vol2-introduction-archetypes.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Distributed training</strong></em> partitions the optimization loop across multiple compute nodes, distributing data or model computation and coordinating contributions according to a specified update protocol.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Partitioning training state across devices can meet a memory budget that exceeds one accelerator. GPT-3 (<code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.params_b_str]</code> parameters) requires approximately <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.weights_gb_str]</code> in BF16 (FP16 also stores 2 bytes per value, so the chapter's byte counts hold for either format), more than <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.weights_over_h100_mult_str]</code> the <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.h100_mem_gb_str]</code> capacity of a single H100. If weights remain entirely in HBM, their aggregate capacity lower bound is <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.min_h100_weights_str]</code>. Adding BF16 gradients plus FP32 Adam master weights and moments gives about <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.training_state_tb_str]</code>, or at least <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.min_h100_state_str]</code> before activations. These lower bounds do not establish a runnable placement; offload changes the capacity calculation, and the time budget can require more accelerators than memory alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: CPU threads can share a coherent address space, while accelerators generally hold separate training-state allocations even within one node. Distributed training extends coordination across those devices and node boundaries through collectives or point-to-point transfers governed by the chosen update protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that distributed training scales linearly with node count. In practice, communication overhead grows with cluster size and the serial fraction of each step (Amdahl's Law): a fixed serial fraction of the single-device baseline bounds strong-scaling speedup.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Distribution expands three costs that determine whether a partition is viable. Communication overhead includes gradient aggregation through AllReduce, which returns the same reduced tensor to each participant, or rank. For $\\Parameters$ parameters across $\\DeviceCount$ devices, a ring AllReduce, in which each worker passes gradient chunks only to its neighbor in a logical ring, sends approximately $2(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount\\times \\GradientPayload$ bytes per worker per step. Here the gradient payload $\\GradientPayload$ is the $\\Parameters$ gradient values times their storage width. The ring volume counts one worker's traffic rather than total fleet traffic.</p>\n<p>↩ The ring schedule and the derivation of its $2(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount$ volume are established in @sec-collective-communication.</p>\n<p>Fault tolerance becomes more demanding as the number of failure sources grows. Under independent constant-rate failures, <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.reliability_gpu_count_str]</code> GPUs that each have a mean time to failure (MTTF) of <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.gpu_mtbf_hours_str]</code> produce a GPU failure about every <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.cluster_mtbf_hours_str]</code>, the cluster's mean time between failures (MTBF). Whether each event interrupts training depends on recovery and redundancy; @sec-appdx-reliability-foundations-mtbf-cascade develops the assumptions.</p>\n<p>Algorithmic stability adds a separate constraint when the partition changes global batch size or update ordering. Learning-rate scaling and warmup are candidate responses to large-batch behavior [@goyal2017accurate], not requirements unique to multiple machines. A viable design must keep all three costs within budget.</p>\n<p>Communication and coordination costs enter every step through the Fleet Law (principle \\ref{pri-distributed-step-time}), which decomposes the per-step time:</p>\n<p>$$ \\DistributedStepTime(\\DeviceCount) = \\frac{\\IronLawComputeTime}{\\DeviceCount} + \\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount) + \\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount) - \\OverlappedTime $$</p>\n<p>Each step takes the single-device compute time divided among the devices, plus the time spent moving data, plus the time spent waiting on coordination such as barriers and stragglers, minus whatever part of that time stays hidden behind computation.</p>\n<p>↩ The Fleet Law and its compute, communication, and coordination terms are established in @sec-vol2-introduction-fleet-law.</p>\n<p>The <strong>communication-computation ratio</strong>, $\\CommunicationComputeRatio= \\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)/(\\IronLawComputeTime/\\DeviceCount)$, compares communication time with ideally partitioned compute time for fixed global work. It is the time consequence of communication intensity $\\CommunicationIntensity$ (@eq-ci-ratio), the bytes moved per operation of the workload. When transfers run at link bandwidth $\\LinkBandwidth$ and each device computes at sustained rate $\\PeakRate\\EfficiencyHw$, $\\CommunicationComputeRatio\\approx \\CommunicationIntensity \\times \\PeakRate\\EfficiencyHw/\\LinkBandwidth$, so the same workload becomes more communication-bound on faster accelerators or slower links. This ratio identifies the pressure to overlap communication; the complete step time also depends on how much exchange is hidden and how much coordination remains exposed.</p>\n<p>↩ The $\\MessageStartupLatency$-$\\LinkBandwidth$ message model, with startup latency $\\MessageStartupLatency$ and sustained bandwidth $\\LinkBandwidth$, is established in @sec-network-fabrics-performance-model.</p>\n<p>↪ The communication-computation ratio, evaluated for representative data-parallel and point-to-point transfers and related to communication intensity, is developed in @sec-appdx-fleet-foundations-comm-compute-ratio.</p>\n<p>Two regimes follow from this ratio. When $\\IronLawComputeTime/\\DeviceCount\\gg \\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$, the cluster is compute-bound, and communication is small relative to useful computation, provided coordination and input delivery do not introduce another limit. Large-batch training of a dense convolutional model such as ResNet commonly sits here. When $\\CommunicationComputeRatio\\gtrsim 1$, $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ matches or exceeds $\\IronLawComputeTime/\\DeviceCount$ and the cluster is communication-bound. Insufficient overlap then leaves substantial time waiting for gradients or activations. Large language models (LLMs) with billions of parameters to synchronize frequently land in this regime. Deep Learning Recommendation Model (DLRM)-style workloads also load the network through their embedding exchanges, but memory capacity and lookup rate often limit them first (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-parameter-servers-embedding-sharding-8821). These three classifications are typical operating points rather than fixed labels. Batch size, partitioning, and placement can move the same workload across the boundary, so the ratio must be measured for the configuration at hand. Where a workload lands also depends on how its training loop is cut across devices, the choice each parallelism strategy makes.</p>\n<p>↪ The optimizations that help, and the ones that are wasted, for a compute-bound, communication-bound, or coordination-bound fleet are collected for reference in @sec-appdx-c3-taxonomy-bottleneck-table.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Parallelism Design Space</h2>\n<p>Once a model or its training time outgrows a single GPU, the training loop offers three nested axes to split across devices: the examples in a batch, the layers of the model, and the matrix operations inside each layer. Data parallelism assigns different examples to model replicas; pipeline parallelism assigns successive layer groups to different devices; tensor parallelism splits the operations inside a layer. Because every split adds inter-device communication and synchronization overhead on top of single-machine compute and memory limits (@sec-appdx-dam-taxonomy), profiling and design must treat these three strategies as <em>loop transformations</em>.</p>\n<p>In compiler terms, data parallelism acts like a parallel for-loop that assigns different batch indices to devices running the same model. Tensor parallelism resembles vectorization, or single instruction, multiple data (SIMD), by partitioning the work inside matrix operations. This SIMD comparison is an analogy for the work split rather than for a shared instruction stream or register file, since the devices still exchange tensor shards over an interconnect. Pipeline parallelism instead resembles instruction pipelining, assigning successive layer groups to stages so that overlapping microbatches, the slices of a batch that flow through the stages one after another, keep those stages active, subject to dependency delays and load balance.</p>\n<p>However the loop is split, distributed training spreads the workload across machines that must coordinate to train a single model. Coordination here means keeping every model shard or replica on a compatible training step. Basic barriers can keep a small research run ordered, but long-running training jobs also need timeout, checkpoint, and recovery mechanisms so one failed worker does not waste days of compute.</p>\n<p>↪ Checkpointing and recovery for long-running training jobs are developed in @sec-fault-tolerance-reliability.</p>\n<p>: <strong>DistBelief</strong>: Google's DistBelief (2012) was an early framework for training neural networks across thousands of machines, but its parameter server architecture created bandwidth bottlenecks at central nodes. This limitation drove the shift to decentralized AllReduce patterns in successors such as Horovod and PyTorch DistributedDataParallel (DDP)}, where each data-parallel worker sends and receives about $2(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount$ times the gradient size per step rather than concentrating traffic at a single server [@dean2012large; @sergeev2018horovod; @li2020pytorch; @patarasuk2009].</p>\n<p>Mapping those three loop transformations onto hardware follows the cluster's interconnect hierarchy. Within a single node, multiple GPUs coordinate across separate device memories over high-bandwidth NVLink or PCIe links, with collective libraries such as NCCL scheduling the transfers.</p>\n<p>: <strong>NVLink</strong>: NVIDIA's point-to-point GPU interconnect delivers <code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_a100_gb_per_s_str]</code>--<code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_h100_gb_per_s_str]</code> per-direction bandwidth, roughly <code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_hdr_ratio_low_mult_str]</code>--<code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_hdr_ratio_high_mult_str]</code> InfiniBand} HDR per port. This bandwidth gap is why tensor parallelism, which requires AllReduce on every layer, favors the fast NVLink domain, while pipeline and data parallelism can tolerate slower links when their traffic budgets fit. NVLink Switch systems can extend that fast domain beyond one server.</p>\n<p>Crossing node boundaries adds slower inter-node network links, fault-tolerance exposure, and cluster orchestration. Single-GPU kernel and memory efficiency must be tuned before crossing them, because any waste on one device is multiplied across the fleet. Matching the three loop splits to these intra-node and inter-node tiers decomposes the design space onto a three-dimensional grid, $\\DeviceCount= \\DataParallelDegree \\times \\PipelineParallelDegree \\times \\TensorParallelDegree$, that partitions cluster execution across tensor parallelism ($\\TensorParallelDegree$, intra-layer sharding), pipeline parallelism ($\\PipelineParallelDegree$, sequential stage partitioning), and data parallelism ($\\DataParallelDegree$, batch replication). Each cell of the grid is one accelerator coordinate (@fig-3d-parallelism-cube).</p>\n<p>Under the three-dimensional partitioning of @fig-3d-parallelism-cube, called <strong>3D parallelism</strong>, the total accelerator allocation scales as the product $\\DeviceCount= \\DataParallelDegree \\times \\PipelineParallelDegree \\times \\TensorParallelDegree$. The axes represent distinct mathematical partitions, but they are not independent configuration knobs. Tensor and pipeline degrees must divide the model structure, the data-parallel degree and microbatch schedule must produce a viable global batch, every partition must fit memory, and each communication group must map onto an adequate topology and acceptable failure domain. Training systems therefore select a jointly feasible coordinate rather than tuning $\\DataParallelDegree$, $\\PipelineParallelDegree$, and $\\TensorParallelDegree$ in isolation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Parallelism strategy selection and engineering trade-offs</h3>\n<p>A parallelism strategy that places frequent, high-volume exchanges on a slow link fails before it leaves the whiteboard, because communication stalls the accelerators it was meant to feed. Choosing a strategy is therefore a constraint satisfaction problem governed by parameter count $(\\Parameters)$, batch size $(\\BatchSize)$, and interconnect bandwidth. A first feasibility screen compares the traffic each strategy creates against the hardware topology (@tbl-parallelism-tradeoffs). In that screen, $\\LayerCount$ denotes layer count, $\\SequenceLength$ sequence length, and $\\HiddenDimension$ hidden width. The table identifies traffic sources rather than complete byte budgets; datatype width, partition degree, and communication frequency enter the worked mechanisms below.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Strategy</strong></th><th><strong>Communication pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Comm. volume</strong></th><th><strong>Hardware constraint</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Data Parallel (DP)</strong></td><td>AllReduce Gradients</td><td>$\\propto \\GradientPayload$ (gradient bytes)</td><td>Requires high bisection BW</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Tensor Parallel (TP)</strong></td><td>AllReduce Activations</td><td>$\\propto \\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension\\times \\LayerCount$ (per-layer activations)</td><td>Fast domain matched to layer traffic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Pipeline Parallel (PP)</strong></td><td>Point-to-Point (P2P)</td><td>$\\propto \\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension$ (Activations)</td><td>Link must sustain stage activations</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Parallelism Communication Costs</strong>: Tensor parallelism exchanges partial layer results frequently and favors a fast communication domain, which can span servers. Pipeline parallelism transfers boundary activations; its bandwidth need depends on tensor shape and microbatch schedule. Data parallelism synchronizes gradient bytes, whose exposed cost depends on overlap and topology. Memory sharding addresses capacity but does not automatically reduce communication. {#tbl-parallelism-tradeoffs}</p>\n<p>Those bandwidth requirements impose a hard constraint on hardware placement: each parallelism axis must map onto an interconnect tier that can sustain its traffic within the compute budget. A quick back-of-envelope bandwidth feasibility check catches a mismatch before any code is written.</p>\n<p>↩ The communication domains and bandwidth hierarchy used to evaluate placement are established in @sec-compute-bandwidth-hierarchy.</p>\n<p>Tensor parallelism leaves each device with part of a layer's output, and an AllReduce sums those partial outputs twice per transformer block in the forward pass, once after attention and once after the feed-forward network (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-tensor-parallelism-d76e). These frequent tensor-parallel collectives must fit the layer-time budget. The test compares the time one layer's local share of computation takes with the time its collectives take on each candidate link. For the forward pass of one GPT-3 layer split <code>[BandwidthFeasibilityNapkin.tp_str]</code> ways, processing one <code>[BandwidthFeasibilityNapkin.tokens_str]</code>-token sequence at half of an H100's dense FP16 peak:</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\\tcbbreak</code></pre>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Local compute per layer: about <code>[BandwidthFeasibilityNapkin.compute_ms_str]</code></li>\n<li>Tensor-parallel transfer per layer over NVLink: about <code>[BandwidthFeasibilityNapkin.nvlink_ms_str]</code></li>\n<li>The same transfer over one NDR InfiniBand port: about <code>[BandwidthFeasibilityNapkin.ib_ms_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>On NVLink the exchange is a fraction of the layer's compute; across InfiniBand it would take longer than the compute it serves. The amount hidden depends on independent work available in the execution schedule. NVLink is one suitable fast domain, but the test uses message bytes, exposed latency, and sustained link rate rather than an interconnect brand or chassis boundary. Cross-rack placement may favor pipeline or data parallelism when the available fabric cannot meet the tensor-parallel budget; their activation and gradient traffic must also be checked.</p>\n<p>The communication costs in @tbl-parallelism-tradeoffs already narrow the choice before any mechanism has been developed, because a strategy is viable only when its traffic fits the tier that must carry it. What each formula implies in practice depends on the mechanics of data, tensor, and pipeline parallelism developed over the rest of the chapter, and @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-parallelism-strategy-comparison-d92a turns the same constraints into a decision procedure once those mechanics are in place.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h2\">Data Parallelism</h2>\n<p>When a model fits on one accelerator but training takes weeks, replicating its computation lets several workers process examples concurrently. The initial data-parallel baseline gives each worker the same model state and a local portion of one global minibatch. Combining those local contributions before the optimizer update preserves the intended global-batch estimator; the sampling and weighting conditions below explain when that equivalence holds.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Data parallelism</strong></em> distributes training examples across workers that execute the model and combine their contributions to its optimization updates.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: With $\\DeviceCount$ workers each processing a local batch of $\\LocalBatchSize$ examples once per update, the global batch size is $\\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize$. At fixed local work, 1,024 workers offer an ideal 1,024$\\times$ throughput increase before exposed overhead. A 1B-parameter model with BF16 gradients contributes a 2 GB gradient payload; ring AllReduce moves $2(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount$ times that payload per worker. Realized speedup depends on local compute, collective service, overlap, and load balance.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: The replicated synchronous baseline keeps a full model copy on each worker and aggregates gradients before each optimizer update. Model parallelism instead partitions the model's computation. Sharded data parallelism, developed below, retains data-parallel computation while distributing training-state storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Data parallelism does not scale indefinitely with worker count. Holding the local batch $\\LocalBatchSize$ fixed while adding workers grows the global batch. Beyond the workload-dependent critical batch size, additional examples yield diminishing reductions in the steps needed to reach target quality [@Shallue2019measuring]. The crossover developed in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-critical-batch-size-parallelism-hurt-4961 therefore limits the statistical benefit even when gradient variance continues to decrease.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Data parallelism applies when examples can be processed independently, as in training a ResNet model [@he2016] on ImageNet. Each replica computes its local loss gradient, and the workers combine those contributions to reproduce the intended global-batch gradient. The averaging rule makes the sampling and weighting assumptions explicit.</p>\n<p>Consider a model with parameters $\\ModelParameters$ training on a dataset $\\TrainingDataset$. The loss function for a single data point $\\ModelInput_i$ is $\\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters, \\ModelInput_i)$. In standard stochastic gradient descent (SGD) with batch size $\\BatchSize$, the gradient update for a minibatch is: $$ \\MinibatchGradient= \\frac{1}{\\BatchSize} \\sum_{i=1}^\\BatchSize\\nabla_{\\ModelParameters} \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters, \\ModelInput_i) $$</p>\n<p>In data parallelism with $\\DeviceCount$ devices, each device $\\WorkerIndex$ computes gradients on its own minibatch $\\MiniBatchSet_k$: $$ \\MinibatchGradient_k = \\frac{1}{|\\MiniBatchSet_k|} \\sum_{\\ModelInput_i \\in \\MiniBatchSet_k} \\nabla_{\\ModelParameters} \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters, \\ModelInput_i) $$</p>\n<p>When all workers use the same local batch size, the global update averages these local gradients: $$ \\GlobalBatchGradient= \\frac{1}{\\DeviceCount} \\sum_{k=1}^\\DeviceCount\\MinibatchGradient_k $$</p>\n<p>Under that equal-batch assumption, the averaging is mathematically equivalent to computing the gradient on the combined batch $\\CombinedBatch= \\bigcup_{k=1}^\\DeviceCount \\MiniBatchSet_k$: $$ \\GlobalBatchGradient= \\frac{1}{|\\CombinedBatch|} \\sum_{\\ModelInput_i \\in \\CombinedBatch} \\nabla_{\\ModelParameters} \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters, \\ModelInput_i) $$</p>\n<p>For unequal local batch sizes, the combined-batch gradient is the weighted average $\\GlobalBatchGradient= (1/|\\CombinedBatch|)\\sum_k |\\MiniBatchSet_k|\\MinibatchGradient_k$. Data parallelism therefore maintains the statistical properties of SGD training, because sampling the intended global batch across devices, computing local gradient sums independently, and normalizing by the aggregate denominator reproduces its estimator. The reduction step uses an AllReduce over local gradient sums and valid-item counts, the numbers of examples or tokens that contribute to the loss.</p>\n<p>Tracking those example and token counts across partitions requires keeping three recurring batch quantities apart. A worker's local batch $\\LocalBatchSize$ is the set of examples it processes for one update, playing the role of $\\MiniBatchSet_k$ in the averaging rule. The global batch $\\GlobalBatchSize= \\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize$ is the set the update averages over. Pipeline parallelism later splits a batch into $\\MicrobatchCount$ microbatches of size $\\MicrobatchSize$ that flow through stages one after another; the word microbatch is reserved for those pipeline slices.</p>\n<p>↪ The ring and tree AllReduce cost models that set the price of this synchronization are developed in @sec-appdx-comm-foundations-allreduce.</p>\n<p>Verify your understanding of how data parallelism distributes work:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>State placement</strong>: determine whether data parallelism shards or replicates the model state (weights) across GPUs.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Global batch size</strong>: calculate the effective global batch size for 8 GPUs with a per-GPU batch size of 32.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Single-device equivalence</strong>: explain why data parallelism is mathematically equivalent to single-device training with a larger batch size.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Limiting constraint</strong>: identify the primary hardware resource that constrains data parallelism as more nodes are added: GPU TFLOP/s or network bandwidth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Averaging across those local batches to form one global batch parallels gradient accumulation, where a device accumulates contributions from multiple forward and backward passes before one optimizer update. Both techniques use gradient addition and the intended loss denominator to process a larger effective batch. Distribution adds communication, partial failures, and coordination to that arithmetic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Data parallelism implementation</h3>\n<p>The SGD equivalence holds only when each phase preserves the intended aggregate sampling and weighting, complete local gradient contributions, and a single synchronized update. The five-stage execution pipeline in @fig-dist-train-data-parallelism follows the dataflow across four GPU swimlanes, from assigning local batches to synchronizing the computed gradients. Identical model replicas ingest disjoint batch shards and compute local gradients ($\\MinibatchGradient_0$--$\\MinibatchGradient_3$). They then synchronize through a ring AllReduce collective before applying uniform parameter updates.</p>\n<p>The gradient synchronization stage of @fig-dist-train-data-parallelism (stage 4) creates a dependency, because each rank must receive the completed reduction before applying its optimizer update. The exposed wait can reflect bandwidth, collective startup, or a late contribution. Measuring that wait determines whether synchronization limits scaling; the dependency alone does not identify the limiting resource.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h4\">Dataset splitting</h4>\n<p>Data splitting is the first place where the SGD equivalence can fail. The aggregate examples or tokens, their sampling probabilities, and their gradient weights must reproduce the intended global-batch estimator. Unique, equal-sized shards satisfy that invariant, but they are sufficient rather than required. Uniform sampling without replacement can assign disjoint shards. Sampling with replacement can legitimately repeat examples. Variable-length batches can assign unequal token counts if the reduction weights each worker by its valid-example or valid-token denominator, which excludes the padding tokens that fill out shorter sequences. An unweighted mean of unequal local means instead overweights workers with smaller denominators.</p>\n<p>The sampler is therefore an integral component of the distributed training system rather than merely an input-loader convenience. A distributed sampler coordinates workers by rank and can deterministically partition or draw indices from a seeded sampling stream. The <code>[DistTrainSamplerSplit.n_examples_m_str]</code> training images of ImageNet-1k do not divide evenly across <code>[DistTrainSamplerSplit.n_gpus_str]</code> GPUs. A sampler that pads by repeating whole examples, unlike the token padding of variable-length batches, gives each GPU <code>[DistTrainSamplerSplit.per_rank_str]</code> examples per epoch and adds <code>[DistTrainSamplerSplit.padded_repeats_str]</code> repeats across the job. The loss normalization or sample weights must account for those repeats when exact epoch-level weighting matters. A recorded seed and deterministic index stream make sample assignment repeatable. Bitwise run reproducibility additionally depends on kernel determinism, collective reduction order, numerical precision, and checkpointed random-number-generator state.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h4\">Compute-phase forward and backward passes</h4>\n<p>Once the sampler assigns each replica its local batch, for a replicated model whose local forward and backward operations require no cross-worker state, the computation phase is <em>embarrassingly parallel</em>. Each GPU executes the model on its local batch; synchronized normalization or additional model partitioning can introduce communication within that phase. To isolate the activation term, consider an unpartitioned <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> reference model at a local batch of <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.micro_batch_str]</code> sequences and sequence length <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.seq_len_str]</code>. This calculation is a hypothetical memory budget, not a feasible one-GPU training configuration, because weights and optimizer state must fit as well. Without optimization, storing these activations for backpropagation would consume roughly <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.act_unchecked_tb_str]</code> of HBM, an order of magnitude beyond the capacity of even an H100 GPU. Activation checkpointing recomputes selected activations during the backward pass rather than retaining them, reducing this example's footprint to <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.act_checkpointed_gb_str]</code> at the cost of extra computation.</p>\n<p>The capacity test must include activations rather than stopping at bytes per parameter. @Sec-appdx-dam-taxonomy-training-memory tabulates the 16-byte-per-parameter training state from which this test starts. At the point of peak residency, $$ \\TotalMemoryFootprint = \\WeightMemory + \\GradientMemory + \\OptimizerStateMemory + \\ActivationMemory, $$ with communication buffers and temporary workspaces consuming additional headroom. Sharding (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-memoryefficient-data-parallelism-zero-fsdp-0e69) reduces the first three terms according to its stage, whereas sequence length, local batch size, and pipeline scheduling govern $\\ActivationMemory$. Activation checkpointing reduces that last term by retaining only selected boundaries and recomputing the omitted forward operators during backpropagation [@narayanan2021]. Full recomputation re-executes about one extra forward pass per step. Because a backward pass costs roughly two forward passes, a step that needed three forward-pass equivalents of arithmetic now needs four, about a third more [@chen2016training]. Selective recomputation re-executes only operators whose activations are large but cheap to produce, such as the attention softmax and dropout, and keeps most of the memory saving for a few percent of extra arithmetic [@korthikanti2023activation]. Recomputed operations raise hardware FLOPs utilization (HFU) but not model FLOPs utilization (MFU), which credits only the model's own forward and backward work. A plan that buys feasibility with full recomputation therefore pays for it in useful throughput. The memory saved is purchased with additional FLOPs and a longer critical path. A feasible parallelism plan must budget both effects.</p>\n<p>As the backward pass traverses the computation graph in reverse, it produces gradients for the parameters held by each replica or shard. Under pure data parallelism, a full <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> FP16 replica would imply a <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.weights_gb_str]</code> gradient tensor per worker, which exceeds single-accelerator memory budgets. Large runs therefore combine data parallelism with sharding, tensor parallelism, or pipeline parallelism. Each local gradient contributes to the intended global-batch estimator. Aggregation can begin as gradient tensors become ready, overlapping later backward computation, but the optimizer must wait for the completed contributions it consumes. This readiness schedule connects local computation to collective communication.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h4\">Gradient synchronization</h4>\n<p>Gradient synchronization combines local contributions into the synchronous update. Each replica must receive the aggregate before applying that update. In a centralized parameter-server design, where servers hold the parameters and workers send them gradients, fixed per-worker payload and update frequency give the server $\\mathcal{O}(\\DeviceCount)$ incoming traffic. That concentration becomes a bottleneck when demand exceeds the server tier's service capacity; sharding the server state or changing the communication pattern can relieve it.</p>\n<p>The replicated synchronous baseline distributes aggregation through collective communication. AllReduce lets each worker contribute its local gradient tensor and receive the same reduced result, which the training loop normalizes to obtain the global average. In sharded variants such as ZeRO and FSDP, ReduceScatter and AllGather change which result each worker retains, leaving each worker only its own shard of the reduced gradient and reconstructing full parameters on demand. Small messages emphasize startup latency; large messages emphasize sustained bandwidth. Centralized aggregation remains another design choice, particularly for the sparse workloads developed later.</p>\n<p>↩ The result contracts of AllReduce, ReduceScatter, and AllGather, and the decomposition of AllReduce into a ReduceScatter followed by an AllGather, are established in @sec-collective-communication-six-core-primitives-8a66.</p>\n<p>At the Distributed Execution row of the fleet stack, the loop specifies an AllReduce contract while its implementation determines the cost. For a large worker group, ring AllReduce transfers approximately $2\\GradientPayload$ bytes per worker for an $\\GradientPayload$-byte gradient payload. @Sec-collective-communication derived this approximate $2\\GradientPayload$ volume and compared algorithms whose startup costs, topology, and contention differ. That chapter also examined how libraries such as NCCL schedule exchanges within and across communication domains.</p>\n<p>Because those algorithms predict different times on the same hardware, diagnosing a measured collective starts by comparing its trace against each prediction. A typical comparison sets a flat ring against a hierarchical AllReduce, which reduces within each NVLink node before crossing the inter-node fabric. Consider a hypothetical A100 cluster with HDR InfiniBand running an AllReduce of a <code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.tensor_gb_str]</code> gradient tensor across <code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.nodes_str]</code> (<code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.total_gpus_str]</code>). The flat-ring estimate is <code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.flat_ring_ms_str]</code>, the idealized hierarchical estimate is <code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.hierarchical_total_ms_str]</code>, and the trace measures <code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.observed_ms_str]</code>. The observation rules out the flat schedule, and the residual above the hierarchical ideal must come from lower achieved rates, startup, or a late participant. Switch counters showing inter-node links at <code>[HierarchicalAllReduceDebug.ib_util_pct_str]</code> utilization do not settle the question, because an aggregate can hide one saturated port during the exposed interval. Per-port and per-channel traces must locate the bottleneck before any topology change.</p>\n<p>↩ Hierarchical AllReduce and its cost relative to a flat ring are established in @sec-communication-collective-operations-collective-operations-hierarchical-allreduce-1338.</p>\n<p>Across the wider design space, @fig-coll-comm compares three synchronization topologies. The bandwidth-optimal ring AllReduce circulates chunks along neighbor links ($2(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount$ volume). The centralized parameter server is constrained by network interface card (NIC) saturation at the server node. The dense mesh of logical AllToAll peer traffic requires $\\DeviceCount(\\DeviceCount-1)$ messages. In dense synchronous data-parallel settings, ring AllReduce avoids a single reducer bottleneck by distributing traffic evenly across participating links, as Baidu's implementation illustrates [@gibiansky2017baidu].</p>\n<p>The panels separate an aggregation algorithm, a centralized architecture, and a collective traffic pattern. They do not provide interchangeable implementations of the same operation. Only the first two aggregate gradients; the AllToAll panel previews the exchange that sparse embedding tables and mixture-of-experts layers need, in which every worker sends different data to every other worker (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-expert-parallelism-mixture-experts-bc45). Libraries choose algorithms and routes for the requested collective on the available fabric; a logical complete graph does not guarantee one-round physical delivery.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h4\">Parameter updating</h4>\n<p>Parameter updating closes the replicated synchronous contract. After the gradient AllReduce [@patarasuk2009], every worker holds the same global gradient and applies it to its own replica with SGD, momentum, or Adam, so the update stays decentralized without losing equivalence to single-device training. Identical gradients alone are insufficient, because optimizer moments, learning rates, and step counts must also match across replicas. Frameworks automate the cycle of splitting data, computing gradients, synchronizing, and updating, but they cannot remove the ordering constraint that keeps the replicas coherent. Every worker must finish aggregating the current step's gradient before any replica computes from the updated weights.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h4\">Synchronization models</h4>\n<p>Whether every worker must wait at a barrier on every step defines the job's synchronization model. The baseline is bulk synchronous parallel (BSP)}, the superstep-and-barrier model, in which workers compute from one parameter version, combine their gradient contributions, and complete the update before computing from the next version. This superstep barrier defines a dependency order, not a requirement that every device update at the same wall-clock instant.</p>\n<p>↩ Bulk synchronous parallel execution and its barrier cost are established in @sec-network-workload-inversion.</p>\n<p>With the same global batch and reduction, BSP matches the corresponding synchronous single-device update mathematically. It does not by itself guarantee bitwise reproducibility. Floating-point reduction order, nondeterministic kernels, data-order state, and failure recovery can still change the result. The synchronization cost is that the slowest worker determines iteration time. A worker that keeps producing correct results but runs markedly slower than its peers is a <strong>straggler</strong>, and under BSP every straggler delays the entire step. Relaxing the barrier changes the optimization process as well as the waiting time.</p>\n<p>Stale synchronous parallel (SSP)} relaxes this constraint by allowing workers to proceed up to $\\StalenessBound$ iterations ahead of the slowest worker before blocking. This slack window bounds staleness while reducing synchronization delays. SSP requires careful learning rate tuning since workers compute gradients on slightly different parameter versions. The bounded staleness guarantee provides a middle ground between BSP's strong consistency and fully asynchronous approaches [@ho2013more].</p>\n<p>Asynchronous parallel (ASP) execution} removes the global update barrier, allowing a worker's contribution to be applied while others continue computing; asynchronous SGD is the optimizer run under that protocol. Less barrier waiting can improve update throughput, but communication and shared-state contention remain. Gradients computed from older parameter versions introduce staleness that can affect convergence. @Sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-staleness-impact-bsp-vs-ssp-vs-asp-e153 develops the objective and delay assumptions needed to evaluate that trade-off. The three models lie on one spectrum, from the strong consistency of BSP to the barrier-free updates of ASP (@tbl-sync-models).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Model</strong></th><th><strong>Consistency</strong></th><th><strong>Throughput</strong></th><th><strong>Convergence</strong></th><th><strong>Use case</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>BSP</strong></td><td>Strong</td><td>Bounded by slowest worker</td><td>Same synchronous update semantics</td><td>Controlled final training runs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>SSP</strong></td><td>Bounded staleness</td><td>Less global-barrier waiting</td><td>Depends on delay and tuning</td><td>Hyperparameter search</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>ASP</strong></td><td>Weak</td><td>No global update barrier</td><td>Depends on staleness and objective</td><td>Large heterogeneous clusters</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Synchronization Model Trade-Offs</strong>: BSP, SSP, and ASP trade consistency for throughput along a spectrum. BSP preserves synchronous update boundaries but pays for the slowest worker; SSP permits bounded progress ahead of slower workers; ASP removes the global update barrier without a finite protocol-wide staleness bound. Throughput and time to target quality depend on the workload and implementation. Reproducibility requires a separate deterministic-execution contract. {#tbl-sync-models}</p>\n<p>Execution traces for four workers with one straggler make the differences visible (@fig-sync-model-timeline). Under BSP, global barriers force the faster workers into idle wait blocks while straggler W3 completes. Under SSP, bounded slack ($\\StalenessBound=1$) lets them run ahead and throttles the drift with brief pauses. Under ASP, asynchronous updates remove the global barrier without imposing a finite staleness bound, although communication and resource contention can still leave workers waiting.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h4\">Barrier semantics and failure modes</h4>\n<p>When a job stays with the synchronous baseline, an optimizer update that consumes an AllReduce result must wait for every required contribution. Workers can execute independent computation while a collective is in flight, but they must issue compatible collectives in the same group order and wait for completion before consuming the result. This coupling creates failure modes absent from single-device training.</p>\n<p>A worker failure can leave peers waiting for a contribution that will never arrive. The runtime may report a communication error, or the operation may remain blocked until a timeout detects the failure. Watchdogs bound that wait so the job can enter recovery rather than retain an unusable allocation indefinitely.</p>\n<p>Collective mismatches occur when workers issue incompatible operations, tensor shapes, or ordering within a communication group. Conditional computation can create such a mismatch if some workers omit an expected reduction. Variable sequence lengths or different expert routes do not inherently violate the protocol; the runtime must construct a compatible communication schedule for those varying local computations.</p>\n<p>Stragglers extend an iteration when their late work lies on its critical path. Thermal throttling, network congestion, or OS jitter can delay a required contribution even when other workers are ready. At <code>[StragglerOdds.workers_str]</code> GPUs with a <code>[StragglerOdds.p_straggle_pct_str]</code> straggler probability per GPU per step, the expected count is <code>[StragglerOdds.expected_str]</code> stragglers per step, but the number that matters under BSP is the chance of a step with none at all. That chance is the per-GPU on-time probability raised to the GPU count, about <code>[StragglerOdds.p_clean_pct_str]</code>. Almost every step therefore waits for at least one slow worker, which is why the slowest worker, not the typical one, sets step time. The resulting exposed wait depends on how late that contribution arrives and what independent work remains. Deployed systems bound these waits with timeouts and heartbeat monitoring, and elastic training lets a job continue with a resized worker group after a failure.</p>\n<p>↪ Heartbeat and collective-timeout failure detection, and the trade-off between restarting healthy workers and leaving peers blocked, are developed in @sec-fault-tolerance-reliability-reliability-failure-detection-mechanisms-efed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h3\">Data parallelism trade-offs</h3>\n<p>Those per-step synchronization and straggler costs explain why data parallelism, despite leaving each replica's model computation intact, eventually hits either a communication wall or a statistical wall as workers are added [@ben-nun2019]. Near-linear throughput scaling requires local compute efficiency, balanced input delivery, and small exposed synchronization costs. Which wall arrives first depends on whether the global batch stays fixed or grows as workers are added. Holding the global batch fixed while adding workers is <strong>strong scaling</strong>: each worker's local batch shrinks, so communication must hide behind less computation. Holding the local batch fixed so that the global batch grows with $\\DeviceCount$ is <strong>weak scaling</strong>: per-worker computation stays constant, but each update averages more examples, which eventually stops reducing the number of updates needed. The rest of the chapter refers to the two regimes by these names.</p>\n<p>Weak scaling pays off when the larger global batch still reaches the target accuracy. @goyal2017accurate trained ResNet-50 on ImageNet in about one hour on <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.gpus_str]</code> at a global batch of <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.minibatch_str]</code> images; the learning-rate rules that let that batch reach the target accuracy are developed in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-learning-rate-scaling-rules-fa26.</p>\n<p>Replicated data parallelism retains the model's local operators, and frameworks can trigger gradient reductions as backward computation produces them. Each replica executes a complete forward and backward pass, avoiding the bubbles of pipeline parallelism, the idle slots while layer stages fill with work and drain. Input stalls, imbalance, and exposed reductions can still leave devices idle.</p>\n<p>These advantages hold only until replication runs into memory capacity, communication bandwidth, or statistical diminishing returns. The memory wall arrives first on large models because a replicated worker holds every parameter, gradient, and optimizer state, demanding more than 1 TB per replica for the <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code>-parameter example before activations. Even when the state fits, communication limits useful scaling once its exposed cost grows relative to local work. As $\\DeviceCount$ increases, the ideal ring bandwidth term $\\frac{2(\\DeviceCount-1)}{\\DeviceCount} \\frac{\\GradientPayload}{\\LinkBandwidth}$ approaches a constant for a fixed gradient payload $\\GradientPayload$ and link bandwidth $\\LinkBandwidth$, while startup latency, topology, and shrinking local computation worsen the balance. Statistical efficiency then sets the ceiling under weak scaling, because growing $\\GlobalBatchSize= \\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize$ with $\\DeviceCount$ eventually yields diminishing reductions in update count. Strong scaling avoids that batch-size ceiling only by shrinking each worker's local batch, which exposes the communication trade-off sooner.</p>\n<p>A GPT-2 scaling scenario makes the efficiency loss concrete by holding the model fixed and changing only the communication domain. GPT-2 stands in for the 175B reference here because it fits on one GPU, so pure replicated data parallelism applies and communication is the only variable. This notebook and the next report parallel efficiency, the achieved speedup divided by the GPU count, which @sec-distributed-training-scaling-math develops as scaling efficiency. The first case keeps all eight GPUs inside a single NVLink node.</p>\n<p><strong>Single-GPU baseline</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Batch size: <code>[Scaling8GPU.batch_per_gpu_str]</code> (with activation checkpointing, fits in <code>[Scaling8GPU.fits_mem_gb_str]</code>)</li>\n<li>Time per step: <code>[Scaling8GPU.single_gpu_step_s_str]</code></li>\n<li>Baseline time for <code>[Scaling8GPU.single_gpu_steps_str]</code> single-GPU steps: <code>[Scaling8GPU.training_hours_1gpu_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following durations hold the total sample budget fixed, so larger global batches require fewer optimizer updates. They assume those samples attain the same quality; the calculation establishes throughput scaling, not convergence equivalence.</p>\n<p><strong>8 GPUs: single node with NVLink</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Per-GPU batch: <code>[Scaling8GPU.batch_per_gpu_str]</code>, global batch: <code>[Scaling8GPU.global_batch_str]</code></li>\n<li>Gradient synchronization: <code>[Scaling8GPU.gpt2_sync_size_gb_str]</code> @ <code>[Scaling8GPU.nvlink_h100_gb_per_s_str]</code> (NVLink, per direction) $\\approx$ <code>[Scaling8GPU.comm_8gpu_ms_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Performance results:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Compute: <code>[Scaling8GPU.compute_8gpu_ms_str]</code> per step</li>\n<li>Communication: <code>[Scaling8GPU.comm_8gpu_ms_str]</code> per step</li>\n<li>Total: <code>[Scaling8GPU.total_8gpu_ms_str]</code> per step</li>\n<li>Speedup (throughput): <code>[Scaling8GPU.speedup_8gpu_mult_str]</code></li>\n<li>Parallel efficiency: <code>[Scaling8GPU.efficiency_8gpu_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Training time: <code>[Scaling8GPU.training_hours_1gpu_str]</code> ÷ <code>[Scaling8GPU.speedup_8gpu_divisor_str]</code> = <code>[Scaling8GPU.training_8gpu_hours_str]</code></p>\n<p>Inside the node, NVLink keeps gradient exchange small relative to compute, so efficiency stays near <code>[Scaling8GPU.efficiency_8gpu_str]</code>. The picture inverts once the same model must synchronize across nodes over a commodity network.</p>\n<p>The second case scales the same GPT-2 run across multiple nodes, replacing the intra-node NVLink hop with inter-node Ethernet for the bulk of the AllReduce. The 10 Gb Ethernet link models a commodity cloud tier without a dedicated training fabric, far slower than the InfiniBand of the reference cluster, which makes the cost of leaving the node easy to see.</p>\n<p><strong>Commodity network configuration</strong>: <code>[Scaling32GPU.gpus_str]</code> across <code>[Scaling32GPU.n_nodes_str]</code></p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Per-GPU batch: <code>[Scaling32GPU.batch_per_gpu_str]</code>, global batch: <code>[Scaling32GPU.global_batch_str]</code></li>\n<li>Intra-node communication: <code>[Scaling32GPU.intra_node_comm_ms_str]</code> (NVLink)</li>\n<li>Inter-node communication: <code>[Scaling32GPU.gpt2_sync_size_gb_str]</code> @ <code>[Scaling32GPU.ib_hdr_gb_per_s_str]</code> (10GbE) $\\approx$ <code>[Scaling32GPU.inter_node_ms_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Performance results:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Compute: <code>[Scaling32GPU.compute_32gpu_ms_str]</code> (<code>[Scaling32GPU.compute_pct_str]</code> of time)</li>\n<li>Communication: <code>[Scaling32GPU.comm_32gpu_ms_str]</code> (<code>[Scaling32GPU.comm_pct_str]</code> of time), so communication dominates and becomes the bottleneck.</li>\n<li>Total: <code>[Scaling32GPU.total_32gpu_ms_str]</code> per step</li>\n<li>Speedup (throughput): <code>[Scaling32GPU.speedup_32gpu_mult_str]</code> faster → <code>[Scaling32GPU.training_32gpu_hours_str]</code></li>\n<li>Parallel efficiency: <code>[Scaling32GPU.efficiency_32gpu_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The scale-out run bought its larger global batch with four nodes on a slow network. A single node can reach the same global batch another way, by running several local microsteps and accumulating their gradients before one AllReduce. The comparison below is therefore between two placements, one node with accumulation against four nodes without it. Accumulation's job is to keep the global batch equal, while the saving comes from never leaving the NVLink domain.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A GPT-2 run on a commodity <code>[GradAccumScenario.network_label_str]</code> network is communication-bound at <code>[GradAccumScenario.n_gpus_32_str]</code> across <code>[GradAccumScenario.baseline_nodes_str]</code>, costing <code>[GradAccumScenario.baseline_cost_str]</code> for a fixed number of samples. Can a single 8-GPU node that uses gradient accumulation to match that global batch process the same samples for less?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Effective batch size</strong>: <code>[GradAccumScenario.n_gpus_ga_str]</code> $\\times$ batch <code>[GradAccumScenario.batch_per_gpu_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[GradAccumScenario.accum_steps_str]</code> accumulation steps = <code>[GradAccumScenario.effective_batch_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Communication overhead</strong>: With <code>[GradAccumScenario.accum_steps_str]</code>-step accumulation, the job AllReduces once every <code>[GradAccumScenario.accum_steps_str]</code> steps (overhead = <code>[GradAccumScenario.comm_8gpu_ms_str]</code> / (<code>[GradAccumScenario.accum_steps_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[GradAccumScenario.compute_8gpu_ms_str]</code>) $\\approx$ <code>[GradAccumScenario.comm_overhead_pct_math]</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Training duration</strong>: Total time is <code>[GradAccumScenario.ga_training_hours_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Scale-out baseline cost</strong>: <code>[GradAccumScenario.baseline_nodes_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[GradAccumScenario.baseline_training_hours_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[GradAccumScenario.hourly_rate_str]</code> = <code>[GradAccumScenario.baseline_cost_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accumulation cost</strong>: <code>[GradAccumScenario.ga_training_hours_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[GradAccumScenario.hourly_rate_str]</code> = <code>[GradAccumScenario.ga_cost_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: The one-node plan saves <code>[GradAccumScenario.ga_savings_str]</code> (<code>[GradAccumScenario.ga_savings_pct_str]</code>). Almost all of that saving comes from placement, because the four-node run spent most of each step on the slow inter-node link and the single node never uses it. Gradient accumulation is what makes the single node a fair substitute, because it reproduces the four-node global batch. Its own contribution to speed is small, since the same node already ran at <code>[Scaling8GPU.efficiency_8gpu_str]</code> parallel efficiency without accumulation. Accumulation on a multi-node job still requires inter-node synchronization once per window.</p>\n<p>In this GPT-2 scenario, changing the communication domain drops efficiency from <code>[Scaling8GPU.efficiency_8gpu_str]</code> to <code>[Scaling32GPU.efficiency_32gpu_str]</code>. Accumulation can preserve the effective batch while avoiding the slow scale-out link, at the cost of additional local computation between updates. It leaves each worker's replicated model state unchanged, however; reducing that memory burden requires sharding.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">Memory-efficient data parallelism with ZeRO and FSDP</h3>\n<p>Replicated data parallelism stores an identical copy of weights, gradients, and optimizer state on every GPU, so a <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.params_b_str]</code>-parameter model demands <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.total_state_gb_str]</code> on each device and overflows an <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.a100_mem_gb_str]</code> accelerator before a single activation is allocated. The Zero Redundancy Optimizer (ZeRO) [@rajbhandari2020] and PyTorch's Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP)} [@zhao2023] remove that duplication by sharding training state across workers while keeping each worker's full forward and backward computation. Sharding can make a model fit in memory without splitting its layer computations across devices.</p>\n<p>: <strong>ZeRO (Zero Redundancy Optimizer)</strong>}: Introduced by Microsoft researchers in 2019 and released in the DeepSpeed library [@rasley2020], ZeRO's stages progressively partition optimizer states, gradients, and parameters. FSDP draws on the fully sharded approach associated with ZeRO's deepest stage; the shared partitioning principle does not imply identical implementations or communication schedules.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Sharded data parallelism</strong></em> partitions optimizer state, gradients, and at the deepest stage the parameters themselves across data-parallel workers. Each worker processes its own minibatch shard and uses collectives to obtain the parameters and reduced gradients it needs. Full sharding reduces each worker's persistent state toward $1/\\DeviceCount$ of the replicated training state; activations and temporary buffers still contribute to peak memory.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Under the mixed-precision Adam convention used here, <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_total_per_param_str]</code> bytes of state per parameter gives a <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.params_b_str]</code>-parameter model <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.total_state_gb_str]</code> of training state, exceeding a <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.a100_mem_gb_str]</code> accelerator before activations. ZeRO Stage 3 across <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.n_gpus_zero_count_str]</code> workers reduces persistent state per device to <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.zero3_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> (<code>[DistTrainA100Memory.effective_bpp_str]</code> bytes per parameter). The resulting AllGather and ReduceScatter traffic trades memory capacity for communication whose exposed cost depends on the interconnect and available overlap.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike vanilla data parallelism, which replicates training state, sharded data parallelism partitions its storage. Unlike model parallelism, it retains each worker's full forward and backward computation. Fully sharded execution gathers parameters for the current computation; the resharding schedule determines when it releases them.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that sharding is free memory. The on-demand AllGathers place parameter traffic on the critical path of every layer in every step. On slower interconnects or at small per-worker batch sizes, the exposed communication erodes throughput faster than the memory savings help. Capacity is purchased with bandwidth.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The scale of the savings is clearest on a model whose replicated training state only modestly overflows one accelerator, so that the effect of each sharding stage stays visible. The 175B reference returns in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-model-parallelism-7437, when the question becomes whether sharding alone is enough.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Training a <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.params_b_str]</code> parameter Llama-2 model in mixed precision requires <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_per_param_value_str]</code> bytes per parameter for weights, gradients, and optimizer state. Does the full training state fit on a single A100-<code>[DistTrainA100Memory.a100_mem_gb_str]</code>, and how does ZeRO Stage 3 with <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.n_gpus_zero_str]</code> change the per-device memory requirement?</p>\n<p><strong>Baseline: Standard DDP (replicated state)</strong> Per-Parameter Memory Cost:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Weights (FP16)</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp16_str]</code> bytes</li>\n<li><strong>Gradients (FP16)</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp16_str]</code> bytes</li>\n<li><strong>Optimizer state (FP32)</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_optimizer_str]</code> bytes (<code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp32_str]</code> master weight + <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp32_str]</code> momentum + <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp32_str]</code> variance)</li>\n<li><strong>Total</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_total_per_param_str]</code> bytes/parameter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Total Memory for <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.params_b_str]</code> Model:</p>\n<p><code>[DistTrainA100Memory.total_state_display_math]</code></p>\n<p><strong>Baseline outcome</strong>: Out-of-memory (OOM)} on A100-<code>[DistTrainA100Memory.a100_mem_gb_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Optimization: ZeRO-3 (fully sharded)</strong> With $\\DeviceCount=$ <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.n_gpus_zero_count_str]</code> GPUs, state is partitioned:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Weights</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp16_str]</code>$/\\DeviceCount$ bytes</li>\n<li><strong>Gradients</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_fp16_str]</code>$/\\DeviceCount$ bytes</li>\n<li><strong>Optimizer</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_optimizer_str]</code>$/\\DeviceCount$ bytes</li>\n<li><strong>Total</strong>: <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.bytes_total_per_param_str]</code>$/\\DeviceCount=$ <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.effective_bpp_str]</code> bytes/parameter effective storage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Per-GPU Memory:</p>\n<p><code>[DistTrainA100Memory.zero3_per_gpu_display_math]</code></p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: The persistent state fits, leaving ~<code>[DistTrainA100Memory.activation_headroom_gb_str]</code> for activations, gathered parameters, temporary buffers, and runtime allocations. Their simultaneous peak determines whether the chosen batch size fits.</p>\n<p>ZeRO reaches that result in stages, and the intermediate stages matter because each one buys memory with a different communication pattern. Optimizer sharding alone (ZeRO-1) already brings the replicated footprint of the same model under the accelerator ceiling, because optimizer state is three-quarters of it. Gradient sharding (ZeRO-2) and parameter sharding (ZeRO-3/FSDP) continue toward the fully sharded figure (@fig-zero-memory). Each stage also changes which collectives the update uses (@tbl-zero-stages).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Stage</strong></th><th><strong>What is sharded</strong></th><th><strong>Memory reduction</strong></th><th><strong>Communication overhead</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>ZeRO-1</strong></td><td>Optimizer states only</td><td>~4$\\times$</td><td>Reduce to owner + AllGather ($1\\times$ DDP volume)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>ZeRO-2</strong></td><td>+ Gradients</td><td>~8$\\times$</td><td>ReduceScatter + AllGather ($1\\times$ DDP volume)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>ZeRO-3/FSDP</strong></td><td>+ Parameters</td><td>~$\\DeviceCount$ (linear in workers)</td><td>AllGather before each layer ($1.5\\times$ DDP volume)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>ZeRO Sharding Stages</strong>: Each stage shards a different layer of training state and trades a different communication primitive. ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 both replace the gradient AllReduce with a reduction to each shard's owner and a parameter AllGather, matching DDP volume, and differ only in whether each worker keeps a full-size gradient buffer; ZeRO-3 (FSDP) adds per-layer AllGathers during forward and backward passes, unlocking near-linear memory scaling at $1.5\\times$ communication. {#tbl-zero-stages}</p>\n<p>The first step, ZeRO-1, shards optimizer states across GPUs so each GPU stores only $1/\\DeviceCount$ of the Adam optimizer-related state. During backpropagation, each worker still materializes the full 2 bytes/param gradient buffer locally, which is why gradient memory remains unsharded in @tbl-zero-stages. Once gradients are ready, however, they need only be reduced to the worker responsible for each parameter shard via a ReduceScatter, and after each worker updates its owned shard, an AllGather broadcasts the updated parameters to restore the full replicas. This two-phase schedule (one gradient ReduceScatter plus one parameter AllGather) matches the table's $1\\times$ baseline communication volume, whereas running a full gradient AllReduce followed by a parameter AllGather would add 50 percent more traffic. Under the 12-byte convention that counts FP32 master weights, momentum, and variance, sharding the optimizer state reduces cluster-wide storage from $12\\DeviceCount$ bytes/param to $12$ bytes/param in total, split evenly across the GPUs.</p>\n<p>Building on that owner-update contract, ZeRO-2 eliminates persistent full-model gradient buffers by streaming the ReduceScatter during the backward pass. As each layer or communication bucket finishes its backward computation, workers immediately ReduceScatter that bucket's gradients and free the slices owned by other ranks, leaving each GPU with only its $1/\\DeviceCount$ persistent shard of reduced gradients. Each worker then updates its $1/\\DeviceCount$ parameter partition using the corresponding optimizer state, and an AllGather restores the updated parameter replicas. In the ideal ring accounting, the gradient ReduceScatter and the parameter AllGather each transfer $\\Parameters(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount$ elements per worker, for $2\\Parameters(\\DeviceCount-1)/\\DeviceCount$ across both phases, matching DDP's byte volume ($1\\times$) when gradient and parameter communication widths match. Replicated parameters avoid forward parameter gathers, although model operations may still communicate. Under the FP16-gradient convention, persistent gradient storage falls from $2\\DeviceCount$ bytes per parameter across $\\DeviceCount$ workers to $2$ bytes per parameter in total, or $2/\\DeviceCount$ per GPU, apart from transient bucket buffers.</p>\n<p>The reductions in @tbl-zero-stages count memory per GPU rather than across the cluster. They are large-worker limits, in which the replicated FP16 weights and gradients remain on each GPU under ZeRO-1 and only the FP16 weights remain under ZeRO-2.</p>\n<p>At the deepest stage, ZeRO-3 and FSDP partition the parameters as well, leaving each GPU with a $1/\\DeviceCount$ persistent slice of all three training states. Because workers no longer hold full parameter replicas between steps, the post-update parameter AllGather disappears and is replaced by two on-demand parameter AllGathers during computation. Each sharded unit gathers its parameters before the forward pass and immediately releases the non-local slices, then gathers them a second time before the backward pass and releases them again once local gradients are computed, and finally runs a ReduceScatter to deliver the completed gradients to each shard's owner for the local optimizer update. Across four ranks (@fig-zero-partitioning) and under matching communication widths, ZeRO-1 ($\\OptimizerStateMemory/4$) and ZeRO-2 ($\\OptimizerStateMemory/4, \\GradientMemory/4$) each execute two collective phases per step and retain the baseline $1\\times$ communication volume. ZeRO-3 ($\\WeightMemory/4, \\GradientMemory/4, \\OptimizerStateMemory/4$) executes three collective phases of one parameter-equivalent payload each (forward AllGather, backward AllGather, and gradient ReduceScatter), which yields $3/2 = 1.5\\times$ the baseline two-phase volume in this accounting.</p>\n<p>Full sharding adds parameter-readiness dependencies that replicated DDP avoids. With one sharding unit per layer, the forward pass needs an AllGather to reconstruct parameters ($\\LayerParameterPayload$ bytes). Resharding after forward requires another gather before backward ($\\LayerParameterPayload$ bytes), followed by a ReduceScatter for gradients ($\\LayerParameterPayload$ bytes under equal communication precision). For a model with $\\LayerCount$ layers, this schedule performs about $3\\LayerCount$ collective operations per step. DDP instead AllReduces gradient buckets as they become ready. At large worker counts, the ideal per-worker transfer volumes approach $3\\ParameterPayload$ bytes versus $2\\ParameterPayload$ for DDP. Here $\\ParameterPayload$ is the model's parameter bytes at the communication precision, not full training state, and it equals $\\GradientPayload$ when parameters and gradients travel at the same width. These volumes do not determine elapsed time alone. While layer $\\LayerIndex$ computes, layer $\\LayerIndex+1$ can prefetch parameters, subject to memory headroom and fabric contention.</p>\n<p>The allocation lifetimes in @fig-fsdp-parameter-lifetime separate persistent ownership from the temporary buffers needed to execute one sharding unit.</p>\n<p>A model fits only when all simultaneously live allocations fit. The persistent shards therefore establish a floor, while gathered units, saved activations, and prefetch determine how far the execution peak rises above it.</p>\n<p>The choice between FSDP and DDP begins with peak memory. If replicated state, activations, and working buffers fit, DDP avoids repeated parameter gathers. When optimizer and gradient storage are the obstacle, ZeRO-2 offers savings while leaving parameters replicated. If parameters themselves do not fit, ZeRO-3/FSDP, offload, or partitioned computation must address that remaining constraint. For a 70B+ model on <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.a100_mem_gb_str]</code> devices, the need for tensor parallelism depends on the gathered layer size, activation budget, and throughput target; parameter count alone does not decide it.</p>\n<p>Memory-efficient data parallelism requires careful tuning of sharding strategy (by layer, by transformer block, or flat) and mixed precision settings. Finer sharding reduces per-GPU memory but increases communication frequency, because more AllGather and ReduceScatter operations must execute per training step. Replicated or sharded, a data-parallel design earns its added workers only when they turn into faster progress toward target quality, and scaling efficiency measures that conversion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h2\">Scaling Efficiency and Convergence</h2>\n<p>Data parallelism and ZeRO sharding show how communication and synchronization enter every training step, yet raw step throughput alone cannot judge a distributed run. When doubling the number of GPUs yields only 1.5$\\times$ speedup for the same work, scaling efficiency is 75 percent, and if the larger global batch also requires more optimizer updates to reach the same loss, wall-clock time to quality degrades further. Evaluating a distributed configuration therefore requires separating two questions: how efficiently added devices execute each step, and how batch growth or stale updates affect the number of steps to convergence. The hardware scaling metrics below apply to every parallelism strategy; the convergence analysis that follows concerns data-parallel batch growth and synchronization slack, establishing a batch-size constraint that tensor and pipeline configurations must also respect when combined with data parallelism.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">The mathematics of scaling efficiency</h3>\n<p>That first question is answered by scaling efficiency, the fleet-level ratio introduced with the Fleet Law in @sec-vol2-introduction-fleet-law and reported as parallel efficiency in the GPT-2 scaling notebooks. If a model trains in time $\\SingleDeviceRunTime$ on one device, ideal (linear) scaling would train it in time $\\SingleDeviceRunTime/\\DeviceCount$ on $\\DeviceCount$ devices; communication overhead, pipeline bubbles, and load imbalance widen the gap between that ideal and the realized time $\\MultiDeviceRunTime$. @Eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency formalizes the ratio:</p>\n<p>$$\\ScalingEfficiency = \\frac{\\SingleDeviceRunTime}{\\DeviceCount\\times \\MultiDeviceRunTime}$$ {#eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency}</p>\n<p>where $\\MultiDeviceRunTime$ is the training time on $\\DeviceCount$ devices. Measured per step of the same work, $\\SingleDeviceRunTime$ is the quantity the Fleet Law writes as $\\IronLawComputeTime$, the single-device compute time that its compute term divides by $\\DeviceCount$. An efficiency of 1.0 means perfect linear scaling; an efficiency of 0.5 means the run achieves only half the ideal speedup.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Scaling efficiency</strong></em> $(\\ScalingEfficiency)$ is the ratio of actual training throughput to ideal linear throughput when increasing the number of ML compute devices ($\\DeviceCount$).</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The ratio in @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency measures added devices against the specified baseline. At 0.50 efficiency, a 10,000-GPU run delivers the throughput that 5,000 perfectly scaled devices would, so half of the accelerator-hours buy no progress relative to ideal scaling. The equivalence is to an idealized baseline. A real 5,000-GPU run would have its own, typically higher, efficiency, and the loss is spread across every device as waiting rather than concentrated in idle ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike single-node efficiency, which captures local bottlenecks like $\\Bandwidth$, scaling efficiency measures how performance changes with device count. Communication time $(\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount))$ and synchronization contribute, but changing local kernel shapes or input delivery can also affect the ratio.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that scaling efficiency is constant. In reality, it depends on both device count and problem size. For a fixed model, the communication-to-compute ratio typically worsens as $\\DeviceCount$ increases (Amdahl's Law). At a fixed $\\DeviceCount$, a smaller model or local batch leaves less computation per device to hide startup latency and coordination, so its efficiency falls sooner.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The Fleet Law leads to the scaling efficiency metric for fixed global work. Substituting its step time (principle \\ref{pri-distributed-step-time}) for $\\MultiDeviceRunTime$ in @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency, with $\\IronLawComputeTime$ standing for $\\SingleDeviceRunTime$ per step as noted there, gives @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency-fleet:</p>\n<p>$$ \\ScalingEfficiency = \\frac{\\IronLawComputeTime}{\\DeviceCount\\times \\DistributedStepTime(\\DeviceCount)} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{\\DeviceCount(\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount) + \\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount) - \\OverlappedTime)}{\\IronLawComputeTime}} $$ {#eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency-fleet}</p>\n<p>This form states the scaling efficiency bound (principle \\ref{pri-scaling-efficiency}), under which perfect linear scaling $(\\ScalingEfficiency = 1.0)$ is a <em>theoretical limit</em>, not a <em>practical target</em>. Real systems fall short of it by an amount that depends on the configuration and grows with $\\DeviceCount$. The 175B data-parallel example below reaches only <code>[CommScalingMath.scaling_efficiency_str]</code> under its stated assumptions, and the hybrid layouts later in the chapter, priced on the reference training step rather than on this counterfactual, still stay below 1.0. The gap between $\\ScalingEfficiency = 1.0$ and the achieved efficiency is the price of distributed execution, paid in communication and coordination.</p>\n<p>The equation also exposes the <strong>scaling wall</strong>. As $\\DeviceCount$ increases, the compute term $(\\IronLawComputeTime/\\DeviceCount)$ shrinks, but the communication and synchronization terms can remain constant or grow. A ring AllReduce's bandwidth term, for example, stays near its bound however many workers join (principle \\ref{pri-allreduce-bandwidth-bound}). Eventually, the denominator is dominated by overhead, driving efficiency toward zero. The equation holds the global work fixed, which is strong scaling, and that regime reaches the wall sooner. Under weak scaling each device keeps its local batch, so its compute time per step stays level against the same ring term and the wall arrives later, while the growing global batch runs toward the statistical limit of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-critical-batch-size-parallelism-hurt-4961.</p>\n<p>For data-parallel training of the <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> reference model, the per-step communication cost is dominated by the AllReduce of <code>[CommScalingMath.gradient_gb_str]</code> of gradients, and how much of that exchange hides behind computation decides the efficiency. The scaling efficiency depends on computation per communicated byte. At fixed tokens per update, increasing parameter count raises both transformer computation and gradient bytes roughly proportionally, so model size alone does not guarantee better scaling. Larger batches raise computation per optimizer step without changing gradient size. Large batches, however, can harm convergence and so require learning-rate tuning and warmup schedules.</p>\n<p>Network bandwidth enters directly because doubling InfiniBand bandwidth halves the bandwidth term, but efficiency changes according to the full step-time denominator rather than in direct proportion. The network fabric is therefore a first-order determinant of cluster productivity, not a secondary concern. Every point of scaling efficiency it recovers is returned across the much larger accelerator investment, which is why fabric spending is judged by the efficiency it buys rather than by its own price.</p>\n<p>These factors interact, so efficiency cannot be inferred from model size or GPU count alone. The following worked model fixes the parameter count, tokens per update, accelerator count, effective bandwidth, local compute efficiency before communication, and overlap before calculating one efficiency point. Changing any of those inputs requires recomputing the full step-time model.</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong>: A communication-only model for training a <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> model on a DGX H100 cluster with <code>[CommScalingMath.ib_bw_gbit_per_s_str]</code> InfiniBand per GPU. Pure replicated data parallelism is a counterfactual baseline here: the calculation isolates gradient exchange but does not establish that the full training state fits on each device.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Compute per step</strong> (assuming batch size <code>[CommScalingMath.batch_tokens_m_str]</code> tokens, <code>[CommScalingMath.flops_per_token_value_str]</code> FLOPs per parameter per token):</li>\n<p><code>[CommScalingMath.compute_per_step_math]</code> FLOPs</p>\n</ul>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Per-GPU compute time</strong> on <code>[CommScalingMath.n_gpus_str]</code>, each at <code>[CommScalingMath.h100_fp8_tflop_per_s_str]</code> peak in the 8-bit floating-point (FP8) format developed in @sec-distributed-training-fp8 (<code>[CommScalingMath.local_efficiency_pct_str]</code> local compute efficiency before communication). The general matrix multiplications (GEMMs) run in FP8 while the gradients below still cross the wire in FP16, because the collective's data type is chosen independently of the arithmetic's:</li>\n<p><code>[CommScalingMath.t_compute_math]</code> seconds</p>\n</ul>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>AllReduce time</strong> (for <code>[CommScalingMath.gradient_gb_str]</code> of gradients using ring-AllReduce with overlap):</li>\n<p><code>[CommScalingMath.t_comm_math]</code> seconds (raw ring transfer takes <code>[CommScalingMath.comm_time_formula_math]</code>, about <code>[CommScalingMath.raw_comm_s_str]</code>; the schedule could hide <code>[CommScalingMath.assumed_overlap_pct_str]</code> of that transfer, but the available compute window caps hidden communication at <code>[CommScalingMath.overlap_pct_str]</code> of raw transfer time)</p>\n</ul>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Scaling efficiency</strong>: $\\ScalingEfficiency = \\PerDeviceComputeTime/(\\PerDeviceComputeTime+ \\ExposedCommunicationTime)$, which is @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency-fleet with the per-device compute window $\\PerDeviceComputeTime= \\IronLawComputeTime/\\DeviceCount$ and the exposed term standing for $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount) + \\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount) - \\OverlappedTime$. Here the numerator is <code>[CommScalingMath.t_compute_s_str]</code> and the complete denominator is <code>[CommScalingMath.exposed_step_s_str]</code>, giving <code>[CommScalingMath.scaling_efficiency_pct_str]</code>. The exposed communication term already subtracts the hidden portion from the raw transfer time and includes collective synchronization; subtracting overlap again would double-count the same saving.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: The low efficiency (<code>[CommScalingMath.scaling_efficiency_pct_str]</code>) shows why naive data parallelism at this scale is insufficient. It is one operating point, fixed by <code>[CommScalingMath.batch_tokens_m_str]</code> per update, <code>[CommScalingMath.n_gpus_str]</code>, and <code>[CommScalingMath.overlap_pct_str]</code> overlap, not a universal threshold. Another cluster size requires recomputing per-device compute time, collective cost, overlap, and topology. A hierarchical reduction, which first reduces inside each node over NVLink so that each InfiniBand port carries only a fraction of the gradient, shrinks the exposed term, and so does a longer compute window per step. @Sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-12a0 prices both on the reference training step, where memory rather than communication rules out pure data parallelism.</p>\n<p>↩ The compute window that bounds how much of a collective can hide is established in @sec-communication-overlap-limits.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h4\">Parallelism-infrastructure interaction</h4>\n<p>The scaling efficiency of a run is set by the <em>interaction</em> between the model's communication requirements and the infrastructure's bandwidth hierarchy, not by the <em>model architecture alone</em>. Making a large model both fit and run efficiently means mapping each parallelism dimension onto the bandwidth tier that can carry its traffic. @Sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-12a0 works through four concrete configurations, priced on the reference training step, once tensor and pipeline parallelism have been developed.</p>\n<p>The collective algorithm adds its own dependence on message size. Tree-based AllReduce reaches every rank in $\\mathcal{O}(\\log \\DeviceCount)$ steps and suits small, latency-bound messages, whereas large gradient buffers favor the bandwidth-optimal ring; message size, topology, and contention decide between them.</p>\n<p>↩ The message size at which ring AllReduce overtakes tree AllReduce, derived from the latency and bandwidth terms of each algorithm, is established in @sec-communication-collective-operations-algorithm-crossover.</p>\n<p>Even pre-frontier transformers carry gradient buffers large enough to demand substantial link bandwidth from that ring. BERT-Base (<code>[BertGradientSync.base_params_str]</code> parameters) requires approximately <code>[BertGradientSync.base_grad_mb_str]</code> of gradient synchronization per iteration in FP32, while BERT-Large (<code>[BertGradientSync.large_params_str]</code> parameters) requires approximately <code>[BertGradientSync.large_grad_gb_str]</code>. Because a ring AllReduce across <code>[BertGradientSync.n_gpus_str]</code> moves nearly twice the gradient bytes through each GPU's link, finishing BERT-Large synchronization within <code>[BertGradientSync.sync_budget_ms_str]</code> demands roughly <code>[BertGradientSync.large_bw_str]</code> of sustained per-GPU bandwidth, and BERT-Base about a third of that. At <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> parameters, meeting the step budget requires combining 3D parallelism, gradient accumulation, and overlap rather than relying on a single flat collective, and whatever communication remains unhidden acts as the unscalable residual that Amdahl's Law prices next.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h3\">Scaling physics and Amdahl's Law with communication</h3>\n<p>Even a small unscalable cost in a single-device step eventually dominates distributed execution, because adding $\\DeviceCount$ devices shrinks the parallelizable compute share ($\\SingleDeviceRunTime / \\DeviceCount$) while leaving the fixed residual untouched. An Amdahl-style model} separates that scalable work from the fixed residual fraction $\\SerialFraction$ of the single-device baseline (@sec-appdx-fleet-foundations-amdahls-fleet). In units of the single-device step, a distributed step then takes the scalable share divided by the device count plus the whole residual, and speedup is the reciprocal of that time.</p>\n<p>That baseline fraction is not the synchronization share a profiler reports for a distributed step (the distributed Amdahl pitfall of @sec-vol2-introduction-fleet-law), because the compute share has been divided by $\\DeviceCount$ while the residual has not. A residual of <code>[AmdahlObservedShare.r_pct_str]</code> of the single-device step occupies about <code>[AmdahlObservedShare.share_pct_str]</code> of each step on <code>[AmdahlObservedShare.n_str]</code>. Reading the observed share as $\\SerialFraction$ would therefore overstate the residual many times over and predict a far lower speedup ceiling than the model implies.</p>\n<p>Plotted on log-log axes up to 512 GPUs, the same fixed-residual model shows how much speedup each residual fraction gives up (@fig-scaling-tax), complementing the scaling efficiency bound (principle \\ref{pri-scaling-efficiency}). In this illustrative model, a small baseline fraction ($\\SerialFraction= 0.1\\%$) stays closer to ideal scaling than a larger fraction ($\\SerialFraction= 1\\%$). A residual equal to a tenth of baseline time ($\\SerialFraction= 0.10$) limits asymptotic speedup to $1/\\SerialFraction= 10\\times$. Actual collective and coordination costs can change with worker count, so predicting a training run requires the full step-time accounting of @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency-fleet.</p>\n<p>Published benchmark runs show the same shortfall in practice. MLPerf Training times complete runs to a fixed quality target, and several submitters report the same system at several sizes within one round [@mattson2020mlperf; @mlcommons2025training]. Each of <code>[MLPerfScaling.series_count_str]</code> such series for the GPT-3 and Llama 3.1 405B benchmarks falls below the ideal line drawn through its smallest configuration (@fig-mlperf-training-scaling). On NVIDIA's Eos cluster, GPT-3 trained in about <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_t0_str]</code> minutes on <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_n0_str]</code> and <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_t1_str]</code> minutes on <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_n1_str]</code>. A <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_n_mult_str]</code> increase in accelerators therefore cut the time <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_t_mult_str]</code>, a scaling efficiency (@eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency, measured relative to the smallest configuration rather than one device) of <code>[MLPerfScaling.eos_eff_pct_str]</code>. Across the series, the largest configuration keeps <code>[MLPerfScaling.eff_range_str]</code> of ideal. Two effects compound in these numbers. Communication and coordination grow with worker count, and the larger configurations also train with larger global batches, which can require more samples to reach the target (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-critical-batch-size-parallelism-hurt-4961). Separating the two requires the step-level accounting of @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency-fleet.</p>\n<p>Predicting a run means pricing each term of @eq-distributed-training-scaling-efficiency-fleet for the configuration at hand. For a large-message ring AllReduce, the communication term's bandwidth contribution is $\\frac{2(\\DeviceCount-1)}{\\DeviceCount} \\frac{\\DataVolume}{\\LinkBandwidth}$. This bandwidth term is the fleet counterpart of the single-machine iron law's data-movement ratio $\\DataVolume/\\Bandwidth$. Here $\\DataVolume$ is the payload being reduced (the gradient bytes $\\GradientPayload$ under data parallelism, or a shard of model state under sharding), and $\\LinkBandwidth$ is sustained per-link bandwidth. The expression omits per-message startup latency, and a wait it already counts must not be charged again under synchronization.</p>\n<p>↪ The single-machine iron law and its data-movement term are developed in @sec-appdx-dam-taxonomy-iron-law-mapping.</p>\n<p>This communication overhead costs energy as well as wall-clock time, and the energy cost of communication grows with the physical distance between devices. At the scale of <code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.cluster_gpus_str]</code>, moving data is itself a power load. A full H100 NVLink envelope of roughly <code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.nvlink_aggregate_pb_per_s_str]</code> at <code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.nvlink_energy_pj_per_bit_str]</code> represents about <code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.nvlink_power_kw_str]</code> before cooling and switch overhead. The smaller NDR InfiniBand envelope (<code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.ib_aggregate_tb_per_s_str]</code> at <code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.ib_energy_pj_per_bit_str]</code>) still represents about <code>[DistTrainCommEnergyEnvelope.ib_power_kw_str]</code>. The per-boundary accounting behind these per-bit costs is developed in @sec-communication-collective-operations-physics-data-movement. Overlap hides communication time but not its energy, so avoiding unnecessary movement is the direct way to reduce the power term.</p>\n<p>In step time as well as power, as the device count grows for a fixed workload, the balance can move from a linear scaling regime toward a communication-bound regime. The transition has no universal GPU count, because it depends on compute per device, message sizes, topology, and overlap. Coordination overhead becomes consequential when its exposed time is no longer small relative to useful computation. Measurements at the intended workload and placement must establish the efficiency curve.</p>\n<p>The efficiency metrics directly influence the choice of parallelism strategy. Data parallelism works well in the linear scaling regime but becomes communication bound at scale. The model-parallel strategies developed after this section replace part of that gradient traffic with other costs, which the same efficiency equation prices once each mechanism is in place.</p>\n<p>Hardware efficiency metrics govern throughput, yet convergence theory determines whether distributed training reaches the same solution quality as single-device training. Parallelism affects optimization convergence in four ways: convergence rate changes with batch size, stale updates can weaken that rate, learning-rate schedules must be revalidated when the global batch changes, and adding workers yields diminishing returns beyond the critical batch size.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h3\">Convergence rate for synchronous data parallel SGD</h3>\n<p>Averaging gradients across $\\DeviceCount$ workers processes $\\DeviceCount$ times as many examples per step, but only reduces the number of steps to convergence when gradient noise, rather than loss curvature, is what holds back a larger step size. Formally, let $\\LossFunction$ be lower bounded by $\\LossLowerBound$ and have $\\SmoothnessConstant$-Lipschitz gradients, so the smoothness constant limits how sharply the gradient can change as parameters move. Each worker computes gradients from $\\LocalBatchSize$ independent samples. At update $\\OptimizerStepIndex$, the aggregated estimator $\\MinibatchGradient_t$, the average of the $\\DeviceCount$ workers' local gradients, is unbiased, and its conditional variance satisfies $\\mathbb{E}[\\|\\MinibatchGradient_t-\\nabla\\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters_t)\\|^2\\mid\\ModelParameters_t]\\leq\\SampleGradientStandardDeviation^2/(\\DeviceCount \\LocalBatchSize)$. When averaging reduces that noise, synchronous SGD makes steady progress toward parameters where the gradient is small. These assumptions bound progress toward a small gradient norm, not convergence to a global minimizer or a particular validation accuracy.</p>\n<p>For the update $\\ModelParameters_{t+1}=\\ModelParameters_t-\\LearningRate\\MinibatchGradient_t$ with constant step size $0&lt;\\LearningRate\\leq 1/\\SmoothnessConstant$, after $\\OptimizerUpdateCount$ synchronized updates,</p>\n<p>$$ \\frac{1}{\\OptimizerUpdateCount}\\sum_{t=0}^{\\OptimizerUpdateCount-1}\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\|\\nabla\\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters_t)\\|^2\\right] \\leq \\underbrace{\\frac{2\\bigl(\\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters_0)-\\LossLowerBound\\bigr)}{\\LearningRate \\OptimizerUpdateCount}}<em>{\\text{optimization term}} + \\underbrace{\\frac{\\LearningRate \\SmoothnessConstant\\SampleGradientStandardDeviation^2}{\\DeviceCount \\LocalBatchSize}}</em>{\\text{stochastic-variance term}}. $$</p>\n<p>Choosing $\\LearningRate=\\min\\!\\left\\{1/\\SmoothnessConstant,\\sqrt{2(\\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters_0)-\\LossLowerBound)\\DeviceCount \\LocalBatchSize/(\\SmoothnessConstant\\SampleGradientStandardDeviation^2\\OptimizerUpdateCount)}\\right\\}$ balances the two terms. Before the smoothness cap becomes active, the stochastic contribution scales as $\\mathcal{O}(1/\\sqrt{\\DeviceCount \\LocalBatchSize \\OptimizerUpdateCount})$.</p>\n<p>The theorem isolates the benefit that follows from the assumptions. With equal local batch size $\\LocalBatchSize$, synchronous data-parallel SGD computes the same minibatch estimator as one worker using batch size $\\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize$ for that update, and the estimator variance decreases as $1/(\\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize)$. That product appears only in the stochastic-variance term of the bound, so at a fixed step size a larger batch shrinks that term and leaves the optimization term alone. The reduction in updates comes through the step size. A smaller variance term lets the balanced step size grow, and the updates the bound requires fall in inverse proportion to the batch. Once the step size sits at the smoothness cap, the optimization term sets the update count, and added samples no longer reduce it in proportion. The critical batch size marks that diminishing return. How far added samples reduce those updates is the statistical efficiency of distributed training, separate from hardware efficiency. Because the theorem assumes perfect synchronization (BSP), convergence guarantees degrade as staleness increases whenever workers proceed at different rates or apply stale gradients.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h3\">Staleness impact across synchronization models</h3>\n<p>When a slow worker applies a gradient computed on an older parameter version, the update pushes the model along a direction that the current parameters may have already outgrown. The staleness parameter $\\GradientStaleness$ counts how many updates occur between computing and applying a gradient, tying the barrier savings of BSP, SSP, and ASP (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-synchronization-models-396f) to solution quality [@ho2013more; @dutta2018slow]. Because the damage from stale gradients depends on the objective, step-size schedule, worker count, and delay distribution, no single additive penalty applies across all runs.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Gradient staleness</strong></em> ($\\GradientStaleness$) is the number of parameter updates that occur between the time a gradient is computed and the time it is applied to the global model state.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: It represents the synchronization error in distributed optimization. Increasing $\\GradientStaleness$ can improve throughput by reducing barrier waits $(\\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount))$, but it typically degrades the rate of convergence, requiring more optimizer steps, and therefore more FLOPs, to reach the same accuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike network latency, which is a physical delay, staleness is an algorithmic offset that arises from the choice of synchronization protocol (e.g., ASP, SSP).</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that staleness is \"always bad.\" In reality, it is a throughput-convergence trade-off: for some large-scale workloads, allowing bounded staleness is one practical way to keep thousands of GPUs in use.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The convergence behavior differs across these models in ways that directly affect training cost and solution quality. In BSP ($\\GradientStaleness=0$), all workers compute on the same parameter version and aggregate before updating. Under the assumptions of theorem \\ref{thrm-distributed-training-convergence-rate-distributed-sgd}, the synchronized estimator inherits the $\\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize$ minibatch guarantee.</p>\n<p>SSP ($\\GradientStaleness\\leq \\StalenessBound$) allows workers to proceed up to $\\StalenessBound$ iterations ahead of the slowest worker. @ho2013more bound the average regret of their SSP update sequence at $\\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{(\\StalenessBound+1)\\DeviceCount/\\OptimizerUpdateCount})$ for $\\DeviceCount$ workers and $\\OptimizerUpdateCount$ updates. Average regret is the per-update gap between the loss its iterates incur and the loss of the best fixed parameters chosen in hindsight. The bound assumes convex Lipschitz component functions, a bounded domain, and a decreasing step size. It makes the staleness dependence explicit, since the bound grows with $\\StalenessBound$. It measures a different quantity from the gradient-norm bound of theorem \\ref{thrm-distributed-training-convergence-rate-distributed-sgd}, which covers nonconvex objectives, so the two rates cannot be compared directly.</p>\n<p>Asynchronous SGD imposes no protocol-wide finite delay bound. Removing the barrier can improve update throughput, but a convergence rate requires additional assumptions about realized delays, the objective, and the step-size schedule. @dutta2018slow analyze this as an error-runtime trade-off in which worker speed and gradient staleness jointly determine whether accepting a delayed update helps. Asynchronous execution alone therefore sets no fixed delay penalty and does not by itself forfeit the $\\DeviceCount$-worker variance reduction; both depend on those assumptions. The protocol fixes each model's delay control, but the strength of its convergence guarantee depends on further assumptions (@tbl-convergence-comparison).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Model</strong></th><th><strong>Delay control</strong></th><th><strong>Scoped guarantee</strong></th><th><strong>Systems effect</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>BSP</strong></td><td>$\\GradientStaleness=0$</td><td>Synchronous $\\DeviceCount\\times \\LocalBatchSize$-minibatch theorem \\ref{thrm-distributed-training-convergence-rate-distributed-sgd}</td><td>Waits for the slowest worker</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>SSP</strong></td><td>$\\GradientStaleness\\leq \\StalenessBound$</td><td>$\\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{(\\StalenessBound+1)\\DeviceCount/\\OptimizerUpdateCount})$ average regret under convex assumptions [@ho2013more]</td><td>Bounds delay while reducing some waits</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>ASP</strong></td><td>No protocol-wide finite bound</td><td>No universal rate without additional delay and objective assumptions</td><td>Removes the global barrier but applies stale updates</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Convergence Properties by Synchronization Model</strong>: The synchronization protocol determines the delay model; the convergence rate follows only after the objective, gradient, delay, and step-size assumptions are fixed. {#tbl-convergence-comparison tbl-colwidths=\"[8,20,47,25]\"}</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h3\">Learning rate scaling rules</h3>\n<p>Even under zero-staleness BSP ($\\GradientStaleness=0$ in @tbl-convergence-comparison), scaling out across $\\DeviceCount$ workers changes the optimization trajectory whenever the global batch grows with the cluster. Multiplying the global batch by $\\BatchScalingFactor$ cuts the number of optimizer updates per epoch by the same factor, so keeping the single-GPU learning rate leaves the model under-trained, while jumping straight to a $\\BatchScalingFactor$-fold larger step size can destabilize early epochs. Whenever data parallelism changes the effective batch size, the learning-rate schedule must be revalidated against the target quality; two scaling rules provide starting points for that adjustment.</p>\n<p>The linear scaling rule [@goyal2017accurate] proposes that when batch size is multiplied by $\\BatchScalingFactor$, learning rate also increases by $\\BatchScalingFactor$:</p>\n<p>$$ \\ScaledLearningRate = \\BatchScalingFactor\\cdot \\BaseLearningRate$$</p>\n<p>The rule rests on an approximation that compares one large-batch step at learning rate $\\BatchScalingFactor\\LearningRate$ with $\\BatchScalingFactor$ consecutive small-batch steps at learning rate $\\LearningRate$. At batch size $\\BatchScalingFactor\\times \\BatchSize$, independent gradient contributions reduce estimator variance by factor $\\BatchScalingFactor$, but lower variance alone does not establish stability. The approximation also requires gradients to change little over the corresponding $\\BatchScalingFactor$ small-batch steps, because only then do those steps add up to nearly the same update as the one large step. @goyal2017accurate validated the recipe for ResNet-50/ImageNet at global batch size <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.minibatch_str]</code> with gradual warmup. A warmup period increases the learning rate from $\\BaseLearningRate$ to $\\BatchScalingFactor\\cdot \\BaseLearningRate$ over the first $\\WarmupStepCount$ iterations to reduce early instability; the schedule still requires validation:</p>\n<p>$$ \\LearningRate_t = \\BaseLearningRate+ \\frac{\\OptimizerStepIndex}{\\WarmupStepCount}(\\BatchScalingFactor\\cdot \\BaseLearningRate- \\BaseLearningRate) \\quad \\text{for } \\OptimizerStepIndex&lt; \\WarmupStepCount$$</p>\n<p>The rule and the warmup were introduced together, and the run that introduced them shows why the scaled learning rate needs the warmup. A learning-rate schedule tuned for an eight-GPU run need not reach the same accuracy at <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.gpus_str]</code> once the global batch grows with the worker count.</p>\n<p><strong>Context</strong>: Facebook AI Research set out to train ResNet-50 on ImageNet in one hour using <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.gpus_str]</code>, pushing data parallelism far beyond the batch sizes that earlier practice treated as safe [@goyal2017accurate].</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: Scaling the global batch to <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.minibatch_str]</code> cut the number of optimizer updates per epoch by the same factor. Keeping the small-batch learning rate left too few effective updates to reach the target accuracy, while scaling the learning rate up by the same factor destabilized optimization in the first epochs, when the loss surface changes fastest.</p>\n<p><strong>Impact</strong>: Without the gradual warmup recipe, the large-batch runs reported higher training and validation error than the small-batch baseline. Higher hardware throughput alone did not preserve the quality target.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix</strong>: The team introduced the linear learning-rate scaling rule paired with a 5-epoch gradual warmup schedule, stabilizing optimization at batch size <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.minibatch_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: Distributed training demands more than parallel hardware alone. Scaling changes the optimization regime, so the cluster, communication schedule, batch size, and learning-rate schedule must be tuned as one system.</p>\n<p>The square root scaling rule is an alternative learning-rate heuristic to test when increasing batch size:</p>\n<p>$$ \\ScaledLearningRate = \\sqrt{\\BatchScalingFactor} \\cdot \\BaseLearningRate$$</p>\n<p>This rule increases the learning rate more slowly than linear scaling. Its suitability depends on the optimizer, schedule, and training regime; exceeding a critical-batch estimate does not make it mandatory or guarantee convergence. The learning rate must be validated against the same target quality.</p>\n<p>Layer-wise adaptive rate scaling (LARS) and layer-wise adaptive moments based optimizer (LAMB) provide related large-batch approaches for ImageNet and BERT-style pretraining [@you2017large; @you2020large]. LARS [@you2017large] scales each layer's update relative to its weight norm. The simplified LARS expression below illustrates the ratio of weight norm to gradient norm with weight decay. LAMB [@you2020large] applies a related layer-wise adaptation to an Adam-based update rather than using this raw-gradient denominator:</p>\n<p>$$ \\LearningRate_\\ell = \\LarsGlobalLearningRate\\cdot \\frac{\\|\\LayerWeights_\\ell\\|}{\\|\\MinibatchGradient_\\ell\\| + \\WeightPenaltyStrength\\|\\LayerWeights_\\ell\\|} $$</p>\n<p>Here $\\WeightPenaltyStrength$ is the weight-decay coefficient in the illustrated LARS expression. Layer-wise scaling controls update magnitude relative to parameter magnitude, but does not guarantee convergence for arbitrary batches. Its systems role is to extend the range of validated batch sizes over which added data-parallel workers can reduce time to quality.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-15\" class=\"cs-h3\">Critical batch size and diminishing returns</h3>\n<p>Even when layer-wise adaptive scaling keeps large-batch updates stable, expanding the global batch cannot reduce the optimization steps needed to reach a fixed target quality indefinitely. The critical batch size $\\CriticalBatchSize$ marks the transition point beyond which increasing batch size yields diminishing returns in convergence per sample seen.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Critical batch size</strong></em> $(\\CriticalBatchSize)$ is a workload-dependent crossover batch size beyond which further batch growth yields diminishing reductions in optimization steps per added sample [@mccandlish2018empirical; @Shallue2019measuring].</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: It characterizes statistical scaling, separately from hardware throughput. In the gradient-noise model, batches well below $\\CriticalBatchSize$ can reduce step count approximately in proportion to batch growth; above it, additional samples buy progressively smaller step-count reductions.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike the memory-limited batch size (determined by memory capacity and activation footprint), the critical batch size depends on the model, data, optimizer, training phase, and target quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that training can be sped up indefinitely by adding GPUs. In reality, $\\CriticalBatchSize$ is a guide to diminishing statistical returns, not a hard physical ceiling or a universal optimum in wall-clock time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The gradient-noise-scale proxy for the critical batch size can be estimated as:</p>\n<p>$$ \\CriticalBatchSize\\approx \\frac{\\text{tr}(\\GradientCovarianceMatrix)}{\\|\\nabla \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters)\\|^2} $$</p>\n<p>where $\\text{tr}(\\GradientCovarianceMatrix)$ is the trace of the gradient covariance matrix (total gradient variance) and $\\|\\nabla \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters)\\|^2$ is the squared gradient norm (signal strength). Intuitively, $\\CriticalBatchSize$ is the batch size at which averaging reduces gradient variance to the level of the true gradient magnitude.</p>\n<p>Published large-batch regimes illustrate the scale rather than providing fixed thresholds. On ImageNet, @goyal2017accurate stabilized ResNet-50 at global batch <code>[ImageNetOneHourRun.minibatch_str]</code> with warmup, and LARS extends ImageNet large-batch training further in some settings [@you2017large]. For BERT-Large pretraining, LAMB reports training at 32K--64K batch sizes with layer-wise adaptive updates [@you2020large]. Across other domains, @mccandlish2018empirical show useful batch-size limits ranging from tens of thousands in ImageNet-like settings to millions in reinforcement-learning workloads, so new frontier-language-model thresholds should be measured rather than copied.</p>\n<p>The gradient-noise model separates statistical efficiency from hardware efficiency. Well below $\\CriticalBatchSize$, where gradient noise rather than loss curvature holds back the step size, increasing batch size can reduce optimization steps almost proportionally while preserving similar total sample use. Near $\\CriticalBatchSize$, the trade-off between fewer updates and more samples becomes material. Above it, curvature caps the step size, so step count approaches a lower limit while sample use continues to increase. Neither maximum samples per second nor minimum training time necessarily occurs at $\\CriticalBatchSize$: those objectives also depend on device utilization, communication, and the validated learning-rate schedule.</p>\n<p>On log-log axes, the gradient-noise trade-off appears as a divergence between optimization step count and total sample consumption (@fig-critical-batch-size). In the normalized model plotted there, steps relative to their minimum equal one plus the critical batch divided by the batch, and samples relative to their minimum equal one plus the batch divided by the critical batch. Below the critical threshold ($\\BatchSize/\\CriticalBatchSize&lt; 1$), increasing batch size yields near-linear reductions in optimization steps with negligible sample waste. Past the crossover point at $\\BatchSize/\\CriticalBatchSize= 1$ (where both normalized costs equal 2), the step count curve asymptotically flattens toward 1 while total examples processed climb steeply along the $1 + \\BatchSize/\\CriticalBatchSize$ trajectory.</p>\n<p>Dividing the critical batch by the per-worker batch $\\LocalBatchSize$ estimates where worker growth leaves the near-proportional statistical scaling regime. With one local batch per update and its size held fixed, the crossover occurs around $\\DeviceCount\\approx \\CriticalBatchSize/\\LocalBatchSize$ workers. Past that point, additional workers mainly raise sample throughput that the optimizer cannot convert into proportionally fewer steps. Gradient accumulation increases each worker's contribution per update and lowers the corresponding worker count. Shrinking $\\LocalBatchSize$ moves the crossover outward but leaves less computation per worker to hide communication, coupling statistical and hardware efficiency.</p>\n<p>That crossover informs a cost-time trade-off rather than setting a mandatory worker count. Larger batches may still reduce time to target even as they consume more examples. That benefit must be established with measured step time and a validated learning-rate schedule at the same target quality. Changing synchronization or compression also changes the optimization process, so its communication savings must be weighed against any additional steps required.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\\Needspace{18\\baselineskip}</code></pre>\n<p>Given a 7B-parameter model distributed across a cluster of 64 A100 GPUs (80 GB HBM2e each), estimate the critical-batch crossover. Use a pilot trace with $\\text{tr}(\\GradientCovarianceMatrix)=1.6 \\times 10^6$, $\\|\\nabla \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters)\\|^2=100$, per-GPU batch $\\LocalBatchSize=4$, and a proposed gradient-accumulation depth of 32 steps, giving $\\GlobalBatchSize= 64 \\times 4 \\times 32 = 8{,}192$ samples.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Critical batch size</strong>: calculate the critical batch size $(\\CriticalBatchSize)$, where the gradient noise scale equals the batch size.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Proposed batch</strong>: use the critical batch size proxy $\\CriticalBatchSize\\approx \\frac{\\text{tr}(\\GradientCovarianceMatrix)}{\\|\\nabla \\LossFunction(\\ModelParameters)\\|^2}$ to evaluate the proposed batch size.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Efficiency distinction</strong>: explain why the gradient-noise estimate can assess statistical scaling but cannot establish hardware scaling efficiency without compute and communication timings.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h3\">Communication cost versus convergence speed</h3>\n<p>Communication savings matter only through the resulting time and cost to reach target model quality. Scaling a transformer language model from 8 to 64 workers, with a per-worker batch of $\\LocalBatchSize= 32$, shows how shorter steps and extra iterations to the same target combine into that cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong>: This illustrative comparison assumes the iteration counts needed to reach one fixed target quality. They are scenario inputs, not measured convergence results or predictions from GPU count. The baseline is <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_k_str]</code> iterations on single GPU with $\\LocalBatchSize=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_batch_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>8 workers (BSP)</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Effective batch size: $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_8_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_batch_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.batch_8_str]</code></li>\n<li>Learning rate: $\\LearningRate=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_8_str]</code> $\\times \\BaseLearningRate$ (illustrative candidate with warmup; validate for the workload)</li>\n<li>Expected iterations: <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_k_str]</code> $/$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_8_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.exp_iter_8_str]</code> iterations</li>\n<li>Assumed iterations to the same target: <code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_8bsp_k_str]</code> iterations (<code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_8_str]</code> efficiency)</li>\n<li>Communication overhead: <code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_8bsp_pct_str]</code> (NVLink intra-node)</li>\n<li>Wall-clock speedup: <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_k_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_factor_str]</code> $/$ (<code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_8bsp_k_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_8bsp_mult_str]</code>) = <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_8_mult_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\\tcbbreak</code></pre>\n<p><strong>64 workers (BSP)</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Effective batch size: $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_batch_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.batch_64_str]</code></li>\n<li>Learning rate ($\\DeviceCount=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> workers): $\\LearningRate= \\DeviceCount\\times \\BaseLearningRate$ is the scenario's assumed candidate schedule, not a prescription determined by $\\CriticalBatchSize$ alone</li>\n<li>Assuming $\\CriticalBatchSize\\approx$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.critical_batch_str]</code> (below critical): the useful-worker bound is $\\CriticalBatchSize/\\LocalBatchSize\\approx$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.critical_batch_str]</code> $/$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_batch_value_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.useful_workers_str]</code> workers, so <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> workers stay inside the near-proportional regime; this estimate does not validate a learning-rate schedule</li>\n<li>Expected iterations: <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_k_str]</code> $/$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.exp_iter_64_str]</code> iterations</li>\n<li>Assumed iterations to the same target: <code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_64bsp_k_str]</code> iterations (<code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_64_str]</code> efficiency)</li>\n<li>Communication overhead: <code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_64bsp_pct_str]</code> (InfiniBand inter-node, <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_nodes_64_str]</code>)</li>\n<li>Wall-clock speedup: <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_k_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_factor_str]</code> $/$ (<code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_64bsp_k_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_64bsp_mult_str]</code>) = <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_64_mult_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>SSP workers</strong>: <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> workers ($\\StalenessBound=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.ssp_staleness_str]</code>)</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Same effective batch size: $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.batch_64_str]</code></li>\n<li>Illustrative learning-rate reduction, not a staleness theorem: $\\StalenessAdjustedLearningRate= \\BspLearningRate/ \\sqrt{1 + \\StalenessBound}$ with $\\StalenessBound=$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.ssp_staleness_str]</code>, giving $\\StalenessAdjustedLearningRate\\approx$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.lr_ssp_str]</code> $\\times \\BspLearningRate$</li>\n<li>Expected iterations: Higher due to staleness penalty</li>\n<li>Assumed iterations to the same target: <code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_ssp_k_str]</code> iterations (<code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_ssp_str]</code> efficiency)</li>\n<li>Communication overhead: <code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_ssp_pct_str]</code> (reduced synchronization)</li>\n<li>Wall-clock speedup: <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_k_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.base_factor_str]</code> $/$ (<code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_ssp_k_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_ssp_mult_str]</code>) = <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_ssp_mult_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Analysis</strong> (@tbl-scaling-bsp-ssp)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Configuration</strong></th><th><strong>Iterations</strong></th><th><strong>Comm. overhead</strong></th><th><strong>Wall-clock speedup</strong></th><th><strong>Sample efficiency</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>1 GPU (baseline)</strong></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.base_iters_tbl_str]</code></td><td>0%</td><td>1$\\times$</td><td>100%</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>8 GPU BSP</strong></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_8bsp_tbl_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_8bsp_tbl_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_8_mult_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_8_tbl_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>64 GPU BSP</strong></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_64bsp_tbl_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_64bsp_tbl_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_64_mult_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_64_tbl_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>64 GPU SSP</strong></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_ssp_tbl_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_ssp_tbl_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_ssp_mult_str]</code></td><td><code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_ssp_tbl_str]</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Illustrative Scaling Comparison</strong>: Assumed iteration counts to one target quality, communication increments relative to local compute time, and the resulting speedup and sample efficiency. These are scenario calculations, not empirical optimizer benchmarks. {#tbl-scaling-bsp-ssp}</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\\tcbbreak</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code>-GPU BSP configuration achieves <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_64_round_mult_str]</code> speedup despite only <code>[ScalingWorkers.sample_eff_64_str]</code> sample efficiency because the communication overhead (<code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_64bsp_pct_str]</code>) is offset by the larger worker count. SSP at <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> workers comes close in wall-clock time with lower per-iteration communication (<code>[ScalingWorkers.comm_ssp_pct_str]</code>), but the extra iterations its stale updates require hold it to <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_ssp_mult_str]</code> against BSP's <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_64_mult_str]</code>. In this scenario it therefore finishes slightly later and costs slightly more than <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code>-worker BSP.</p>\n<p><strong>Cost Analysis</strong> (assuming <code>[ScalingWorkers.gpu_hour_rate_str]</code>):</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_8_str]</code>: <code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_8bsp_k_str]</code> iters $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.iter_s_8_str]</code>/iter $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_8_str]</code>/3600 $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.gpu_hour_rate_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.cost_8_str]</code></li>\n<li><code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> BSP: <code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_64bsp_k_str]</code> iters $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.iter_s_64bsp_str]</code>/iter $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code>/3600 $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.gpu_hour_rate_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.cost_64bsp_str]</code></li>\n<li><code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code> SSP: <code>[ScalingWorkers.actual_ssp_k_str]</code> iters $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.iter_s_64ssp_str]</code>/iter $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.n_workers_64_str]</code>/3600 $\\times$ <code>[ScalingWorkers.gpu_hour_rate_str]</code> = <code>[ScalingWorkers.cost_64ssp_str]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Despite higher parallelism, 64-GPU training costs more per run due to communication overhead and reduced sample efficiency. The 8-GPU configuration is more cost-efficient, but its <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_8_mult_str]</code> speedup against the 64-GPU run's <code>[ScalingWorkers.speedup_64_mult_str]</code> leaves it several times slower in wall-clock time. The choice depends on whether minimizing cost or minimizing time-to-result is the priority.</p>\n<p>On the cost-time plane of @fig-comm-convergence-tradeoff, 64-worker BSP dominates 64-worker SSP on both axes. SSP saves communication per iteration, but its stale updates need enough extra iterations to cost more and finish later, while 8-worker BSP remains the cheaper and slower option.</p>\n<p>Moving a job along this cost-time frontier requires shrinking each message, synchronizing less often, or relaxing the global reduction topology. Gradient compression shrinks the payload directly by transmitting quantized, sparsified, or low-rank approximations. Examples include sign-based updates [@bernstein2018signsgd], Deep Gradient Compression [@lin2018deep], PowerSGD [@vogels2020powersgd], quantized SGD with convergence analysis [@alistarh2017qsgd], and empirical sparse gradient dropping for neural machine translation [@aji2017].</p>\n<p>↩ Quantized, sparsified, and low-rank gradient compression, the error feedback that keeps them convergent, and the criteria for when they pay, are established in @sec-communication-collective-operations-collective-operations-gradient-compression-7a5c.</p>\n<p>Instead of shrinking each payload, Local SGD lowers synchronization frequency by letting workers perform $\\LocalUpdateCount$ local updates before averaging, which cuts collective calls per local update by a factor of $\\LocalUpdateCount$. Results for smooth, strongly convex objectives can recover synchronous SGD's asymptotic rate under specified learning-rate and local-update-interval conditions [@stich2019local], so the synchronization interval remains part of the convergence contract rather than an unrestricted communication saving.</p>\n<p>Where Local SGD keeps a global AllReduce at wider intervals, Decentralized SGD replaces the global collective with sparse neighbor exchanges along a communication graph. Immediate bandwidth demand drops, at the cost of taking additional rounds to spread information across the group. Graph connectivity, link service time, and the optimization effect of delayed mixing determine whether that trade-off helps a geographically distributed workload.</p>\n<p>Choosing among these three levers depends on which bottleneck dominates and how many extra iterations the altered update costs. Each candidate must be judged by measured time to the same target quality. A narrower numeric format offers one more way to shrink the payload without changing the collective schedule, provided the collective transmits the narrower values.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h3\">FP8 for distributed training</h3>\n<p>Running matrix multiplications in 8-bit floating point (FP8) accelerates local compute, which can make a distributed job <em>more</em> communication-bound if gradients still cross the network in 16-bit precision. Compute precision and communication precision are separate choices: casting GEMM operands to half as many bytes reduces operand storage and local memory traffic, but it halves a collective payload only when the communication path explicitly transmits that narrower representation.</p>\n<p>↩ The throughput that narrower precisions buy on current accelerators is established in @sec-compute-infrastructure-precision-throughput-tradeoffs-49a5.</p>\n<p>FP8 matters to distributed training only after four dtypes are distinguished: the stored parameter dtype, the GEMM operand dtype, the accumulator or master-state dtype, and the collective wire dtype. Traditional mixed-precision training commonly retains FP32 optimizer or master state while using FP16/BF16 operands. FP8-capable accelerators add E4M3 (four exponent bits and three mantissa bits) and E5M2 (five exponent bits and two mantissa bits) operand formats optimized for different tensor distributions [@micikevicius2022fp8].</p>\n<p>The two FP8 formats trade precision against range. E4M3 keeps an extra mantissa bit for scaled <em>weights and activations</em>, which cluster within a predictable range, while E5M2 keeps an extra exponent bit for scaled <em>gradients</em>, which can span many orders of magnitude during backpropagation. Because tensor magnitudes drift during training, each tensor is multiplied before conversion by a scale, commonly derived from its recent maximum absolute value, a policy called <em>dynamic scaling</em>. Neither the format nor the scaling policy guarantees that every value survives conversion, so training quality must be validated for each workload.</p>\n<p>↪ Per-parameter training memory under each of these formats is collected for reference in @sec-appdx-dam-taxonomy-training-memory.</p>\n<p>An FP8 training contract pairs FP32 master or optimizer state and FP8 GEMM operands with higher-precision accumulation, and it can approach FP16 training quality on suitable workloads [@micikevicius2022fp8]. FP8 halves bytes only for tensors actually stored or transmitted in FP8, so a collective that reduces FP16 gradients still moves FP16 bytes even when every preceding GEMM ran in FP8. FP8 therefore earns its place in distributed training when lower-precision compute shortens the local critical path, or when an explicit communication cast shrinks payloads while the accumulation and scaling policy protects convergence. It changes representation, not the parallelization axis. Compression, local updates, and narrower formats all make each data-parallel step cheaper, yet none of them partitions an individual layer or its activations; that remaining capacity constraint motivates model parallelism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h2\">Model Parallelism</h2>\n<p>Sharded data parallelism distributes stored state but still asks each worker to execute every layer on its local examples. If one layer's gathered parameters and working memory exceed device capacity, or its execution is too slow for the target schedule, splitting that computation becomes a candidate. Model parallelism addresses this form of the memory capacity gap (principle \\ref{pri-memory-capacity-gap}) by assigning different computations to different devices. Its value must be compared with offload, recomputation, and smaller local batches under the same throughput and quality requirements.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Model parallelism</strong></em> is a distributed training strategy that partitions a single neural network's parameters or operations across multiple devices so each device computes a distinct portion of the model.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: If a model with parameter state $\\WeightMemory$ is split across $\\TensorParallelDegree \\PipelineParallelDegree$ model-parallel devices, the ideal per-device parameter footprint falls toward $\\WeightMemory/(\\TensorParallelDegree \\PipelineParallelDegree)$, enabling training of models that exceed single-device memory. The cost is extra communication for activations and gradients plus idle time from sequential dependencies, so the memory gain must exceed the transfer and pipeline-bubble overhead introduced by the partition.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike data parallelism, which replicates the full model on every worker and shards the minibatch, model parallelism shards the model itself. Pipeline parallelism and tensor parallelism are implementation forms of model parallelism: one partitions layers into stages, while the other partitions individual tensor operations.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that model parallelism automatically speeds up training. Its possible gains are capacity, less traffic on the slow tier, and shorter layer time, and whichever gain a plan relies on must justify the added transfer and idle cost. Without careful scheduling and overlap, downstream devices wait for upstream activations and utilization can fall below a single-device baseline.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Memory fit alone does not settle whether a model needs this kind of partition. For a <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code>-parameter model, weights alone occupy <code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.weight_gb_str]</code>, or about <code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.weight_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU across a <code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.n_gpus_str]</code>-GPU group, 1/<code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.cluster_fraction_denominator_str]</code> of the <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_gpus_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster. Full mixed-precision Adam training state is much larger, <code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.full_state_gb_str]</code> globally, yet ZeRO-3 across the same <code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.n_gpus_str]</code> GPUs reduces it to roughly <code>[ModelParallelMemoryFacts.full_state_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU before activations, which fits an <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.h100_mem_gb_str]</code> accelerator. ZeRO-3 can therefore make the static state fit, as the notebook below confirms. It pays for that fit in traffic, because every step regathers every layer's parameters across the data-parallel fabric, roughly three times the model's parameter bytes per worker (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-memoryefficient-data-parallelism-zero-fsdp-0e69). It also cannot split an individual layer's computation or activations across devices.</p>\n<p>Activations add a separate memory requirement. Under the earlier unchecked-storage assumptions, a <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.seq_len_str]</code>-token sequence through <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> parameters produces roughly <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.act_unchecked_tb_str]</code> at a local batch of <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.micro_batch_str]</code> sequences; optimizer sharding does not reduce that storage. Recomputation trades memory for extra FLOPs, partitioning distributes activations, and offload moves them to another memory tier. Kernel and batch-size choices also change the budget. Full activation checkpointing brings the same modeled case down to <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.act_checkpointed_gb_str]</code>. The following notebook, which uses a single-sequence local batch, checks whether ZeRO-3 plus checkpointing fits the model without partitioning its computation.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Training a <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code>-parameter model (like GPT-3) on a 64-GPU group of NVIDIA A100s (<code>[A100CapacityContext.a100_mem_gb_str]</code>), smaller than the <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.reliability_gpu_count_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster. The A100 carries the same <code>[A100CapacityContext.a100_mem_gb_str]</code> of HBM as the reference H100, so the capacity verdict carries over, and a small group is the harder test of whether sharding alone suffices. Can data parallelism with ZeRO-3 handle this?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Parameter storage</strong>: <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> params $\\times$ <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.bytes_fp16_str]</code> bytes (FP16) = <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.param_gb_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gradients and optimizer state</strong>: Gradients add <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> params $\\times$ <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.bytes_fp16_str]</code> bytes = <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.gradient_gb_str]</code>, while Adam FP32 master weights, momentum, and variance add <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> params $\\times$ <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.bytes_adam_str]</code> bytes = <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.optim_gb_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Total static memory</strong>: <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.total_static_gb_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>ZeRO-3 sharding</strong>: With <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.n_gpus_str]</code>, per-GPU static memory = <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.total_static_gb_str]</code>/<code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.n_gpus_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.per_gpu_gb_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Activation memory</strong>: For sequence length <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.seq_len_str]</code> and batch size <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.micro_batch_str]</code>, the canonical full-checkpointing strategy stores $\\approx$ <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.activation_gb_str]</code> of activations per GPU.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.per_gpu_gb_str]</code> (static) + <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.activation_gb_str]</code> (dynamic) = <code>[MemoryWallAnalysis.total_per_gpu_gb_str]</code>. Under this simplified accounting, ZeRO-3 plus full activation checkpointing fits within the <code>[A100CapacityContext.a100_mem_gb_str]</code> A100 capacity, leaving material headroom for framework buffers and transient layer gathers. If those additional allocations exceed the remaining HBM, the design can reduce the local batch, offload state, increase the sharding degree, or add tensor or pipeline parallelism; tensor parallelism is not the only recourse.</p>\n<p>In this example, sharding and checkpointing make the modeled state fit, leaving two distinct reasons to partition the model itself. One is regather traffic. On the <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_gpus_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster, ZeRO-3 moves about <code>[Zero3GatherCost.traffic_tb_str]</code> per worker per step for this model, which on one NDR InfiniBand port takes about <code>[Zero3GatherCost.ratio_mult_str]</code> as each GPU's computation for one training step, and longer still when gradient accumulation regathers the parameters for every microbatch. The other is layer execution, since full sharding gathers parameters on demand without ever splitting a single layer's computation or activations across devices.</p>\n<p>Model parallelism instead cuts the computation graph directly, either between sequential layer groups (such as layers 1--4 and layers 5--8) or inside a layer across channels and attention heads. Layer cuts introduce pipeline dependencies that leave downstream devices idle while an upstream stage computes, creating the pipeline bubble that governs utilization for this partition (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-pipeline-parallelism-8748). Channel and head cuts, the tensor parallelism of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-tensor-parallelism-d76e, address both regather traffic and layer execution by partitioning individual weight matrices across devices. They expose concurrent work within a layer at the price of intra-layer exchanges, which the column-then-row pairing of @fig-tensor-parallel-split confines to block boundaries.</p>\n<p>These cuts enable the large-scale cases that exceed a single device for different reasons. GPT-3, with <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> parameters, was trained with model parallelism within each matrix multiplication and across layers [@brown2020language]. That partition avoids the full-parameter regather that ZeRO-3 pays on every step. Vision transformers processing high-resolution $16\\Glyph{k}{\\times}16\\Glyph{k}$ pixel images use model parallelism to bound activation memory. Mixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures, whose sparsely activated feed-forward blocks are developed in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-expert-parallelism-mixture-experts-bc45, distribute their conditional computation paths across hardware [@shazeer2017outrageously; @lepikhin2021gshard; @fedus2022switch].</p>\n<p>Each partitioning strategy trades single-device memory overflow for a different systems cost: layer-wise partitioning pays in idle time, pipeline parallelism pays in scheduling complexity to hide that idle time, and tensor parallelism pays in NVLink bandwidth to split individual layers. Sequence and context parallelism, expert parallelism, and sparse embedding sharding then extend the same trade-off to long-context activations, sparsely activated experts, and sparsely touched embedding tables.</p>\n<p>Verify your understanding of model sharding:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Memory relief</strong>: determine whether model parallelism reduces the memory footprint per GPU for a given model.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Sequential dependency</strong>: identify whether sequential dependencies between layer-wise model shards arise in the forward pass, the backward pass, or both.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Pipeline bubble</strong>: explain why unscheduled layer-wise model parallelism can lead to lower GPU utilization than data parallelism through the \"pipeline bubble.\"</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Layer vs. tensor split</strong>: distinguish layer-based and tensor-based ways to split a Transformer layer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h3\">Layer-wise partitioning</h3>\n<p>Where ZeRO-3 met the capacity limit by sharding storage while every worker still executed every layer, layer-wise model parallelism gives each device a <em>unique</em> contiguous block of layers to compute. It is the least invasive model cut: with <code>[FrontierModelParallelPipelineRecap.model_parallel_ways_str]</code>-way partitioning, the <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code>-parameter model's <code>[FrontierModelParallelPipelineRecap.weights_gb_str]</code> of FP16 weights fall to approximately <code>[FrontierModelParallelPipelineRecap.weights_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU, though activations, gradients, and optimizer state still need sharding, offload, or pipeline scheduling (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-12a0).</p>\n<p>Because consecutive layer blocks remain sequentially dependent, however, a single batch passing through those stages in order leaves every other GPU waiting for its turn. In a 16-layer transformer placed four consecutive layers per GPU (@fig-dist-layers-blocks), panel (a) shows each device holding one-quarter of the weights and exchanging only boundary activations and gradients, while panel (b) reveals the utilization penalty: without microbatch pipelining, each GPU computes in only two of the eight schematic time slots and sits idle for the other six.</p>\n<p>Partitioning layers changes where an optimizer update runs without necessarily changing the update rule. Each GPU can apply Adam [@kingma2014adam], AdaFactor, or another optimizer to its parameter slice once the required gradients are complete. A device holding transformer layers 1--12 updates those layers. Tied parameters, which share one weight matrix across stages, can still require coordination, as can global gradient clipping, whose norm spans every stage, and a combined data-parallel group. Preserving synchronous optimization also requires the schedule to use consistent parameter versions. Sequential dependencies introduce pipeline bubbles, and unequal stage service times add waiting. The engineering problem is to overlap microbatches and balance stage work while preserving that update contract [@rasley2020].</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-20\" class=\"cs-h3\">Pipeline parallelism</h3>\n<p>Without pipelining, a four-stage layer partition leaves three of the four GPUs idle at any moment because downstream stages cannot start until upstream stages finish the entire batch (@fig-dist-layers-blocks). Pipeline parallelism breaks that stall by splitting the batch into smaller microbatches and streaming them through the stages like a relay race. As soon as GPU 1 finishes the initial transformer blocks on microbatch 1, it hands the resulting activation tensor to GPU 2 and immediately starts microbatch 2. For the <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> model with hidden dimension <code>[FrontierModelParallelPipelineRecap.hidden_dim_str]</code> and a microbatch of <code>[Gpt3ActivationBudget.micro_batch_str]</code> sequences at <code>[FrontierModelParallelPipelineRecap.seq_len_str]</code> tokens each, that forward handoff carries approximately <code>[FrontierModelParallelPipelineRecap.handoff_mb_str]</code> per stage boundary per microbatch. GPU 2 needs that payload before processing the same microbatch, creating a dependency chain through the stages, while other microbatches occupy the remaining stages concurrently. Backward propagates activation gradients in reverse as each stage computes gradients for its own parameters.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Pipeline parallelism</strong></em> is a model parallelism technique that partitions a neural network's layers into sequential stages assigned to different devices, passing activations forward and gradients backward between stages while overlapping computation across stages using microbatches to maintain throughput.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Each boundary transfers forward activations and backward activation gradients. A forward tensor contains $\\MicrobatchSize \\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension\\times 2$ bytes at BF16, where $\\MicrobatchSize$ is the microbatch size. For a hidden dimension of <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.hidden_dim_str]</code> with microbatch size <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.micro_batch_str]</code> and a <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.seq_len_str]</code>-token sequence, the forward payload is approximately <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.hidden_dim_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.seq_len_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.dtype_bytes_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.payload_mb_str]</code> MB per microbatch per boundary. Backward traffic and all microbatches must be counted before comparing this with a per-update gradient AllReduce or sizing a <code>[PipelineBoundaryPayload.ib_gb_per_s_str]</code> InfiniBand link. In a balanced noninterleaved schedule with ideal transfers, the idle fraction is approximately $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)/(\\MicrobatchCount+\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)$, where $\\PipelineParallelDegree$ is the stage count and $\\MicrobatchCount$ the microbatch count.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Tensor parallelism partitions operations within a layer and introduces communication among their participants. Pipeline parallelism instead transfers activations and their gradients between sequential stages. This coarser exchange can suit slower links when the transfer fits within the stage schedule; the interconnect name alone does not establish feasibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that adding pipeline stages always improves throughput. Each additional stage increases the pipeline bubble fraction $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)/(\\MicrobatchCount+\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)$ unless the number of microbatches $\\MicrobatchCount$ scales proportionally, causing hardware to idle and erasing the benefit of deeper partitioning.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In the GPipe schedule of @fig-pipeline-parallelism, a four-stage pipeline processes four microbatches ($\\PipelineParallelDegree=4, \\MicrobatchCount=4$). Dividing the global batch into discrete microbatches allows forward passes to overlap diagonally across stages before backward passes unfold in reverse order. While microbatching raises hardware utilization above naive sequential placement, pipeline fill ($\\PipelineTimeSlot_1$--$\\PipelineTimeSlot_3$) and drain ($\\PipelineTimeSlot_{12}$--$\\PipelineTimeSlot_{14}$) bubbles still leave each device idle for 6 of 14 time slots.</p>\n<p>Tracing the schedule grid in @fig-pipeline-parallelism column by column reveals how the fill and drain bubbles arise. In the first slot only stage 0 has work, the forward pass of microbatch 1. In the second, stage 0 starts microbatch 2 while stage 1 receives microbatch 1's activations, and by the fourth slot every stage holds a different microbatch. The last stage then turns microbatch 4 around into its backward pass, and gradients travel upstream in reverse microbatch order, so stage 0 waits until the eleventh slot for its first gradient. Each stage therefore idles <code>[FourStagePipelineWalkthrough.fill_slots_str]</code> slots in the forward phase and <code>[FourStagePipelineWalkthrough.fill_slots_str]</code> more in the backward phase, <code>[FourStagePipelineWalkthrough.idle_slots_str]</code> of the <code>[FourStagePipelineWalkthrough.slots_str]</code> slots in the step. Those idle slots may fall at the start of its row, the middle, or the end. That share, about <code>[FourStagePipelineWalkthrough.bubble_pct_str]</code>, is the bubble fraction $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)/(\\MicrobatchCount+\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)$ at $\\PipelineParallelDegree=\\MicrobatchCount=$ <code>[FourStagePipelineWalkthrough.stages_str]</code>. Adding microbatches lengthens the busy diagonal without lengthening fill and drain in this balanced schedule. Increasing $\\MicrobatchCount$ is therefore one way to reduce the idle fraction; later schedules also change which work can occupy the gaps.</p>\n<p>The same bubble term becomes concrete in the following worked example, where an 8-stage pipeline and 32 microbatches still leave a measurable amount of idle time.</p>\n<p>↪ The bubble fraction across stage and microbatch counts, and its interleaved-schedule variant, are developed in @sec-appdx-comm-foundations-pipeline-arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A large-model training run uses pipeline parallelism across <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_stages_str]</code>. To hide the sequential delay, the batch is split into <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_microbatches_str]</code> microbatches. What fraction of GPU cycles does the pipeline bubble leave idle?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: For balanced, noninterleaved synchronous stages under the one-forward-one-backward (1F1B) schedule developed below, with ideal transfers, the bubble fraction follows from the stage and microbatch counts. The time units below each combine one forward and one backward service interval; they are not individual optimizer steps.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Wait time</strong>: At the start and end of each batch, GPUs sit idle for $\\PipelineParallelDegree-1$ steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Productive time</strong>: GPUs compute for $\\MicrobatchCount$ steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Bubble fraction</strong>: $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1) / (\\PipelineParallelDegree-1+\\MicrobatchCount) =$ <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_p_minus_1_str]</code> $/$ (<code>[PipelineBubble.pb_p_minus_1_str]</code> $+$ <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_microbatches_str]</code>) $\\approx$ <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_bubble_pct_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Pipeline parallelism is a <em>utilization-memory trade-off</em>. With this stage count and schedule fixed, reducing the bubble from <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_bubble_pct_round_str]</code> to 5 percent requires more microbatches $(\\MicrobatchCount)$. In general, holding the bubble under 10 percent requires $\\MicrobatchCount&gt; 9(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)$, because $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)/(\\MicrobatchCount+\\PipelineParallelDegree-1) &lt; 0.1$ rearranges to that bound. The activation memory those microbatches need depends on the schedule. GPipe retains activations across the microbatches, whereas the 1F1B schedule developed next releases them during the steady state. The modeled <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_bubble_pct_str]</code> idle fraction also motivates interleaved pipelining, which divides each device's work into smaller stages. Interleaving can reduce the original <code>[PipelineBubble.pb_bubble_pct_round_str]</code> bubble, but additional transfers and memory requirements determine the net gain.</p>\n<p>GPipe [@huang2019gpipe] introduced synchronous pipeline parallelism with microbatch accumulation, while PipeDream [@narayanan2019] developed asynchronous approaches with weight stashing, which keeps one parameter version per in-flight minibatch so that its forward and backward passes use the same weights. Modern systems use 1F1B (one-forward-one-backward) scheduling to reclaim activation memory earlier. That lower memory pressure makes larger microbatch counts practical, and the larger $\\MicrobatchCount$ is what shrinks the bubble term.</p>\n<p>: <strong>1F1B (one-forward-one-backward) scheduling</strong>: Unlike GPipe (which processes all forward passes before any backward passes), 1F1B interleaves them. For equal-sized microbatch activations and a non-interleaved schedule, peak retained activations per stage fall from $\\mathcal{O}(\\MicrobatchCount)$ under GPipe to at most $\\mathcal{O}(\\PipelineParallelDegree)$ under 1F1B, where $\\MicrobatchCount$ is the microbatch count and $\\PipelineParallelDegree$ the pipeline-stage count. Summed across stages, the corresponding bounds are $\\mathcal{O}(\\MicrobatchCount\\PipelineParallelDegree)$ and $\\mathcal{O}(\\PipelineParallelDegree^2)$. This memory reclamation is what enables the large microbatch counts $(\\MicrobatchCount\\gg \\PipelineParallelDegree)$ required to keep the pipeline bubble small.</p>\n<p>: <strong>GPipe</strong>: Published by Google in 2019, GPipe introduced synchronous microbatch pipelining that trained a 557M-parameter AmoebaNet across 8 Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) with near-linear scaling. Its schedule keeps every microbatch's activations live until the backward pass, the cost that later schedules reduce.</p>\n<p>At the schedule level, 1F1B is a warmup, steady-state, and drain loop. In @algo-one-forward-one-backward-pipeline, each stage alternates forward work for newer microbatches with backward work for older microbatches once gradients arrive, so activations can be released before the whole batch has completed.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| label: algo-one-forward-one-backward-pipeline\n#| pdf-line-number: true\n#| pdf-right-comment: false\n#| html-comment-delimiter: &quot;▷&quot;\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{One-forward-one-backward pipeline schedule}\n\\begin{algorithmic}\n\\Require $\\PipelineparallelDegreePp$ ordered pipeline stages; $m$ microbatches; activation links $j\\to j{+}1$ and gradient links $j{+}1\\to j$\n\\Ensure one optimizer update after gradients accumulate across all $m$ microbatches\n\\State split the global batch into $m$ microbatches; assign consecutive layers to the $\\PipelineparallelDegreePp$ stages\n\\State warm-up: launch forwards from stage 0, forwarding each activation downstream as produced\n\\For{each stage in steady state}\n    \\State alternate one backward (oldest ready microbatch) with one forward (next microbatch)\n    \\State release a microbatch&#x27;s activation as soon as that stage&#x27;s backward consumes it\n\\EndFor\n\\State drain: finish the remaining backward passes in reverse stage order\n\\State apply the optimizer update once every stage has accumulated gradients for all $m$ microbatches\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}</code></pre>\n<p>Run on four stages with eight microbatches, the schedule gives each stage a short warm-up of forward passes, a steady state that alternates one backward with one forward, and a drain of the remaining backward passes (@fig-pipeline-1f1b). The warm-up and drain phases still leave a bubble of roughly $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)/(\\MicrobatchCount+\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)$, 6 of 22 slots here. Interleaving backward work in the steady state, however, frees each activation as soon as its backward consumes it. Stage 0 never holds more than four microbatches' activations where an all-forwards-first schedule would hold all eight, so peak activation memory grows with the stage count rather than the microbatch count. That stage-bounded memory footprint is the gain over GPipe-style all-forward-then-all-backward scheduling, and it lets the system raise $\\MicrobatchCount$ until activation memory, recomputation, or the global-batch constraint becomes the next limit.</p>\n<p>The activation handoff has shape $\\MicrobatchSize \\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension$; its completion still constrains the next stage. The 1F1B schedule in @algo-one-forward-one-backward-pipeline reduces activation residency, but fill, drain, communication, and load imbalance leave idle time.</p>\n<p>Zero-bubble pipeline schedules further reduce idle time by decoupling the two matrix multiplications inside each layer's backward pass. In a standard backward pass, the GPU computes both $\\partial \\LossFunction/ \\partial \\LayerInputMatrix$ (the activation gradient propagated backward to the preceding stage) and $\\partial \\LossFunction/ \\partial \\WeightMatrix$ (the weight gradient accumulated locally for the optimizer step) back-to-back as a single backward unit. Zero-bubble scheduling splits them into separate kernels. A B kernel multiplies the incoming upstream gradient by the stage's persistent weights $\\WeightMatrix^T$ to compute only the activation gradient $\\partial \\LossFunction/ \\partial \\LayerInputMatrix$ and immediately sends it to the previous stage. A W kernel multiplies the saved forward activation $\\LayerInputMatrix^T$ by the upstream gradient to compute the weight gradient $\\partial \\LossFunction/ \\partial \\WeightMatrix$ locally. Because upstream stages cannot begin their own backward work until they receive $\\partial \\LossFunction/ \\partial \\LayerInputMatrix$, the B kernel sits on the critical path and must execute promptly, whereas the W kernel has no cross-stage dependency and can be scheduled opportunistically to fill local pipeline bubbles.</p>\n<p>The B/W split creates scheduling freedom, although all deferred W kernels must still finish before the corresponding optimizer update. In the balanced four-stage, eight-microbatch schedule of @fig-pipeline-1f1b, noninterleaved 1F1B still leaves $3/11 \\approx 27$ percent of each stage's time idle, by the bubble fraction derived above. Reordering forward, activation-gradient, and weight-gradient work allows deferred W kernels to soak up those idle slots. Approaching zero bubble time in practice also depends on kernel durations, memory headroom, and optimizer synchronization, so splitting the backward pass alone does not guarantee it.</p>\n<p>The trade-off is memory. Because the B kernel needs only the incoming gradient and the persistent weights $\\WeightMatrix$, whereas the W kernel requires the saved forward activation $\\LayerInputMatrix$, a stage cannot free a microbatch's forward activation when B finishes. Deferring W to fill a later bubble forces the stage to keep both $\\LayerInputMatrix$ and the incoming gradient resident in device memory until W finally executes, increasing peak memory usage. Some implementations address this by combining zero-bubble scheduling with activation checkpointing, selectively recomputing certain activations before W rather than storing them across the delay. The interaction between these techniques creates a three-way trade-off among pipeline bubble size, memory consumption, and recomputation overhead, an example of the displacement of overhead (principle \\ref{pri-conservation-overhead}).</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-21\" class=\"cs-h3\">Tensor parallelism</h3>\n<p>Every pipeline schedule so far keeps whole layers on each device, which is why its traffic is confined to stage boundaries and tolerates moderate interconnect bandwidth. The same property sets its limit. A pipeline cannot help when a single layer's weights or activations exceed one device, and it cannot shorten the time one layer takes, because that layer still runs on one accelerator.</p>\n<p>Tensor parallelism splits the weight matrices within each layer instead of assigning complete layers to devices. This operator-level parallelism (also called intra-layer parallelism) enables finer-grained distribution but requires high-bandwidth interconnects for the frequent intra-layer communication it introduces.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Tensor parallelism</strong></em> partitions individual tensor operations across devices, commonly through column-parallel or row-parallel weight splits. Communication combines or redistributes the resulting tensor shards as required by the next operation. The Megatron-style forward-pass example below illustrates one such arrangement.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The Megatron-LM arrangement developed below uses two AllReduce operations per transformer block in the forward pass, one after attention and one after the MLP. Each reduces a 2-byte activation tensor of $\\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension$ elements, where $\\BatchSize$ counts the sequences in flight through the layer. A ring AllReduce moves roughly $2(\\TensorParallelDegree-1)/\\TensorParallelDegree$ times that payload per GPU, and the exchange recurs in every one of the <code>[TensorParallelBandwidth.layers_str]</code> blocks of the GPT-3 shape, with backward adding corresponding exchanges. Whether that traffic hides behind computation depends on the link that carries it, which the arithmetic after the mechanism quantifies.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike pipeline parallelism, which communicates only at layer boundaries and transfers small activation slices, tensor parallelism synchronizes within every layer via AllReduce. It is therefore sensitive to the per-operation latency of the interconnect and favors a fast communication domain such as NVLink, which can extend beyond one node.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Extending tensor parallelism across nodes without checking the transfer budget can erase the compute saving. The reference NVLink specification is approximately <code>[TensorParallelBandwidth.nvlink_h100_gb_per_s_str]</code> bidirectional, or <code>[TensorParallelBandwidth.nvlink_h100_perdir_gb_per_s_str]</code> per direction; InfiniBand NDR provides <code>[TensorParallelBandwidth.ib_ndr_gb_per_s_str]</code> per port, a <code>[TensorParallelBandwidth.nvlink_ib_gap_mult_str]</code> per-direction gap. These specifications identify a placement risk. Whether the split helps depends on sustained bandwidth, operation size, compute saved, and overlap.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Frequent intra-layer exchanges favor placing tensor-parallel groups within the fastest available communication domain. Pipeline stages communicate at coarser boundaries and can often span slower domains when their transfers fit the schedule. This difference motivates a hybrid that maps each exchange to a suitable fabric tier; it does not impose a universal node boundary for either strategy.</p>\n<p>Megatron-style tensor parallelism [@shoeybi2019megatron] partitions matrix multiplications by alternating column and row splits. In the two-GPU dataflow of @fig-tensor-parallel-split, column-parallel splitting partitions the first weight matrix $\\ColumnParallelWeightMatrix= [\\ColumnParallelWeightMatrix_1 \\mid \\ColumnParallelWeightMatrix_2]$ to produce local activation slices $\\LayerOutputMatrix_1, \\LayerOutputMatrix_2$ from replicated input $\\LayerInputMatrix$. Row-parallel splitting then partitions the second weight matrix $\\RowParallelWeightMatrix= [\\RowParallelWeightMatrix_1 ; \\RowParallelWeightMatrix_2]$ so that each GPU computes partial products $\\RowParallelOutputMatrix_1, \\RowParallelOutputMatrix_2$ locally. This paired split defers cross-device communication to a single AllReduce that reconstructs output $\\RowParallelOutputMatrix$.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Megatron-LM</strong>: NVIDIA's 2019 framework that trained an <code>[MegatronLm2019Run.params_b_str]</code>-parameter transformer (<code>[MegatronLm2019Run.bert_ratio_mult_str]</code> BERT-Large and <code>[MegatronLm2019Run.gpt2_ratio_mult_str]</code> GPT-2 at the time) with only two AllReduce operations per transformer block in the forward pass (one after attention, one after the multilayer perceptron (MLP)). The run reached <code>[MegatronLm2019Run.scaling_efficiency_str]</code> scaling efficiency across <code>[MegatronLm2019Run.gpus_str]</code>, and its partitioning remains influential in large-scale transformer training.</p>\n<p>Column-parallel linear layers split weights along columns. For input $\\LayerInputMatrix$ and weight matrix $\\WeightMatrix= [\\WeightMatrix_1 \\mid \\WeightMatrix_2]$ split across 2 GPUs: $$\\LayerOutputMatrix= \\LayerInputMatrix\\WeightMatrix= \\LayerInputMatrix[\\WeightMatrix_1 \\mid \\WeightMatrix_2] = [\\LayerInputMatrix\\WeightMatrix_1 \\mid \\LayerInputMatrix\\WeightMatrix_2]$$ Each GPU computes its partition independently. The concatenation in the equation is logical, because when the next operation is row-parallel, each output shard remains on its producing device and feeds the matching weight shard without an AllGather. The row-parallel half of the pattern splits weights along rows, $\\WeightMatrix= \\begin{bmatrix} \\WeightMatrix_1 \\\\ \\WeightMatrix_2 \\end{bmatrix}$, and the layer's input arrives split along its columns to match, each GPU holding the shard it kept from a preceding column-parallel layer: $$\\LayerOutputMatrix= \\LayerInputMatrix\\WeightMatrix= \\LayerInputMatrix_1 \\WeightMatrix_1 + \\LayerInputMatrix_2 \\WeightMatrix_2$$ Each GPU computes a partial sum, and the system pays one AllReduce to combine the partial outputs.</p>\n<p>The column-then-row sequence of @fig-tensor-parallel-split embodies the governing architectural invariant. Pairing a column-parallel layer directly with a row-parallel layer allows intermediate activations ($\\LayerOutputMatrix_1, \\LayerOutputMatrix_2$) to remain strictly local on each GPU, confining inter-device synchronization to a single collective at the end of the block. This locality holds because the intermediate operation between the two projections acts independently on each GPU's column slice without cross-rank data.</p>\n<p>Megatron applies that pattern twice in each transformer block. In multi-head attention, the QKV projection is column-parallel along the head dimension so that each GPU receives the complete query, key, and value vectors for a disjoint subset of $\\AttentionHeadCount/\\TensorParallelDegree$ attention heads, computes attention locally for those heads, and feeds the resulting head outputs into the row-parallel attention output projection. In the feed-forward network, the first linear layer is column-parallel, the element-wise nonlinearity applies independently to each coordinate of the local slice, and the second linear layer is row-parallel. The forward pass therefore places one AllReduce after attention and one after the feed-forward network, for two forward AllReduce operations per transformer layer. In the backward pass, the dataflow reverses so that gradients enter each row-parallel output layer without communication, while at the input of each column-parallel layer every GPU computes a partial input gradient from its local weight slice and runs an AllReduce to sum those contributions before the preceding residual connection, adding two symmetric backward AllReduces per layer.</p>\n<p>Across those four AllReduces, communication volume per transformer layer depends on sequence length $\\SequenceLength$, hidden dimension $\\HiddenDimension$, tensor-parallel degree $\\TensorParallelDegree$, and batch size $\\BatchSize$. The batch size is the number of sequences in flight through the layer (the local batch, or the microbatch $\\MicrobatchSize$ when the layer also sits in a pipeline). The activation payload is: $$\\ActivationPayloadSize= \\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension\\times \\ElementStorageSize$$</p>\n<p>A ring AllReduce transfers approximately $2(\\TensorParallelDegree-1)/\\TensorParallelDegree \\times \\ActivationPayloadSize$ bytes per GPU, which is close to $2\\ActivationPayloadSize$ for large $\\TensorParallelDegree$. For the GPT-3 shape ($\\SequenceLength=$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.seq_len_str]</code>, $\\HiddenDimension=$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.hidden_dim_str]</code>, <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.layers_str]</code> layers) with $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.micro_batch_str]</code> and FP16, the activation payload is <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.micro_batch_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.seq_len_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.hidden_dim_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.dtype_bytes_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.payload_mb_str]</code> MB.</p>\n<p>At $\\TensorParallelDegree=$ <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.tp_str]</code> the ring factor is <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.ring_factor_str]</code>, giving roughly <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.per_allreduce_mb_str]</code> per AllReduce. With two AllReduces in each of the <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.layers_str]</code> layers, this totals approximately <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.forward_gb_str]</code> per forward microbatch. The symmetric backward exchanges bring the total to roughly <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.per_step_gb_str]</code> per forward/backward microbatch, before any recomputation. Set against the computation it serves, that volume is modest on the right link and prohibitive on the wrong one. At the per-layer compute rate of the earlier feasibility check, one such microbatch takes about <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.compute_mb_s_str]</code> of forward and backward computation per GPU. Its tensor-parallel traffic takes about <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.nvlink_mb_s_str]</code> on NVLink but about <code>[TensorParallelLayerTraffic.ib_mb_s_str]</code> on one InfiniBand port, longer than the computation. The gap matters because this traffic sits on the critical path of every layer. The next operation consumes each reduced result and cannot start on that data until the reduction completes, whereas a data-parallel gradient reduction can overlap the backward computation that remains. The number of microbatches per update and the available overlap then determine the required fabric service rate. Computing MFU requires the complete compute and exposed-communication time, not the bandwidth ratio alone.</p>\n<p>Tensor parallelism eventually reaches a workload-dependent limit because smaller operation shards leave less computation to cover communication. Per-GPU ring AllReduce bytes approach 2$\\times$ the activation tensor size, while local matrix shapes and collective startup costs change with group size. The useful degree therefore depends on layer shape, microbatch size, and the fast communication domain. Llama 3 405B's TP=8 arrangement within NVLink-connected H100 nodes [@dubey2024llama] and node-local groups in other large training systems [@jiang2024megascale] illustrate a placement choice, not a universal eight-device limit. Tensor parallelism also costs more to adopt than data parallelism. It replaces standard linear layers with column-parallel and row-parallel variants inside the model definition, whereas data parallelism wraps the model externally without modifying its internals.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-22\" class=\"cs-h3\">Sequence and context parallelism</h3>\n<p>While tensor parallelism partitions weight matrices within linear layers, the memory footprint of large-scale transformer training is increasingly dominated by activations. As models scale their context length from thousands to hundreds of thousands or millions of tokens ($\\SequenceLength\\ge 32\\text{k}\\dots 1\\text{M}+$), the activation footprint scales proportionally: $$\\ActivationMemory = \\mathcal{O}(\\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension\\times \\LayerCount)$$ for linear, normalization, and residual layers, and $\\mathcal{O}(\\BatchSize\\times \\AttentionHeadCount \\times \\SequenceLength^2 \\times \\LayerCount)$ for un-tiled attention (or $\\mathcal{O}(\\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension\\times \\LayerCount)$ with tiled FlashAttention, which computes attention block by block without storing the full score matrix). At long contexts, activation memory dwarfs parameter storage, exposing the memory capacity gap (principle \\ref{pri-memory-capacity-gap}). Two complementary methods address this constraint by sharding along the sequence axis: sequence parallelism (SP) within the tensor-parallel domain, and context parallelism (CP) across dedicated long-sequence communication groups.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Sequence and context parallelism</strong></em> partition the token sequence dimension to reduce per-device activation storage. Sequence parallelism (SP) retains sequence shards during operations such as LayerNorm and Dropout within a tensor-parallel group. Context parallelism (CP) partitions the sequence across a dedicated group and exchanges the information needed for attention, through mechanisms developed below that either pass key/value blocks around a ring or transpose between sequence and head partitions.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Long contexts can exhaust activation memory even when sharded model state fits. The relevant limit depends on batch size, layer shapes, attention kernels, and checkpointing. Sequence parallelism divides the targeted activation storage by $\\TensorParallelDegree$ while preserving the ideal collective payload volume in the decomposition below. Context parallelism distributes sequence-dependent storage; linear context growth ($\\ContextParallelDegree\\times \\SingleDeviceContextLength$, with $\\ContextParallelDegree$ denoting the context-parallel degree) is an ideal capacity estimate that still requires communication buffers and computation to fit the budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Tensor parallelism partitions weight matrices along hidden dimensions $\\HiddenDimension$, whereas context parallelism partitions the sequence axis $\\SequenceLength$. Ring Attention exchanges key/value chunks between devices, while Ulysses uses an AllToAll to transpose between sequence and head dimensions. Both require placement that can service their exchanges within the attention schedule.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Assuming context parallelism eliminates the quadratic attention compute complexity. CP distributes the attention computation across devices, reducing <em>per-device</em> memory and FLOPs by $1/\\ContextParallelDegree$, but the <em>aggregate</em> cluster FLOPs for dense self-attention still scale quadratically as $\\mathcal{O}(\\SequenceLength^2)$.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h4\">Megatron sequence parallelism</h4>\n<p>In the Megatron-style arrangement just derived, the column-then-row pairing in @fig-tensor-parallel-split splits the QKV projection and first MLP layer column-wise, and the attention output projection and second MLP layer row-wise. The surrounding LayerNorm, Dropout, and residual operations retain replicated activations across all $\\TensorParallelDegree$ devices. LayerNorm operates across a token's hidden features, while Dropout and residual addition operate element-wise; none requires another token's features. This token independence permits partitioning the otherwise replicated $\\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength\\times \\HiddenDimension$ activation tensor along its sequence dimension.</p>\n<p>Megatron Sequence Parallelism [@korthikanti2023activation] eliminates this redundancy by sharding the sequence dimension across the $\\TensorParallelDegree$ ranks for all non-tensor-parallel operations. Instead of replicating activations, each GPU holds only a $\\BatchSize\\times (\\SequenceLength/\\TensorParallelDegree) \\times \\HiddenDimension$ slice during LayerNorm and Dropout.</p>\n<p>This sharding incurs zero additional communication volume. In standard tensor parallelism, the row-parallel matrix multiplication is followed by an AllReduce to sum partial products across ranks. An AllReduce decomposes into a ReduceScatter followed by an AllGather, the identity established among the collective primitives in @sec-collective-communication-six-core-primitives-8a66: $$\\text{AllReduce}(\\RankContribution) \\equiv \\text{AllGather}(\\text{ReduceScatter}(\\RankContribution))$$ Megatron-SP decouples this collective pair across the transformer block. Following the row-parallel linear layer, a ReduceScatter reduces partial sums while scattering the resulting activations along the sequence dimension, leaving each GPU with an $\\SequenceLength/\\TensorParallelDegree$ token slice. LayerNorm, residual additions, and Dropout then execute on these local slices with zero cross-device communication. Immediately before the next column-parallel linear layer, an AllGather across the sequence dimension reassembles the complete $\\SequenceLength$ tokens required for the weight-partitioned matrix multiplication.</p>\n<p>In this ideal payload accounting, standard TP executes two AllReduces per transformer block ($2 \\times 2(\\TensorParallelDegree-1)/\\TensorParallelDegree \\times \\ActivationPayloadSize$ bytes), while Megatron-SP executes two ReduceScatter and AllGather pairs ($2 \\times [(\\TensorParallelDegree-1)/\\TensorParallelDegree + (\\TensorParallelDegree-1)/\\TensorParallelDegree] \\times \\ActivationPayloadSize= 2 \\times 2(\\TensorParallelDegree-1)/\\TensorParallelDegree \\times \\ActivationPayloadSize$ bytes). The volumes match while the targeted activation storage falls by a factor of $\\TensorParallelDegree$. Equal bytes do not imply equal elapsed time, because separating the collectives changes readiness, startup costs, and overlap opportunities.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h4\">Context parallelism with ring attention versus Ulysses</h4>\n<p>Megatron-SP shards the $\\SequenceLength$ dimension across the $\\TensorParallelDegree$ devices only outside the attention and MLP blocks; inside self-attention, each tensor-parallel rank still processes the full sequence length $\\SequenceLength$ for its local heads, so long-context runs can still exhaust HBM even after sequence parallelism and activation checkpointing. Context parallelism of degree $\\ContextParallelDegree$ breaks that ceiling by partitioning the sequence itself across $\\ContextParallelDegree$ accelerators throughout the entire block, including attention. Because every query token must still attend to keys and values from other sequence partitions, context parallelism requires cross-partition exchanges, supplied either by ring-based point-to-point transfers, by AllToAll head-sequence transpositions, or by a hierarchical combination of both.</p>\n<p>Ring Attention [@liu2023ringAttention] organizes devices in a logical ring where each GPU holds a query chunk $\\AttentionQueryMatrix_i$ of length $\\SequenceLength/\\ContextParallelDegree$ and an initial key/value chunk $(\\AttentionKeyMatrix_i, \\AttentionValuesMatrix_i)$. Computation proceeds in $\\ContextParallelDegree$ rounds along the ring. In each round, GPU $\\ContextParallelRankIndex$ computes block attention between its stationary $\\AttentionQueryMatrix_i$ and resident $(\\AttentionKeyMatrix_j, \\AttentionValuesMatrix_j)$ tiles using an online softmax accumulator. At the same time, it asynchronously transmits $(\\AttentionKeyMatrix_j, \\AttentionValuesMatrix_j)$ to ring neighbor $(\\ContextParallelRankIndex+1) \\bmod \\ContextParallelDegree$ and receives $(\\AttentionKeyMatrix_{j-1}, \\AttentionValuesMatrix_{j-1})$ from $(\\ContextParallelRankIndex-1) \\bmod \\ContextParallelDegree$. After $\\ContextParallelDegree$ rounds every query chunk has attended to every key/value tile, yet no device ever stores the full key/value sequence (@fig-ring-attention-rounds). Normalizing a query's attention scores requires all of them, but they arrive one tile at a time. The online softmax therefore maintains a running maximum and running sum of attention scores as tiles arrive and rescales earlier partial sums whenever the maximum changes. Each round's partial result merges into that running state, which likewise spares each device the full attention matrix.</p>\n<p>For one sequence with standard multi-head attention, let $\\ContextChunkTokenCount=\\SequenceLength/\\ContextParallelDegree$ be the tokens per chunk, $\\HiddenDimension$ the total head width, $\\ElementStorageSize$ the bytes per stored element, $\\PeakRate\\EfficiencyHw$ the sustained attention FLOP/s, and $\\SingleLinkBandwidth$ the one-way transfer rate. Computing both $\\AttentionQueryMatrix\\AttentionKeyMatrix^T$ and the weighted-value product costs approximately $4\\ContextChunkTokenCount^2 \\HiddenDimension$ FLOPs, while sending $\\AttentionKeyMatrix$ and $\\AttentionValuesMatrix$ costs $2 \\ContextChunkTokenCount \\HiddenDimension\\, \\ElementStorageSize$ bytes. Ignoring startup, softmax, and overlap interference gives the necessary tile-size estimate: $$\\frac{4\\ContextChunkTokenCount^2 \\HiddenDimension}{\\PeakRate\\EfficiencyHw} \\ge \\frac{2 \\ContextChunkTokenCount \\HiddenDimension\\, \\ElementStorageSize}{\\SingleLinkBandwidth} \\iff \\ContextChunkTokenCount\\ge \\frac{\\ElementStorageSize \\PeakRate\\EfficiencyHw}{2\\SingleLinkBandwidth}$$ This inequality compares computation time with transfer time; HBM bandwidth cannot substitute for $\\PeakRate\\EfficiencyHw$. It is an idealized overlap condition, not a guarantee.</p>\n<p>For an H100 sustaining half its dense FP16 peak with 2-byte elements, the condition requires chunks of at least about <code>[RingAttentionThreshold.n_nvlink_str]</code> tokens per device when KV tiles travel over NVLink, and about <code>[RingAttentionThreshold.n_ib_str]</code> tokens when they cross one NDR InfiniBand port. A million-token context split across a few dozen devices clears both bounds, whereas splitting a 32K context across many nodes can leave each chunk too short to hide its transfers. Grouped-query attention, in which several query heads share one key-value head, changes the transferred KV width, and causal masking changes the tile work. Both require recomputing the two costs.</p>\n<p>In causal autoregressive attention, a query token can attend only to earlier or current positions, so whenever a rank holding query chunk $\\ContextParallelRankIndex$ receives a key and value chunk from a later sequence position $\\KvTileIndex&gt; \\ContextParallelRankIndex$, the entire upper-triangular tile is masked out. Under naive contiguous partitioning, rank $\\ContextParallelRankIndex = 0$ holds the earliest tokens and performs useful work only on its own chunk ($\\KvTileIndex = 0$), sitting idle during the remaining $\\ContextParallelDegree - 1$ ring rounds while rank $\\ContextParallelDegree - 1$ (holding the final tokens) computes on every round. Because the ring steps synchronously at the pace of the busiest rank, this load imbalance leaves half the cluster's compute cycles idle. Production implementations eliminate this skew with zigzag or striped token partitioning, which splits the sequence into $2\\ContextParallelDegree$ sub-chunks and gives each device one early sub-chunk and one complementary late sub-chunk (for example, one chunk from the start of the sequence and one from the end). Every device then holds the same share of unmasked causal tiles in every round of the ring.</p>\n<p>DeepSpeed Ulysses} [@jacobs2023deepspeed] exploits the independence of attention heads rather than circulating tiles along a ring. Each head attends over the whole sequence without reading any other head's scores, so the attention work can be split by heads instead of by tokens. Outside the attention core, activations are sharded along the sequence dimension ($\\SequenceLength/\\ContextParallelDegree$ tokens, all $\\AttentionHeadCount$ attention heads). Before computing self-attention, an AllToAll collective transposes the tensor from sequence-partitioned <code>[S/c, h, d_head]</code> to head-partitioned <code>[S, h/c, d_head]</code>. Each GPU now holds the full sequence length $\\SequenceLength$, but only a subset $\\AttentionHeadCount/\\ContextParallelDegree$ of attention heads. Each device computes standard local FlashAttention across the full sequence for its heads; each rank owns complete heads, while the kernel handles causal masking locally. A second AllToAll collective transposes the attention output back to <code>[S/c, h, d_head]</code>. Ulysses is conceptually clean and reuses single-GPU attention kernels, but requires that the head count be divisible by the context degree and therefore no smaller than it ($\\AttentionHeadCount \\ge \\ContextParallelDegree$). Under grouped-query attention, where the key-value head count $\\KvHeadCount$ is smaller, a direct head-sharding implementation requires compatible divisibility and $\\ContextParallelDegree\\le \\KvHeadCount$. Replicating KV heads changes that limit and its communication budget.</p>\n<p>The ownership map in @fig-ulysses-ownership-transpose makes this transpose explicit. Unlike the stationary query chunks in @fig-ring-attention-rounds, a Ulysses rank receives every token for its assigned heads.</p>\n<p>The exchanged tiles change where attention executes rather than removing its interactions. Head count and KV-head layout constrain this partition even when the activation bytes fit in memory.</p>\n<p>A hierarchical configuration can combine head-sequence transposes within a fast domain and ring exchanges between domains. For an eight-device intra-node domain, a Ulysses group satisfies ($\\IntraNodeContextParallelDegree\\le 8$), while a second degree ($\\InterNodeContextParallelDegree$) determines the ring's inter-node partition. In this example, Ulysses partitions heads locally and Ring Attention circulates KV blocks across nodes. The arrangement is feasible only if head divisibility, peak memory, link service time, and the overlap condition all hold; hierarchy alone does not prevent fabric saturation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h3\">Expert parallelism (mixture of experts)</h3>\n<p>Tensor, pipeline, sequence, and context parallelism all partition dense layers that every token traverses, so scaling a dense model's parameter count increases its per-token compute cost in lockstep. MoE} architectures [@shazeer2017outrageously] break that lockstep through conditional computation, and expert parallelism distributes the resulting capacity across devices. In an MoE layer, the feed-forward block contains $\\ExpertCount$ independent feed-forward networks (\"experts\"), and a gating network selects only a small subset, often top-1 or top-2, for each token. Adding more experts while holding each expert's shape and the selected count fixed expands stored parameters without increasing expert FLOPs per token, trading dense matrix work for routing and all-to-all communication.</p>\n<p>In a distributed setting, experts are partitioned across workers, with each rank hosting a disjoint subset of expert networks. One routing cycle across four ranks shows the sequence (@fig-moe-all-to-all-routing). After top-$\\RoutedExpertCount$ gating selects destination experts, an AllToAll dispatch shuffles tokens across the fabric to the hosting ranks. Expert FFNs then compute locally, and a second AllToAll return routes the processed activations back to the originating ranks to form the gate-weighted sum. The backward pass repeats both exchanges in reverse order, sending output gradients to the ranks hosting the experts and returning input gradients to the ranks that own the tokens. The primary advantage is decoupling model size from per-token compute, which lets parameter count grow within feasible hardware budgets. The primary constraint is the AllToAll communication, which is bandwidth-intensive and sensitive to load imbalance.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Expert parallelism</strong></em> is the distribution strategy for mixture-of-experts models that places different experts on different devices and routes each token's activations to the devices hosting its selected experts through AllToAll collectives, scaling total parameter count across the cluster without scaling the compute each token consumes.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: It decouples model capacity from compute budget. For illustration, a trillion-parameter MoE model may activate on the order of 10B parameters per token, so per-token FLOPs remain near those of a 10B dense model while capacity grows roughly 100$\\times$. The price is communication structure: every MoE layer requires two AllToAll shuffles per pass (dispatch and combine) whose volume scales with tokens moved times hidden dimension, stressing the cluster's bisection bandwidth rather than any single link.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike tensor parallelism, which splits dense operations so that every device computes a fraction of every token, expert parallelism is conditional computation. Each device computes only the tokens routed to its experts, which makes both the communication pattern and the load distribution data-dependent rather than fixed by the architecture.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that experts share work evenly. A hot expert receiving, for example, 3--5$\\times$ its uniform share can overflow its device's activation buffer while sibling devices idle. Capacity limits and routing penalties are possible responses; a design that retains every assignment must instead accommodate the resulting memory and scheduling demand.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>↩ The terms that set an AllToAll's completion time, from traffic across the fabric bisection to hotspots created by skewed expert loads, are established in @sec-communication-alltoall.</p>\n<p>AllToAll dispatch routes token activations to their assigned experts. Consider $\\ExpertCount=$ <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.experts_str]</code> experts across <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.experts_str]</code> GPUs, processing a routing-group batch of $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.batch_str]</code> sequences of length $\\SequenceLength=$ <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.seq_len_str]</code> and hidden dimension $\\HiddenDimension=$ <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.hidden_dim_str]</code>. Under top-1 routing, $\\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength$ tokens each contribute one activation vector. At FP16, the logical dispatch payload is $\\BatchSize\\cdot \\SequenceLength\\cdot \\HiddenDimension\\cdot 2$ bytes, approximately <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.dispatch_mb_str]</code>; returning expert outputs doubles the forward payload to about <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.per_block_mb_str]</code> per block. Local destinations reduce bytes crossing the network, multiple selected experts increase routes, and backward adds gradient transfers. The resulting service time depends on placement and load balance, including in Switch Transformer [@fedus2022switch] (up to <code>[MoEAllToAllTraffic.switch_max_experts_str]</code> experts) and GShard [@lepikhin2021gshard].</p>\n<p>That load balance rarely stays uniform across experts, so uniform routing is a useful capacity baseline rather than a guarantee. An expert receiving several times its uniform share, the hot expert of the expert-parallelism definition, needs correspondingly more token processing and activation storage. The actual skew depends on the learned router and batch. A hot expert can exhaust its buffer or delay the step while other experts remain underused, coupling memory feasibility to load balance.</p>\n<p>One response bounds expert workload with a capacity factor $\\ExpertCapacityFactor$. For top-1 routing, a capacity of roughly $\\ExpertCapacityFactor\\cdot (\\BatchSize\\times \\SequenceLength)/\\ExpertCount$ token assignments per expert provides headroom above uniform load. A dropping implementation sends excess tokens past that expert through the residual connection; implementations that retain every assignment instead need memory and scheduling to accommodate the imbalance. An auxiliary load balancing loss can encourage less concentrated routing. Models using more than one expert per token, such as Mixtral 8x7B with top-2 routing [@jiang2024mixtral], require a capacity budget that counts all selected assignments rather than reusing the top-1 denominator unchanged.</p>\n<p>Sparse routing gives MoE layers the same coordination problem that the embedding tables of Archetype B (DLRM at Scale) pose. In both, which device a token's data must reach depends on the data itself, so load balance cannot be fixed in advance. MoE language models nonetheless remain Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) workloads, because their dense layers still carry the tensor, pipeline, and data-parallel traffic developed earlier in this chapter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-24\" class=\"cs-h3\">Sparse embedding sharding</h3>\n<p>Expert parallelism places parameters that only some tokens touch. Recommendation models push that sparsity much further. Their parameters are dominated by embedding tables (often <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.dlrm_mem_tb_str]</code>+) that no single GPU can hold, and their updates are sparse, because a batch of users interacts with only a tiny fraction of the items. Sharding those tables is model parallelism over stored state rather than over computation. The parameter server (PS) architecture, formalized by @li2014, remains a common way to do it. A parameter server architecture separates workers, which compute gradients, from servers, which store parameters and apply updates.</p>\n<p>For dense models such as ResNet or BERT, that separation concentrates traffic, as @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-gradient-synchronization-614b noted. With $\\DeviceCount$ workers, a server's inbound links must absorb $\\DeviceCount$ gradient streams at once. This incast problem is why dense training moved to ring AllReduce, in which every worker sends and receives at its own link rate and the aggregate load spreads across all nodes.</p>\n<p>Sparse embedding tables reverse that balance. In a sparse parameter-server design, the tables are sharded across servers, and workers request only rows indexed by their local minibatches. A sparse pull returns those embedding vectors; a sparse push sends updates for the touched rows. This pattern avoids exchanging the complete table, but hot rows, lookup rates, and network contention can still limit throughput. Embedding-sharding frameworks such as TorchRec also support accelerator-based layouts, so sparse partitioning should not be equated with a CPU-only server architecture. Recommendation training at this scale is the volume's Archetype B (DLRM at Scale) regime, and it differs from the dense running example in which resource runs out first.</p>\n<p>Whether the shards live on servers or in accelerator memory, each table must be split along some axis. Row-wise sharding partitions embedding tables by row, placing the whole embedding vector of the entity with identifier id on the shard that @eq-distributed-training-embedding-row-shard selects:</p>\n<p>$$\\ShardIndex = \\text{hash}(\\text{id}) \\bmod \\EmbeddingShardCount$$ {#eq-distributed-training-embedding-row-shard}</p>\n<p>Each shard $\\ShardIndex$ contains approximately $\\EntityCount/\\EmbeddingShardCount$ embeddings, where $\\EntityCount$ is the total number of entities and $\\EmbeddingShardCount$ is the shard count. Column-wise sharding instead partitions each embedding vector $\\EntityEmbedding$ across devices, as @eq-distributed-training-embedding-column-shard writes it:</p>\n<p>$$\\EntityEmbedding = [\\EntityEmbedding^{(0)}, \\EntityEmbedding^{(1)}, \\ldots, \\EntityEmbedding^{(\\EmbeddingShardCount-1)}]$$ {#eq-distributed-training-embedding-column-shard}</p>\n<p>Each device stores a slice of every embedding. Hybrid sharding combines both approaches, and the choice among the three depends on lookup patterns and communication overhead (@tbl-embedding-sharding).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Sharding strategy</strong></th><th><strong>Lookup pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Communication</strong></th><th><strong>Best for</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Row-wise</strong></td><td>Single device per lookup</td><td>AllToAll gather</td><td>Uniform access patterns</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Column-wise</strong></td><td>All devices per lookup</td><td>AllGather</td><td>Hot embeddings</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Hybrid</strong></td><td>Varies by embedding</td><td>Mixed</td><td>Production RecSys</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Embedding Sharding Strategies</strong>: Comparison of row-wise, column-wise, and hybrid embedding placement strategies, balancing lookup locality against network communication collectives and memory load balancing. {#tbl-embedding-sharding}</p>\n<p>The trade-off is locality against load balance. Row-wise sharding keeps each embedding vector whole on one device, while column-wise sharding spreads its dimensions across devices. Both require returning the pieces each consuming computation needs, but placement alone does not prescribe a collective; the runtime can use remote procedure calls to the shards or collectives according to where inputs and outputs reside. A row-wise layout commonly realizes its lookups as an AllToAll of whole vectors, and a column-wise layout as an AllGather of slices of each vector (@fig-embedding-sharding). Hybrid placement can replicate or column-partition hot embeddings while row-sharding the cold tail, trading additional storage and communication against concentrated lookup load.</p>\n<p>On accelerators, a row-wise layout turns each training step's lookups into an AllToAll exchange. Every worker sends the indices its minibatch touches to the shards that own them and receives the embedding vectors back, and the backward pass returns gradients for the touched rows along the same routes. The exchange obeys the AllToAll bound of @sec-communication-alltoall, and like MoE dispatch its volume and balance depend on which rows the data touches, so hot rows overload their owners unless the layout replicates or splits them.</p>\n<p>Each workload scales through a distinct mechanism and is limited by a different resource. Dense LLMs scale through data, tensor, and pipeline parallelism, constrained by compute throughput and by the bandwidth that dense gradient and activation collectives need over fabrics such as NVLink and InfiniBand. Sparse recommendation models such as DLRM scale by sharding embedding tables of <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.dlrm_mem_tb_str]</code> or more across parameter servers or accelerator memory, where aggregate memory capacity, lookup rate, and the latency of many small random accesses dominate. The distinction shapes cluster design, favoring accelerator pods built around high-bandwidth dense fabrics for LLMs and memory-capacity-rich layouts for recommendation systems. This architectural split holds whether the embedding shards live in CPU-hosted servers, in accelerator HBM, or in a tiered hybrid that keeps hot rows on accelerators and cold rows in host memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-25\" class=\"cs-h2\">Hybrid Parallelism</h2>\n<p>The <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code>-parameter running example has now exhausted each parallelism family on its own. Data parallelism with ZeRO-3 makes the state fit, but the per-step parameter regather priced in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-model-parallelism-7437 would take about <code>[Zero3GatherCost.ratio_mult_str]</code> as long as the computation it serves, while tensor parallelism confined to one 8-GPU node still leaves several hundred gigabytes of training state per device. Because replication and state sharding (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-data-parallelism-6132) distribute examples and storage whereas tensor and pipeline partitioning (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-model-parallelism-7437) distribute layer computation, <strong>hybrid parallelism</strong> composes both families orthogonally. Model parallelism splits the architecture so each replica fits available HBM and stays on fast links, while data parallelism scales throughput across multiple model replicas to process <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.training_tokens_str]</code> tokens within calendar limits. This orthogonal composition defines Archetype A training at frontier scale.</p>\n<p>Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) illustrates the motivation for hybrid parallelism. Parameter count $(\\Parameters)$ must first be converted to a complete memory budget before comparison with device capacity $(\\DeviceMemoryCapacity)$, while dataset size $(\\DatasetSize)$ and the deadline determine the throughput target. Tensor parallelism partitions layer computations within a suitable communication domain; pipeline parallelism assigns consecutive layers to stages; and data parallelism distributes examples from $\\DatasetSize$ across replicas. A design combines the dimensions that address its constraints; efficient execution does not require all three in every case.</p>\n<p>To see why the combination matters, consider four parallelism configurations for training the <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> model on a <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_gpus_str]</code>-GPU cluster organized as <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_nodes_str]</code> of <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.gpus_per_node_str]</code> each. The progression from pure data parallelism through tensor and pipeline parallelism shows which axis makes the job fit and what each axis adds to the step time.</p>\n<p>All four configurations run the same modeled training step, the one this book uses whenever it quotes a step time for this model on this cluster, the reference job. One step is one optimizer update over a global batch of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.global_batch_str]</code> sequences of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.seq_len_str]</code> tokens in 16-bit mixed precision, and each replica feeds its share of the batch through the model one sequence per microbatch. Each GPU computes at <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.local_efficiency_pct_str]</code> of its peak before communication, and up to <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.overlap_pct_str]</code> of the data-parallel gradient reduction hides behind backward computation. Because the model, batch, and cluster are fixed, the average computation per GPU is the same <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.t_local_s_str]</code> per step in every configuration. In B, C, and D every replica receives the same whole number of sequences, so every GPU carries exactly that load. A is the exception, because its <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.global_batch_str]</code> sequences do not divide evenly over <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.n_gpus_str]</code> replicas (<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.seq_per_replica_a_str]</code> each). Some GPUs would process two sequences while others process one, so the walk quotes no step time or efficiency for A. The configurations differ only in the training state each GPU must hold and in the time each adds to that computation for tensor-parallel exchanges, exposed data-parallel transfer, and the pipeline bubble.</p>\n<p>In Configuration A, pure data parallelism (DP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_a_value_str]</code>) serves as a counterfactual rather than a feasible placement for this model. Every GPU would hold the full <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.state_gb_str]</code> of training state against <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.hbm_gb_str]</code> of HBM. The FP16 weights alone occupy <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.gradient_gb_str]</code>, so an A100 or H100 cannot replicate them; sharding only optimizer states does not change that fact. Communication is not what rules the layout out. If replication were possible, all <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.n_gpus_str]</code> ranks would AllReduce the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.gradient_gb_str]</code> gradient once per step. A hierarchical reduction first reduces inside each node over NVLink, so each GPU's InfiniBand port carries 1/<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.gpus_per_node_value_str]</code> of the gradient, and the exchange takes about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_a_comm_s_str]</code>, of which about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_a_exposed_s_str]</code> stays exposed beside the average <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.t_local_s_str]</code> of computation. The scaling-efficiency notebook in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-distributed-training-efficiency-metrics-9488 reached only <code>[CommScalingMath.scaling_efficiency_str]</code> for this placement because it priced the exchange as a flat ring on each GPU's port behind a shorter FP8 compute window, which hides far less of the transfer.</p>\n<p>Configuration B introduces intra-node tensor parallelism alongside data parallelism (TP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code>, DP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_b_value_str]</code>). Within each of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_b_value_str]</code> model replicas, <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code> GPUs use tensor parallelism over NVLink. The corresponding tensor shard from every replica forms one <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_b_value_str]</code>-rank data-parallel group, so the layout contains <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code> such groups. Each rank AllReduces its 1/<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code> gradient shard, <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_b_shard_gb_str]</code> rather than <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.gradient_gb_str]</code>, in about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_b_comm_s_str]</code>. Tensor parallelism is not free, however. Its forward and backward activation AllReduces add about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_comm_s_str]</code> per step on NVLink, so the modeled step grows to <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.step_b_s_str]</code>, a scaling efficiency of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_b_efficiency_pct_str]</code>. The layout still does not fit. At <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.bytes_per_param_str]</code> bytes per parameter each GPU would hold about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_b_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> of training state, so tensor parallelism confined to one node is not enough by itself, and the layout would also need gradient and optimizer sharding across its data-parallel group before it fits.</p>\n<p>Configuration C adds pipeline stages across nodes (TP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code>, PP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_c_value_str]</code>, DP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_c_value_str]</code>) so that each of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_c_value_str]</code> model replicas uses <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code>-way tensor parallelism within a stage and <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_c_value_str]</code> pipeline stages across nodes. For each of the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_c_value_str]</code> tensor-and-pipeline shard positions, the corresponding ranks from all replicas form one <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_c_value_str]</code>-rank data-parallel group. Each rank therefore AllReduces a <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_c_shard_math]</code> GB gradient shard, in about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_c_comm_s_str]</code>. Pipeline communication forwards activations between stages and has lower volume than a full-model AllReduce. The trade-off is the pipeline bubble. At the beginning and end of each training step, some pipeline stages are idle while waiting for activations from earlier stages or gradients from later stages. The bubble fraction is approximately $(\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)/(\\MicrobatchCount+\\PipelineParallelDegree-1)$, so with <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_c_value_str]</code> pipeline stages and the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.m_c_value_str]</code> microbatches each replica receives per step, the bubble idles <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.bubble_c_math]</code> of the pipelined step, about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.bubble_c_s_str]</code>. The pipeline cuts the data-parallel traffic by another factor of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_c_value_str]</code>, but the bubble costs more time than the smaller transfer saves, so the modeled step lengthens to <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.step_c_s_str]</code> and efficiency falls to <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_c_efficiency_pct_str]</code>.</p>\n<p>Configuration C does not fit memory by itself either. Its training state is about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_c_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU, above the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.hbm_gb_str]</code> of an H100. Adding ZeRO-1 across its <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_c_value_str]</code>-rank data-parallel group closes that gap. Each GPU then keeps its tensor-and-pipeline slice of weights and gradients but only a 1/<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_c_value_str]</code> share of that slice's optimizer state, about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_c_zero1_gb_str]</code> in all. ZeRO-1 matches plain DDP's ideal data-parallel byte volume at matching communication widths (@tbl-zero-stages), so the model prices the same step for it, but equal bytes do not preserve the overlap schedule. Gradient reduction to owners must precede their optimizer updates, and the updated-parameter AllGather follows those updates. Configuration C with ZeRO-1 passes this simplified persistent-state memory budget, and its predicted step holds only if that reduction, update, and parameter-gather sequence achieves the modeled exposed communication time.</p>\n<p>Configuration D deepens the pipeline instead of sharding the optimizer (TP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code>, PP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_d_value_str]</code>, DP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_d_value_str]</code>). It is the layout of the reference job, in which tensor parallelism spans one node and the pipeline is the shallowest power of two at which unsharded mixed-precision Adam state fits. Each GPU holds about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_d_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> of training state, which leaves about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.headroom_d_gb_str]</code> of the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.hbm_gb_str]</code> budget for activations, workspaces, and communication buffers. Each of the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_d_value_str]</code> replicas runs its <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.m_d_value_str]</code> sequences as <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.m_d_value_str]</code> one-sequence microbatches, so the bubble idles <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.bubble_d_math]</code> of the pipelined step, about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.bubble_d_s_str]</code>, and the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_d_value_str]</code>-rank data-parallel groups reduce their smaller shards in about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_d_comm_s_str]</code>. With tensor-parallel, data-parallel, and bubble time all charged, the modeled step takes <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.step_d_s_str]</code>, a scaling efficiency of <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_d_efficiency_pct_str]</code>. Against configuration C with ZeRO-1, the deeper pipeline costs about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.step_gap_cd_s_str]</code> per step, its larger bubble less its smaller transfer. Its optimizer still waits for the reduced gradients, but each GPU holds the optimizer state for its whole tensor-and-pipeline slice and updates that slice in place, so the post-update parameter AllGather that ZeRO-1 adds never enters the step.</p>\n<p>Within this step model, communication does not separate the layouts. B, C, and D finish within <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.spread_pts_str]</code> percentage points of one another, and each added axis lengthens the modeled step slightly because it brings its own exchange or idle time. Memory decides the design. Tensor parallelism inside the NVLink domain divides the training state by the node size at the price of frequent exchanges on the fastest link, pipeline stages divide it again at the price of a bubble, and data parallelism leaves only small gradient shards for InfiniBand. Peak memory, kernel shape, and scheduling still need verification before that prediction becomes a runnable design. The infrastructure hierarchy therefore dictates the parallelism hierarchy. Here, hierarchy-aware parallelism means matching exchange requirements to measured communication domains rather than choosing each degree independently.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Hierarchy-aware parallelism</strong></em> is the strategy of mapping different parallel execution modes to the physical bandwidth tiers of the cluster.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: It places high-frequency synchronization (for example, tensor parallelism) on fast links such as NVLink, while assigning lower-frequency exchanges (for example, data parallelism) to slower tiers when their transfers fit the schedule. This alignment can improve scaling efficiency $(\\ScalingEfficiency)$ by reducing exposed communication time $(\\CommunicationTime)$ and latency $(\\Latency)$; the gain depends on readiness, contention, and available overlap.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike uniform parallelism, which treats all node-to-node links as equal, hierarchy-aware strategies respect the bandwidth cliffs between die, node, and rack boundaries.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that any model can be sharded across any number of nodes. In reality, if the hierarchy mapping is wrong (for example, sharding a large tensor across a slow inter-rack link), the communication time will dwarf the compute time, making the scale-out useless.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dependence runs both ways, because a parallelism configuration also determines which traffic the fabric must carry. In the reference job's TP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code>, PP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_d_value_str]</code>, DP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_d_value_str]</code> layout, tensor reductions stay within each fast local domain, pipeline traffic crosses neighboring stages, and data-parallel groups synchronize corresponding shards across replicas. Rail-optimized and hierarchical fabrics can exploit that structure, but a workload mix that adds expert AllToAll traffic changes the global bandwidth requirement. Infrastructure planning must therefore evaluate the anticipated communication groups together rather than optimizing the fabric for a single layout.</p>\n<p>↩ Rail-optimized fabrics, which cable each GPU slot position to its own rail switch, are established in @sec-network-fabrics-rail-optimized.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-26\" class=\"cs-h3\">The 3D training loop</h3>\n<p>To trace how those three communication groups interact inside a single iteration, a <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code>-parameter model trained with 3D parallelism (@fig-3d-parallelism-cube) can be followed on a fleet configured with TP of <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.tp_str]</code>, PP of <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.pp_value_str]</code>, and DP of <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.dp_value_str]</code>. This configuration uses <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.total_gpus_str]</code> (<code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.tp_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.pp_value_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.dp_value_str]</code>) organized into a hierarchy of bandwidth domains. That allocation is <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.fleet_ratio_mult_str]</code> the <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_gpus_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster, so every level of the hierarchy is exercised even though parameter count alone would not require all three dimensions. @Sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-configuration-design justifies its pipeline depth.</p>\n<p>The training step begins at the data-parallel level. Each of the <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.dp_value_str]</code> model replicas receives a distinct slice of the global batch. Within each replica, the model is split across <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.pp_value_str]</code> pipeline stages (nodes), with microbatches flowing sequentially from the embedding layer on Node 0 to the loss calculation on Node 15. At the finest granularity, within each node, the <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.tp_str]</code> fuse into a single \"super-accelerator\" via TP. The transformer matrix multiplications are split across these devices; column- and row-parallel pairs retain local intermediate shards and exchange partial results at the reduction points already derived. For a 175B-scale hidden dimension, the activation payload is roughly <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.activation_payload_mb_str]</code>. The resulting tensor-parallel traffic is about <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.per_stage_forward_gb_str]</code> per pipeline stage per microbatch in the forward pass, or <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.per_stage_forward_backward_gb_str]</code> including the backward pass. Across the full <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.layers_str]</code>-layer replica, the forward tensor-parallel traffic is about <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.full_forward_gb_str]</code>. This traffic is the highest-intensity in the system, but its exposed latency can remain small relative to compute because the chips communicate over <code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_a100_gb_per_s_str]</code>--<code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_h100_gb_per_s_str]</code> of local bandwidth per direction.</p>\n<p>The backward pass exposes dependencies between parallelism dimensions. Nodes exchange activation gradients point-to-point, roughly <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.activation_payload_mb_str]</code> per microbatch per stage boundary in this example. A smaller payload still stalls a stage if it arrives after the stage needs it. Data-parallel synchronization creates another dependency. Although full-model gradients occupy about <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.gradient_gb_str]</code>, each data-parallel group exchanges only the corresponding tensor- and pipeline-parallel parameter shard. Gradient bucketing groups ready gradients as backward computation proceeds. Which exchange limits the step depends on its readiness, service time, and overlap; data parallelism is not necessarily the final bottleneck.</p>\n<p>↩ Bucket fusion, which groups gradients that become ready together into larger reductions so communication can begin during backpropagation, is established in @sec-communication-bucket-fusion.</p>\n<p>The architectural imperative is <em>bandwidth matching</em>, in which each exchange's startup and transfer time must fit its available compute or overlap interval. In this mapping, frequent TP exchanges use the NVLink domain (<code>[DistTrainNvlinkIntro.nvlink_a100_gb_per_s_str]</code> per direction or higher), while PP transfers cross nodes and DP reductions overlap backward computation. Accumulation can enlarge the compute interval between DP synchronizations, subject to the global-batch constraint. An infeasible mapping leaves part or all of the <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.total_gpus_str]</code> fleet waiting. Comparing service time with the schedule completes the feasibility check introduced in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-engineering-tradeoffs-selecting-parallelism-strategy-b344.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-27\" class=\"cs-h3\">Hybrid-parallelism worked example</h3>\n<p>Applied to physical infrastructure, bandwidth matching turns cluster design into a placement problem, and memory capacity per device (<code>[DistTrainNvlinkHybridRecap.hbm_h100_gb_str]</code>) bounds every placement. For the 3D training loop's layout on <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.total_gpus_str]</code>, built from 8-GPU NVLink nodes, this reasoning leads to a concrete placement. Fix tensor parallelism at $\\TensorParallelDegree=8$ so the bandwidth-heavy matrix-multiplication collectives stay within one 8-GPU node. Map pipeline parallelism across nodes in the same high-bandwidth rack or island, here $\\PipelineParallelDegree=16$, with $\\PipelineParallelDegree=8$ as the alternative when the per-device memory budget allows. Use data parallelism for the remaining scale-out dimension across pods. The result is not a generic rule but a consequence of matching each traffic pattern to the cheapest fabric tier that can carry it. With the placement fixed, the next question is whether the static model state fits inside each accelerator's HBM budget.</p>\n<p>The memory budget for a <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.params_b_str]</code> model must include every persistent training component. Under the mixed-precision Adam convention used here, each parameter has FP16 weights and gradients, an FP32 master weight, and two FP32 optimizer moments, totaling <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.bytes_per_param_str]</code> bytes before activations and workspaces. With tensor parallelism degree <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.tensor_parallel_str]</code> alone, these components occupy <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.full_adam_tp_static_gb_str]</code> per device, exceeding H100-class capacity. Adding <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.pipeline_parallel_str]</code> pipeline stages spreads that state across the product of the two degrees, yielding <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.full_adam_hybrid_static_gb_str]</code> per device under even partitioning. A budget that counts only weights and optimizer moments, about <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.hybrid_static_gb_str]</code> per device here, understates this total. Activation peaks, layer imbalance, and communication workspaces must fit in the remaining memory before this configuration is feasible. Activation checkpointing can bring those peaks within budget, but the plan then pays in useful throughput, because recomputation costs about one extra forward pass per step when full and a few percent when selective (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-compute-phase-forward-backward), work that HFU counts and MFU does not.</p>\n<p>Once the configuration fits in memory, its traffic still follows the same tiers, and each data-parallel group reduces only its tensor-and-pipeline shard of the <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.gradient_gb_str]</code> gradient buffer once per update. Gradient bucketing overlaps that reduction with backward computation, so its exposed cost depends on the computation remaining before the optimizer needs the reduced shard.</p>\n<p>The last cost is scheduling rather than bandwidth, because a pipelined model is only efficient when enough microbatches are in flight to keep all stages busy. This remaining cost is the pipeline bubble developed in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-pipeline-parallelism-8748. Applying its bubble fraction $\\frac{\\PipelineParallelDegree-1}{\\MicrobatchCount+ \\PipelineParallelDegree - 1}$ to this hybrid layout, $\\PipelineParallelDegree=$ <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.pipeline_parallel_str]</code> stages with $\\MicrobatchCount=$ <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.microbatches_str]</code> microbatches waste <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.bubble_math]</code> of theoretical compute capacity, nearly one-third. Configuration C's <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_c_value_str]</code> stages at the same microbatch count would waste roughly <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.bubble_c_same_m_str]</code>.</p>\n<p>The deeper pipeline is chosen at this scale for two reasons. Its <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.pipeline_parallel_str]</code> stages spread the full training state to about <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.full_adam_hybrid_static_gb_str]</code> per device without optimizer sharding. Filling <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.total_gpus_str]</code> with configuration C's <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.replica_gpus_c_str]</code>-GPU replicas would instead need <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.depth_ratio_mult_str]</code> as many data-parallel replicas, which multiplies the global batch $\\DataParallelDegree \\times \\MicrobatchCount\\times \\MicrobatchSize$ by the same factor at the same per-replica schedule. The shallow pipeline spends global batch; the deep one spends bubble. The 1F1B scheduling that reclaims activation memory, and the larger microbatch counts it makes practical, drive the bubble fraction down asymptotically. Every added microbatch also enlarges the global batch, so the bubble target must be reconciled with the critical-batch constraint from @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-critical-batch-size-parallelism-hurt-4961.</p>\n<p>Widening the fast communication domain can relax that pipeline-depth trade-off, as Blackwell, the accelerator generation after the H100, shows for 3D parallelism choices. NVLink 5 provides <code>[ThreeDParallelism.nvlink5_bw_tb_per_s_str]</code> of bidirectional bandwidth per GPU, twice the H100's capacity, which shortens the transfer term for a fixed payload but does not reduce message startup latency. Larger tensor-parallel ($\\TensorParallelDegree$) groups, such as $\\TensorParallelDegree=$ <code>[ThreeDParallelism.tp_blackwell_value_str]</code> or $\\TensorParallelDegree=$ <code>[ThreeDParallelism.tp_alt_value_str]</code> across nodes, remain subject to topology and local-kernel efficiency. The <code>[ThreeDParallelism.die_to_die_bw_tb_per_s_str]</code> internal link joins Blackwell's two dies behind one logical GPU, so those dies are not separate tensor-parallel ranks.</p>\n<p>For a larger <code>[ThreeDParallelism.params_trillion_str]</code>-parameter model on this hardware, one candidate uses $\\TensorParallelDegree=$ <code>[ThreeDParallelism.tp_blackwell_value_str]</code> across two <code>[ThreeDParallelism.gpus_per_node_value_str]</code>-GPU nodes joined by NVLink Switch and $\\PipelineParallelDegree=$ <code>[ThreeDParallelism.pp_blackwell_value_str]</code>. Relative to the H100 layout's $\\TensorParallelDegree=$ <code>[ThreeDParallelism.tp_h100_value_str]</code> and $\\PipelineParallelDegree=$ <code>[ThreeDParallelism.pp_h100_value_str]</code>, this preserves the model-partition product while halving stage count, and the bubble must be recomputed from the new stage and microbatch counts rather than assumed to halve. Improving local bandwidth shifts pressure outward. Once intra-node exchanges limit the step less, the inter-node fabric that carries pipeline and data-parallel traffic, together with any expert AllToAll service, becomes the term to check against the next tier. The same placement constraints define a search over the TP, PP, and DP factorizations of a fleet.</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario</strong>: An engineering team schedules a <code>[AutoParallelism.params_b_str]</code>-parameter model across <code>[AutoParallelism.gpu_count_str]</code> H100 GPUs (<code>[AutoParallelism.hbm_gb_str]</code> HBM each), <code>[AutoParallelism.scale_ratio_mult_str]</code> the <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.reliability_gpu_count_str]</code>-GPU reference cluster so that the pipeline and data-parallel degrees both have room to vary. The team navigates the multi-dimensional 3D-parallelism search space (TP $\\times$ PP $\\times$ DP).</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong>: Candidate configurations must fit within <code>[AutoParallelism.hbm_gb_str]</code> HBM and satisfy the modeled communication budget. The search evaluates TP $\\times$ PP $\\times$ DP factorizations and selects TP=<code>[AutoParallelism.opt_tp_str]</code>, PP=<code>[AutoParallelism.opt_pp_value_str]</code>, and DP=<code>[AutoParallelism.opt_dp_value_str]</code> as the best predicted MFU among configurations feasible under its assumptions. That result is a candidate for measurement, not proof of the best attainable runtime.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: This search favors TP=<code>[AutoParallelism.opt_tp_str]</code> within the reference topology's fast domain and PP=<code>[AutoParallelism.opt_pp_value_str]</code>, with <code>[AutoParallelism.opt_mfu_str]</code> projected MFU. The result balances memory, local computation, transfers, and pipeline idle time within the search's cost model. A different topology, microbatch schedule, or kernel efficiency can change the preferred factorization.</p>\n<p>Adjusting any one dimension shifts the pressure onto the other two, making the factorization TP $\\times$ PP $\\times$ DP a tightly coupled system rather than three independent knobs. A projected MFU such as this search's still needs comparison with measured execution, and published runs report MFU well below peak that can fall as the same model moves to more GPUs (@fig-mfu-progression in @sec-performance-engineering-fleet-efficiency-metric-dff4).</p>\n<p>Verify your understanding of how parallelism strategies combine:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Dimension roles</strong>: identify which dimension in a TP=8, PP=16, DP=128 configuration partitions operations within each layer inside one node.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Bubble vs. depth</strong>: determine how moving from PP=16 to PP=8 changes the pipeline bubble fraction.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>DP vs. PP</strong>: explain the trade-off, with the budget fixed at that layout's <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.total_gpus_str]</code>, between increasing DP (data parallelism) and increasing PP (pipeline parallelism).</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Latency sensitivity</strong>: identify which dimension, TP, PP, or DP, is most sensitive to inter-node latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The four configurations and the worked layout tell one story. Pure data parallelism would replicate <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.state_gb_str]</code> of training state on every GPU, which no fabric can repair. Keeping tensor parallelism inside the NVLink domain cuts each rank's state and gradient shard by the tensor-parallel degree at the price of tensor-parallel exchanges, and pipeline stages cut them again at the price of a bubble. Communication alone does not make a layout feasible, however. Each layout must also hold the full training state, which is why configuration B would need gradient and optimizer sharding across its data-parallel group, why configuration C needs ZeRO-1, and why the reference job's TP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.tp_value_str]</code>, PP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.pp_d_value_str]</code>, DP-<code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.dp_d_value_str]</code> layout deepens the pipeline until unsharded state fits, at a modeled <code>[HybridConfigFeasibility.config_d_efficiency_pct_str]</code> scaling efficiency. The worked layout at <code>[TensorParallel3DTraffic.fleet_ratio_mult_str]</code> that scale goes deeper still, until the persistent state falls to about <code>[HybridMemoryAccounting.full_adam_hybrid_static_gb_str]</code> per device. Hybrid parallelism is therefore the joint solution of a memory constraint, a bandwidth hierarchy, and a batch-size limit, and every axis added to satisfy one of them tightens another. Alignment training tests that joint solution hardest, because it asks one fleet to host several models with different memory and compute profiles at the same time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-28\" class=\"cs-h2\">Multi-Model Training for Alignment and RLHF</h2>\n<p>A cluster partitioned to train a single model cannot run post-training alignment in place, because reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)} coordinates up to four distinct models inside a single training step. In proximal policy optimization (PPO), an active policy generates responses token by token, a preference-trained reward model scores them, a frozen reference copy constrains policy drift, and a learned value model estimates expected returns to guide the gradient update. Those roles carry sharply different memory footprints and alternate between memory-bandwidth-bound autoregressive generation and compute-bound backward passes. Direct preference optimization (DPO) sidesteps online generation and drops the separate reward and value models, trading live exploration for a simpler two-model loop. Designing an alignment cluster therefore starts by tallying which models, states, and execution phases must coexist on the fleet.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-29\" class=\"cs-h3\">The multi-model coordination problem</h3>\n<p>Holding four models in accelerator memory at once multiplies the static state budget before a single prompt is even decoded, and only two of those models ever run a backward pass. In PPO} [@schulman2017proximal], as used in InstructGPT-style systems [@ouyang2022instructgpt], the four roles differ sharply in execution mode and memory burden (@tbl-rlhf-model-coordination-roles). In @fig-rlhf-ppo-step, the policy $\\Policy$ generates responses for scoring by the frozen reference and reward models and evaluation by the value model $\\CriticModel$. The penalized reward ($\\ResponseReward- \\KlPenaltyCoefficient\\,\\text{KL}$) subtracts a Kullback–Leibler divergence penalty, weighted by a coefficient $\\KlPenaltyCoefficient$ unrelated to link bandwidth $\\LinkBandwidth$, to discourage drift from the reference. Generalized advantage estimation (GAE) combines rewards and value estimates to judge how much better a response was than expected. Those estimates guide policy updates; the value model learns to predict returns, the cumulative discounted reward that follows each generated token. Reference and reward models remain forward-only in this loop, and shared-backbone implementations can combine roles without changing their distinct responsibilities.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Model</strong></th><th><strong>Role in PPO-style RLHF</strong></th><th><strong>Execution mode</strong></th><th><strong>Memory burden</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Policy Model</strong></td><td>The model being aligned and optimized.</td><td>Training mode with gradients, optimizer moments, and activations.</td><td>For a <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code>-parameter model in mixed precision with Adam, memory is approximately <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code> $\\times$ (<code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.bytes_weights_str]</code> $+$ <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.bytes_grad_str]</code> $+$ <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.bytes_adam_str]</code>) = <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_train_gb_str]</code>, the same budget as standard pretraining.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Reference Model</strong></td><td>A frozen copy of the pretrained policy that supplies the KL-divergence penalty.</td><td>Inference mode only.</td><td>FP16/BF16 parameters without gradients or optimizer state; a <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code> model needs about <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.bytes_infer_str]</code> = <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.ref_mem_gb_str]</code>, roughly <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.train_to_infer_mult_str]</code> less than the training configuration.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Reward Model</strong></td><td>A preference-trained scorer that produces scalar rewards for generated sequences.</td><td>Inference mode only.</td><td>Often smaller than the policy; a <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.critic_params_b_str]</code> reward model requires approximately <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.reward_mem_gb_str]</code> in FP16, but it must process every generated sequence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Value Model</strong></td><td>The PPO critic that estimates expected future reward.</td><td>Training mode, either separate or sharing a policy backbone.</td><td>A full-size separate critic can add another <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_train_gb_str]</code>; smaller models or shared-backbone designs reduce this to 200--400 GB.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>RLHF Model Coordination Roles</strong>: PPO-style RLHF coordinates models with different execution modes and memory footprints, so placement decisions must separate trainable policy and value state from inference-only reference and reward state. {#tbl-rlhf-model-coordination-roles tbl-colwidths=\"[16,24,22,38]\"}</p>\n<p>The aggregate memory demand of the four-model PPO system dwarfs standard pretraining. A naive co-location of a <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code> policy, <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code> reference, <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.critic_params_b_str]</code> reward, and <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.critic_params_b_str]</code> value model requires approximately <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.ppo_total_mem_gb_str]</code> of accelerator memory, before accounting for the KV caches and intermediate activations generated during sequence generation. A key-value (KV) cache stores attention keys and values for previously processed tokens so the policy can generate the next token without recomputing that state. It grows with the number and length of active sequences, creating a temporary memory budget distinct from weights and optimizer state. On H100 GPUs with <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.h100_mem_gb_str]</code> of HBM each, this system requires a minimum of <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.min_gpus_mem_str]</code> as an aggregate static-state capacity lower bound, including optimizer state and gradients. This division does not establish a feasible placement, because each phase must also fit its KV or activation peak, workspaces, and uneven shards. Replicated policy groups increase the total state further.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-30\" class=\"cs-h3\">Infrastructure asymmetry between training and inference models</h3>\n<p>Beyond that aggregate memory footprint, RLHF stresses cluster scheduling through the <em>asymmetry</em> between the models' compute profiles. Because the policy and value models run full backward passes with gradient computation, activation checkpointing, and optimizer updates, they execute compute-intensive operations that benefit from tensor parallelism and high arithmetic intensity. The reference and reward models, by contrast, run only forward scoring passes over already-generated sequences, requiring neither optimizer state nor token-by-token decoding, whereas the policy's rollout phase is bound by autoregressive generation and its growing KV-cache budget. Separating scoring from generation matters because forward-only execution does not imply a single memory or compute profile.</p>\n<p>The asymmetry creates a placement dilemma. Co-location permits devices to serve different phases, but inactive model state can occupy memory needed for the active phase. Dedicated pools allow each role to use a suitable execution configuration, but pools may idle while waiting for another role's output. Separation also adds transfers of generated sequences, scores, and updated policy weights. The design must compare phase utilization and data movement rather than assume that either placement eliminates waste.</p>\n<p>The PPO rollout phase introduces autoregressive dependencies absent from the teacher-forced pretraining baseline, in which each input token comes from the training data rather than from the model's previous output. The policy generates responses before sequence-level rewards guide its updates. At small active batches, decoding is often memory bandwidth bound because each new token requires another model pass. Each token in the KV cache holds a key vector and a value vector per KV head in every layer. For a Llama-2-70B-style grouped-query attention policy generating <code>[RLHFKVCache.seq_len_str]</code> tokens for each of <code>[RLHFKVCache.batch_size_str]</code> active sequences, those per-token bytes, written out in @eq-kv-bytes-per-token, put the generated-token portion at <code>[RLHFKVCache.kv_total_gb_str]</code>. That portion alone already exceeds the <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.h100_mem_gb_str]</code> of HBM on one H100, and prompt tokens add KV state as well. This temporary cache must fit alongside the state live during rollout; its lifetime differs from training activations and optimizer state.</p>\n<p>↪ The capacity wall this cache imposes on serving systems is developed in @sec-inference-scale-kv-cache-wall.</p>\n<p>RLHF systems address this asymmetry through temporal multiplexing. During the generation phase, the cluster behaves like an inference system: the policy model generates sequences while the reference model computes log-probabilities, and batching active sequences alongside their KV state determines how much useful work shares each weight read. During the training phase, the same fleet switches back to training mode and computes gradients through the policy and value models using the 3D parallelism configuration of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-12a0, where activation residency and gradient synchronization again govern the schedule. Because PPO alternates between inference-shaped rollout work across four model roles and training-shaped updates, reformulating the alignment objective itself can eliminate part of that infrastructure overhead.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-31\" class=\"cs-h3\">Resource requirements for PPO versus DPO</h3>\n<p>Standard offline DPO} [@rafailov2023dpo] takes that route by removing the separate reward and value models, along with the online rollout phase, from the update loop, reformulating alignment as a supervised loss over offline preference pairs. The policy learns directly from preferred and dispreferred responses using their log-probabilities relative to a frozen reference; because those responses are already in the training data, the update requires neither autoregressive generation nor advantage estimation.</p>\n<p>A DPO implementation that retains a resident reference has two model roles, the trainable policy and the frozen reference. For fixed preference pairs and a fixed reference, reference log-probabilities can instead be precomputed, removing that model from the repeated update loop. Offline DPO needs no autoregressive rollout during those updates. Variable lengths still require padding, packing, or length-aware batching, and policy training can use the same partitioning, accumulation, and overlap mechanisms as pretraining.</p>\n<p>The infrastructure difference follows from how each method obtains feedback. Offline DPO learns from a fixed preference dataset, so the policy never receives feedback on new responses. PPO-style training scores responses from the evolving policy and can expose behavior the dataset lacks, provided the reward signal is reliable. The generation phase, the reward and value models, and the memory they occupy are the price of that online loop, so the choice is settled by achieved quality per unit of total resource cost.</p>\n<p>To make the infrastructure trade-off concrete, consider aligning a <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.policy_params_b_str]</code> policy model using a policy/reference pool of 256 H100 GPUs (<code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.h100_mem_gb_str]</code> HBM each). PPO's separate reward and value pools sit outside that allocation. The policy is a 70B-class model rather than the 175B reference, a size at which each role's memory budget stays readable while the four PPO roles together still need at least <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.min_gpus_mem_str]</code>, more than one node.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: This alignment setup co-locates a <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_b_str]</code> policy model with a <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_b_str]</code> reference model and, for PPO, uses pools outside the <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_pool_gpus_str]</code>-GPU policy/reference allocation for a <code>[RLHFBudget.reward_b_str]</code> reward model and a <code>[RLHFBudget.value_b_str]</code> value model. Under 3D parallelism (TP=<code>[RLHFBudget.tp_str]</code>, PP=<code>[RLHFBudget.pp_value_str]</code>, DP=<code>[RLHFBudget.dp_value_str]</code>), how much memory resides on each policy GPU, and which separate model pools and generation-phase memory requirements does DPO remove?</p>\n<p><strong>PPO memory budget</strong> (per-GPU, with TP=<code>[RLHFBudget.tp_str]</code>, PP=<code>[RLHFBudget.pp_value_str]</code>, DP=<code>[RLHFBudget.dp_value_str]</code> for policy)</p>\n<p>The policy model under 3D parallelism with TP=<code>[RLHFBudget.tp_str]</code> and PP=<code>[RLHFBudget.pp_value_str]</code> distributes one replica's <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_mem_gb_str]</code> training state across <code>[RLHFBudget.n_gpus_str]</code>, yielding <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU. The reference model, requiring only <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_mem_gb_str]</code> for inference, is co-located with the policy GPUs at an additional <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU (<code>[RLHFBudget.ref_mem_gb_str]</code> / <code>[RLHFBudget.n_gpus_str]</code>). It could instead be sharded across an additional <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_pool_gpus_str]</code> pool outside the policy allocation at <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_per_gpu_sep_gb_str]</code> each. The <code>[RLHFBudget.reward_b_str]</code> reward model adds <code>[RLHFBudget.reward_mem_gb_str]</code> shared across its pool. The <code>[RLHFBudget.value_b_str]</code> value model in training mode adds approximately <code>[RLHFBudget.value_mem_gb_str]</code> (<code>[RLHFBudget.value_b_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[RLHFBudget.bytes_train_value_str]</code> bytes/param) across its pool.</p>\n<p>Total static memory (one co-located policy + reference replica on <code>[RLHFBudget.n_gpus_str]</code>): <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> + <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[RLHFBudget.static_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU, leaving $\\sim$ <code>[RLHFBudget.remaining_gb_str]</code> for activations and KV cache. With generation-phase KV caches for a rollout batch containing <code>[RLHFBudget.rollout_seq_str]</code> and <code>[RLHFBudget.rollout_tokens_str]</code> inside one $\\Glyph{T} \\Glyph{P}{\\times}\\Glyph{P} \\Glyph{P}$ policy replica, each policy GPU must reserve an additional <code>[RLHFBudget.kv_total_gb_str]</code>/<code>[RLHFBudget.n_gpus_str]</code> $\\approx$ <code>[RLHFBudget.kv_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> for its KV cache shard. The remaining $\\sim$ <code>[RLHFBudget.remaining_kv_gb_str]</code> is generation-phase headroom. Once rollout KV state is released, the update phase recovers the <code>[RLHFBudget.remaining_gb_str]</code> headroom before allocating training activations and other runtime buffers. The phase peaks must be evaluated separately.</p>\n<p><strong>DPO memory budget (same parallelism configuration)</strong></p>\n<p>The policy model uses the same <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU. The reference model adds <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU (co-located). No reward model, no value model, no KV cache for generation.</p>\n<p>On the policy GPUs, total static memory is <code>[RLHFBudget.policy_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> + <code>[RLHFBudget.ref_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> = <code>[RLHFBudget.static_per_gpu_gb_str]</code> per GPU, identical to PPO's co-located policy/reference footprint. Both have <code>[RLHFBudget.remaining_gb_str]</code> available before update-phase activations and runtime buffers when PPO releases its rollout KV cache. DPO removes the separate generation peak and the reward/value model pools; the absence of rollout KV state alone does not establish larger update microbatches.</p>\n<p><strong>Throughput comparison</strong></p>\n<p>PPO alternates generation and parameter updates. If generation consumes <code>[RLHFBudget.gen_phase_pct_str]</code> of an iteration in this illustrative schedule, updates occupy the remaining <code>[RLHFBudget.train_phase_pct_str]</code>. That time fraction is a share of the schedule rather than an MFU, so update-phase MFU must be multiplied by it to report update computation over the whole iteration, using the same allocated hardware denominator. DPO eliminates online generation, but its measured throughput still depends on sequence lengths, reference scoring, parallelism, and memory use. The static-state comparison in @tbl-ppo-vs-dpo is therefore kept separate from these schedule-dependent effects.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Metric</strong></th><th><strong>PPO (70B policy)</strong></th><th><strong>DPO (70B policy)</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Models required</strong></td><td>4 (policy, ref, reward, value)</td><td>2 (policy, reference)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Static state, one copy of each model</strong></td><td>$\\sim$ <code>[RLHFBudget.ppo_total_mem_gb_str]</code></td><td>$\\sim$ <code>[RLHFBudget.dpo_total_mem_gb_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Device count</strong></td><td>Depends on placement and phase peak</td><td>Depends on placement and activation peak</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Generation phase</strong></td><td>Yes (autoregressive)</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Update throughput</strong></td><td>Shares the schedule with generation</td><td>No online-generation phase</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Data pipeline</strong></td><td>Online generation</td><td>Static preference pairs</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>PPO vs. DPO State and Execution Phases</strong>: Static-state totals sum one copy of each model across its device pool, including trainable optimizer state and gradients. Data-parallel replication and phase-specific runtime allocations add to these totals; the table does not determine a minimum device count or measured MFU. {#tbl-ppo-vs-dpo}</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: In this <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.critic_params_b_str]</code> reward/value-model scenario, DPO reduces the one-copy static-state budget by <code>[RLHFBudget.dpo_reduction_pct_str]</code> by eliminating the reward and value models. It also removes online generation, but the throughput benefit requires a measured schedule. Infrastructure constraints therefore weigh on the choice between PPO and DPO as much as modeling preferences do. An organization with a limited GPU budget may prefer DPO because it restores the workload to a fixed-data training loop. If the PPO value model is policy-sized rather than <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.critic_params_b_str]</code>, the reduction is larger still, from <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.ppo_policy_sized_value_mem_gb_str]</code> to <code>[DistTrainH100Capacity.dpo_total_mem_gb_str]</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-32\" class=\"cs-h3\">Sequence length variance and batching challenges</h3>\n<p>The throughput comparison in notebook \\ref{nbk-distributed-training-rlhf-infrastructure-budget-ppo-vs-dpo} noted that even after DPO removes online generation, realized step time still depends on sequence-length variance. Pretraining often packs documents into fixed-length blocks, such as 2,048 or 4,096 tokens, whereas alignment batches combine prompts and responses whose lengths can span a 10--50$\\times$ range within a single minibatch. Those variable lengths directly govern activation residency, padding waste, and load balance across workers.</p>\n<p>Length variance creates two interacting problems. Padding every sequence to the longest in a batch can leave most token positions unused. A batch of one <code>[DistTrainPaddingWaste.long_len_str]</code>-token sequence and fifteen <code>[DistTrainPaddingWaste.short_len_str]</code>-token sequences, padded to <code>[DistTrainPaddingWaste.long_len_str]</code> tokens, fills only <code>[DistTrainPaddingWaste.occupied_str]</code> of its <code>[DistTrainPaddingWaste.positions_str]</code> positions. Kernels that execute padded positions therefore spend about <code>[DistTrainPaddingWaste.padding_pct_str]</code> of their work on padding, while kernels that skip them recover most of that waste. Dynamic batching groups similar lengths to reduce padding, but assignments across workers still need balancing. A worker processing longer sequences can have a larger activation peak and finish later, leaving other workers waiting for the required gradient contributions.</p>\n<p>Sequence packing combines short sequences within a fixed-length input, with attention masking that keeps their contexts separate, and adaptive microbatching sizes each microbatch by token count or predicted memory. Packing must also respect the weighting rule of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-dataset-splitting-1edf. The reduction must keep the objective's own denominator, valid-token counts for a token-mean PPO loss and pair counts for standard DPO [@rafailov2023dpo], so packing changes layout but not weighting. Balancing token work across workers then remains a performance goal rather than a correctness requirement.</p>\n<p>RLHF shows why no single parallelism strategy is sufficient by itself. The policy model may need tensor and pipeline parallelism, the reference and reward models behave like inference services, the value model may require training state, and the rollout data pipeline changes shape from step to step. The procedure that handles this case is the general one, which identifies the limiting constraint first and then chooses the parallelism pattern that moves the least data while fitting the required state.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-33\" class=\"cs-h2\">Parallelism Strategy Comparison</h2>\n<p>A parallelism strategy is useful only if it moves the limiting constraint without creating a larger one. Assembling data, tensor, pipeline, and hybrid partitioning into a coherent decision procedure requires evaluating where each strategy relocates overhead across the cluster's memory and interconnect hierarchy. For a new model, the decisive questions are which memory state fits, how often communication occurs, where the pipeline bubble appears, and which abstraction cost the engineering team can tolerate. Sequence, context, and expert parallelism sit beside the 3D core in @tbl-parallelism-compare, and they remain subject to the same batch, memory, and topology constraints.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Strategy</strong></th><th><strong>Binding constraint</strong></th><th><strong>Primary movement</strong></th><th><strong>Placement decision</strong></th><th><strong>Validation signal</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>DDP</strong></td><td>Model state fits on one accelerator</td><td>AllReduce gradients every step</td><td>Replicate full model across data-parallel workers</td><td>Step time dominated by compute, not synchronization</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>FSDP/ZeRO</strong></td><td>Model state exceeds one accelerator</td><td>Gather parameters; ReduceScatter gradients</td><td>Size prefetch and reshard windows within peak memory</td><td>State plus activations and workspaces fit</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Tensor parallelism</strong></td><td>One layer exceeds local capacity or runs too slowly</td><td>Reduce or gather partial layer results</td><td>Keep tightly coupled partitions on fast local links</td><td>Exposed collective time fits the layer-time budget</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Pipeline parallelism</strong></td><td>Layer stack fits only when split by stage</td><td>Activations and gradients between stages</td><td>Balance stages and live microbatch activations</td><td>Bubble and peak activation memory meet budget</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sequence/context parallelism</strong></td><td>Activations or attention context exceed local capacity</td><td>Exchange sequence shards or attention state</td><td>Partition long sequences across a low-latency group</td><td>Longer context fits without communication dominating</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Expert parallelism</strong></td><td>Sparse expert capacity exceeds local memory</td><td>Route token activations with AllToAll</td><td>Balance expert load across a high-bisection group</td><td>Expert imbalance and dropped-token rate stay bounded</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Hybrid parallelism</strong></td><td>More than one constraint binds at once</td><td>Multiple collectives on coupled axes</td><td>Satisfy divisibility, batch, topology, and failure domains</td><td>MFU improves within memory and network budgets</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Parallel Training Strategies</strong>: Data, tensor, and pipeline parallelism form the canonical 3D core. Sequence/context parallelism extends the partition along token positions, while expert parallelism extends it across sparse model capacity. Strategy selection remains an engineering feasibility test: identify the binding constraint, the communication primitive it creates, the placement decision it forces, and the measurement that proves the strategy is helping. {#tbl-parallelism-compare tbl-colwidths=\"[15,22,20,23,20]\"}</p>\n<p>A decision path condenses the table, starting from memory fit and then asking which structure breaks the budget (@fig-parallelism-flowchart). The <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> reference model takes the layer branch on step time rather than capacity. An average layer holds about <code>[DecisionPathReference.layer_state_gb_str]</code> of training state, which fits one GPU. Without tensor parallelism, however, the rule that sets configuration D's depth, the shallowest power-of-two pipeline at which unsharded state fits, gives <code>[DecisionPathReference.pp_no_tp_str]</code> single-GPU stages instead of <code>[DecisionPathReference.pp_ref_str]</code>. At the same <code>[DecisionPathReference.m_ref_str]</code> microbatches per replica, that pipeline's bubble idles <code>[DecisionPathReference.bubble_no_tp_pct_str]</code> of each step rather than <code>[DecisionPathReference.bubble_ref_pct_str]</code>, and the modeled step grows from <code>[DecisionPathReference.step_ref_s_str]</code> to <code>[DecisionPathReference.step_no_tp_s_str]</code>. Tensor parallelism inside the NVLink node is what keeps the pipeline shallow, and because its <code>[DecisionPathReference.tp_shard_gb_str]</code> shards still exceed one GPU, the path ends at combining axes. Before accepting any branch, check peak memory, global batch, communication service time, and load balance together; bandwidth is a feasibility constraint throughout the decision, not an afterthought.</p>\n<p>Identifying the limiting constraint comes before choosing an implementation stack, which is why both the strategy comparison and the decision path stay deliberately coarse. Dense large-model training and recommendation-style workloads motivated each of these partitioning strategies. The same constraint logic also reaches the federated edge case (@tbl-partitioning-archetypes), where the bottleneck shifts from accelerator memory and fabric bandwidth to unreliable devices, privacy, and intermittent connectivity. A configuration chosen across these regimes still has to be expressed in software, and the way a framework expresses it decides which of the chapter's budgets the engineer controls directly.</p>\n<p>Partitioning and coordination choices differ with the system's primary bottleneck:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Archetype</strong></th><th><strong>Primary partitioning strategy</strong></th><th><strong>The logic</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3)</strong></td><td>Hybrid 3D parallelism</td><td>Combine tensor (width), pipeline (depth), and data (throughput) parallelism to fit roughly <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.gpt4_mem_tb_str]</code> of weights under third-party estimates (OpenAI has not disclosed the parameter count).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Archetype B (DLRM at Scale)</strong></td><td>Embedding sharding</td><td>Partition massive <code>[DistTrainReliabilityFacts.dlrm_mem_tb_str]</code>+ tables across parameter servers or accelerator shards; use sparse AllToAll updates.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Archetype C (Federated MobileNet)</strong></td><td>Federated learning</td><td>The same logic extends to coordinating on-device MobileNet updates across unreliable, privacy-constrained edge devices; @sec-edge-intelligence-federated-systems develops this regime.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Partitioning Strategy by Archetype</strong>: Partitioning and coordination choices differ with the dominant bottleneck. {#tbl-partitioning-archetypes}</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-34\" class=\"cs-h2\">Frameworks and Runtime Contracts</h2>\n<p>A TP-8, PP-16, DP-128 layout that passes every feasibility check on paper can still hang at its first collective if two ranks disagree on the membership of their communication group, or run slowly if gradient buckets become ready in an order the collective runtime cannot overlap. Choosing DDP, FSDP/ZeRO-style sharding, tensor parallelism, or pipeline parallelism is therefore also a decision about which constraints the runtime owns and which constraints the engineer must manage directly. Sparse workloads add another contract, the parameter-server push/pull interface [@li2014] developed for Archetype B in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-parameter-servers-embedding-sharding-8821.</p>\n<p>The training framework defines process groups (its records of which ranks form each tensor-parallel, pipeline, or data-parallel group), gradient buckets, and the point in backpropagation when each reduction becomes ready; the collective runtime maps those reductions onto ring, tree, or hierarchical algorithms. The engineer must choose compatible group membership and bucket order and verify which communication remains exposed on the step's critical path.</p>\n<p>Production frameworks expose these same strategies under different names, which makes a translation aid useful when reading their documentation or configuration files. Four widely used stacks name the chapter's strategies differently (@tbl-framework-strategy-map); the names change between releases, but the partition each one implements does not.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Strategy</strong></th><th><strong>PyTorch</strong></th><th><strong>DeepSpeed</strong></th><th><strong>Megatron-LM</strong></th><th><strong>JAX</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Data parallel</strong></td><td>DistributedDataParallel (DDP)</td><td>DeepSpeed engine, ZeRO stage 0</td><td>Data-parallel process groups</td><td>Batch axis sharded over a device mesh</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sharded data parallel</strong></td><td>Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP)</td><td>ZeRO stages 1--3</td><td>Distributed optimizer</td><td>Parameter and optimizer state sharded over a mesh axis</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Tensor parallel</strong></td><td>DTensor tensor-parallel APIs</td><td>AutoTP or Megatron integration</td><td>Column- and row-parallel linear layers</td><td>Sharding annotations on model axes, partitioned by the compiler</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Pipeline parallel</strong></td><td><code>torch.distributed.pipelining</code></td><td>Pipeline engine</td><td>Pipeline schedules (1F1B, interleaved)</td><td>User-written stage schedules</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Framework Names for the Parallelism Strategies</strong>: A translation aid, not a ranking. Each entry names the mechanism a framework uses for the partition developed in this chapter; exact class and option names vary across releases. {#tbl-framework-strategy-map tbl-colwidths=\"[16,20,18,22,24]\"}</p>\n<p>The training design therefore determines message size, readiness order, and frequency, while collective algorithms determine how those messages move. No framework abstraction changes those physical costs, so distributed-training efficiency ultimately depends on interconnect bandwidth, memory capacity, synchronization latency, and the useful overlap admitted by the computation graph. Confusing the simplicity of a framework API with exemption from those physical costs produces the recurring engineering mistakes examined next.</p>\n<p>↩ Overlap of gradient exchange with backpropagation, the lever that takes communication off a step's critical path, is established in @sec-communication-overlap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fallacies and Pitfalls</h2>\n<p>Even when a framework makes switching from single-node DDP to multi-node execution a one-line configuration change, the underlying physics still governs the step time. In the GPT-2 scale-out of notebook \\ref{nbk-distributed-training-gpt-2-data-parallel-scaling} and notebook \\ref{nbk-distributed-training-gpt-2-scale-out}, moving the same model from one NVLink node to four Ethernet-connected nodes quadrupled the GPU count but cut parallel efficiency from <code>[Scaling8GPU.efficiency_8gpu_str]</code> to <code>[Scaling32GPU.efficiency_32gpu_str]</code>. The following misconceptions generalize that error, each treating one term of the step-time accounting as if it were the whole budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Linear speedup is achievable with sufficient engineering effort.</em></p>\n<p>Amdahl's Law limits speedup when a fixed serial component remains exposed. Synchronous gradient aggregation creates an update dependency, but some of its work can overlap backward computation. As @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-distributed-training-efficiency-metrics-9488 shows, $\\ScalingEfficiency = 1/(1 + \\DeviceCount(\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount) + \\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount) - \\OverlappedTime)/\\IronLawComputeTime)$ depends on the exposed overhead relative to useful compute as $\\DeviceCount$ changes. A 1,000-GPU allocation therefore does not by itself justify a 1,000$\\times$ speedup forecast. Local kernel efficiency, memory feasibility, communication, and convergence must be evaluated against the specified baseline. Linear scaling is a result to demonstrate for an operating range, not a return guaranteed by device count.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Optimizing MFU without considering scaling efficiency.</em></p>\n<p>A single-node MFU measurement does not predict fleet throughput by itself. For a fixed workload and hardware peak convention, scaling efficiency compares measured distributed throughput with ideal replication of that single-node baseline. Multiplying the baseline MFU by this scaling efficiency gives distributed MFU; multiplying an already measured distributed MFU by the same scaling efficiency counts communication and synchronization losses twice. The <code>[FrontierTrainingContext.frontier_params_b_str]</code> reference job makes the difference concrete. A single node running at <code>[MfuScalingPitfall.local_mfu_str]</code> MFU, scaled with the job's <code>[MfuScalingPitfall.eta_str]</code> modeled scaling efficiency on the <code>[DenseLLMReferenceFacts.cluster_gpus_str]</code>-GPU cluster (TP-<code>[MfuScalingPitfall.tp_str]</code>, PP-<code>[MfuScalingPitfall.pp_str]</code>, DP-<code>[MfuScalingPitfall.dp_str]</code>, configuration D of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-hybrid-parallelism-12a0, with tensor-parallel and data-parallel communication and the pipeline bubble all charged), delivers about <code>[MfuScalingPitfall.fleet_mfu_str]</code> MFU across the fleet. A planner who measures that <code>[MfuScalingPitfall.fleet_mfu_str]</code> on the full cluster and then applies the <code>[MfuScalingPitfall.eta_str]</code> again would budget for about <code>[MfuScalingPitfall.double_counted_str]</code> and buy capacity the job does not need. Capacity planning must state the device count and measurement window for each metric. Checkpointing, recovery, and replay require additional accounting only when those losses fall outside that window. Useful goodput must count retained model progress rather than repeated work, under the accounting boundaries of @sec-performance-engineering-goodput-boundaries.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Hyperparameters tuned on small clusters transfer directly to large-scale training.</em></p>\n<p>Engineers tune hyperparameters on 8-GPU workstations then deploy to 256-GPU clusters expecting identical behavior. At scale, convergence behavior changes. The most sensitive hyperparameter is learning rate. As @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-data-parallelism-6132 explains, global batch increases with GPU count when local batch and accumulation depth remain fixed, so the learning-rate schedule must be revalidated. The linear scaling rule of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-learning-rate-scaling-rules-fa26, which the one-hour ImageNet run established, suggests $\\ScaledLearningRate = \\BaseLearningRate\\times (\\ScaledBatchSize/\\BaseBatchSize)$, but this relationship holds only within bounds. As models scale, they eventually encounter the critical batch size of @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-critical-batch-size-parallelism-hurt-4961, where adding more data per step yields diminishing returns in convergence.</p>\n<p>Beyond the critical batch size, this relationship breaks down in a model-, data-, and optimizer-dependent way. A team that takes a small-cluster learning rate, multiplies it mechanically with GPU count, and jumps directly to a much larger global batch may see lower final quality or slower sample efficiency even though hardware throughput improved. Warmup schedules, weight decay adjustment, layer-wise optimizers such as LARS/LAMB, and careful momentum tuning can recover accuracy in some regimes, but require systematic experimentation at target scale. Organizations that skip these scaling studies waste thousands of GPU-hours on suboptimal runs.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Adding GPUs to data-parallel jobs without modeling communication.</em></p>\n<p>More GPUs improve time to target only if the gain in processing rate outweighs communication overhead and any additional sample use. With fixed local batch size, adding data-parallel workers increases the global batch; fixing the global batch instead reduces local work and can expose more communication. The critical-batch analysis in @sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-critical-batch-size-parallelism-hurt-4961 supplies a workload-dependent statistical guide, not universal GPU-count or model-size thresholds. Compare complete learning curves and cost at the same target quality before committing to a larger allocation.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Memory capacity alone determines the parallelism strategy.</em></p>\n<p>A model that exceeds device capacity needs a feasible partition, but memory fit does not select the fastest one. Tensor parallelism exchanges partial results within layers, while pipeline parallelism transfers stage-boundary activations and introduces scheduling bubbles. Their relative cost depends on layer shapes, microbatch count, placement, and overlap. Compare complete training-state and activation budgets together with measured time to target; an unmeasured throughput estimate or a weights-only fit cannot settle the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Applying FSDP or ZeRO without measuring efficiency trade-offs.</em></p>\n<p>Engineers adopt FSDP universally after reading that it \"reduces memory and enables larger models.\" Sharding trades memory capacity for communication overhead. Full-shard FSDP gathers parameters before layer execution, gathers them again for the backward pass if the forward pass resharded them, and reduces gradient shards during backward. The relevant comparison begins with the complete training-state budget. Under the mixed-precision Adam convention established here, the 7B worked example exceeds single-device capacity before activations, so standard DDP cannot fit that state on an A100-<code>[A100CapacityFallacies.a100_mem_gb_str]</code>. A weights-only fit does not establish a feasible training baseline. When the complete state, activations, and workspaces fit without sharding, DDP can avoid the repeated gathers; when they exceed the <code>[A100CapacityFallacies.a100_mem_gb_str]</code> capacity, the comparison must include a feasible alternative such as sharding or offload. FSDP provides value when it enables training at all or when the larger local batch it permits offsets its communication cost. ZeRO-Offload extends capacity through CPU memory but adds transfers of its own. The decision therefore requires measuring peak memory and exposed communication for the same workload and optimizer contract.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Parallelism overhead is roughly constant regardless of model size.</em></p>\n<p>Engineers benchmark parallelism strategies on convenient small models then apply those conclusions to large-scale training. For dense transformers at fixed tokens per update, computation and gradient bytes both grow roughly proportionally with parameter count, as the earlier scaling analysis established. Larger parameter count alone therefore does not imply a better computation-to-communication ratio. Architecture, sequence length, local batch size, partition degree, and kernel efficiency determine how much useful work each rank performs; gradient representation and process-group membership determine how much it communicates. A larger model can also require a different partition that introduces new layer collectives or pipeline bubbles. Reliable strategy selection holds the workload and target quality explicit, then recomputes memory fit, local compute time, communication volume, and the available overlap window at the target scale. A small-model benchmark becomes transferable evidence only when those governing terms remain comparable.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Treating scaling efficiency as a property of the hardware alone.</em></p>\n<p>A vendor benchmark might report 85 percent scaling efficiency at 1,024 GPUs, and procurement teams then plan capacity as if that number were a cluster constant. Scaling efficiency is a joint property of four things: the workload's communication-to-computation ratio, the parallelism strategy (data, pipeline, tensor, hybrid), the network topology (fat-tree vs. torus vs. dragonfly), and the software stack (NCCL version, kernel fusion, overlap quality). Reference numbers from dense large-language-model benchmarks transfer poorly to other workloads and fabrics. They may overstate scaling for graph neural networks with irregular communication and for embedding-heavy recommendation models, whose AllToAll embedding exchanges and many small random lookups load the fabric differently from dense gradient AllReduce (@sec-distributed-training-systems-systems-parameter-servers-embedding-sharding-8821). They also say nothing about a commodity-Ethernet placement like the GPT-2 scale-out, which lost most of its parallel efficiency on leaving the node. Capacity planning should use measured scaling on the target workload, not a vendor hero benchmark.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Gradient accumulation is free.</em></p>\n<p>Gradient accumulation can reduce synchronization from every microstep to once per window of $\\AccumulationStepCount$ microsteps, eliminating $(\\AccumulationStepCount-1)/\\AccumulationStepCount$ of those synchronization events when the implementation defers reduction. The saving has costs. A resident gradient buffer remains live throughout the accumulation window; for the <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.params_b_str]</code> model, the FP16 buffer occupies <code>[DistTrainA100Memory.weights_gb_str]</code>. Retaining graphs across microsteps or increasing the local batch size can add activation pressure. Accumulating eight microsteps makes updates 8$\\times$ less frequent per processed microstep, but wall-clock spacing also depends on the communication saved. Larger effective batches can change convergence, and narrow accumulation formats can lose small contributions or overflow. The dtype, loss normalization, and reduction schedule must therefore be validated at the intended batch size. Accumulation trades communication frequency against update spacing, memory residency, and numerical stability.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-36\" class=\"cs-h2\">Summary</h2>\n<p>Scaling laws keep pushing frontier training budgets past what one machine can hold or finish in time, and distributed training is how the fleet absorbs that growth. It confronts the scaling wall, the workload-dependent operating point beyond which communication and coordination can consume the compute time saved by adding accelerators. A feasible design must preserve the intended optimization update while satisfying model-state, activation, batch-size, and interconnect constraints. Hardware throughput alone is insufficient; the comparison must also account for time to the target model quality.</p>\n<p>Each partition relocates cost along the C³ axes. Data parallelism divides compute across replicas and pays in communication, the gradient aggregation whose exposed share set the scaling efficiency of the 175B communication example. Tensor parallelism divides work inside a layer and pays in communication on the critical path, which confines it to a fast domain. Pipeline parallelism divides layer depth and pays mostly in coordination, the fill and drain bubble that only more microbatches, better balance, or a smarter schedule can shrink. The 3D parallelism cube in @fig-3d-parallelism-cube unites these trade-offs in one coordinate space ($\\DataParallelDegree \\times \\PipelineParallelDegree \\times \\TensorParallelDegree$). A feasible point must satisfy peak memory and global-batch constraints as well as communication and coordination budgets.</p>\n<p>Sharding and model partitioning make different parts of the training state fit, while synchronization and precision policies determine whether the resulting updates remain useful. Hybrid layouts compose these choices within the bandwidth hierarchy. The alignment workload extends the same reasoning to multiple models and alternating generation and update phases, where peak memory depends on which state is live together.</p>\n<p>The chapter's strategies are complementary dimensions of one design space rather than alternatives. Systems such as Megatron-LM map tensor, pipeline, data, and expert exchanges to the communication domains that can carry them, and that matching is what delays the communication-bound regime as the fleet grows. The workload archetypes weight those dimensions differently. Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) combines dense-model partitioning with state sharding and, at long contexts, sequence or context partitioning. Archetype B (DLRM at Scale) shards sparse embedding state and pays in lookup traffic rather than layer collectives. Archetype C (Federated MobileNet) moves the coordination problem onto unreliable edge devices. In each case the limiting constraint, not the archetype label, selects the configuration.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Takeaways</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Splitting work moves the bottleneck</strong>: Distributed training does not make the underlying computation smaller; it converts memory pressure into communication volume, synchronization delay, or idle pipeline time (principle \\ref{pri-scaling-efficiency}). The winning strategy is the split that sends overhead to the least constrained part of the fleet.</li>\n<li><strong>The slowest worker sets synchronous step time</strong>: Under bulk-synchronous updates every step waits for its last required contribution, so a rare per-worker delay becomes a near-certain per-step delay at thousands of workers. That wait preserves the single-device global-batch update, which also needs matching sampling, valid-item denominators, and optimizer state; stale and asynchronous protocols trade that wait for staleness, which can cost extra steps to the same target quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Throughput must serve convergence</strong>: Larger global batches can improve hardware efficiency while yielding diminishing progress per sample. Compare time to the same target quality, and treat the critical-batch estimate as a workload-dependent guide for selecting batch size and update schedules.</li>\n<li><strong>Sharding buys memory with messages</strong>: ZeRO and FSDP partition optimizer state, gradients, and parameters (principle \\ref{pri-memory-capacity-gap}), reducing replicated storage. Peak memory must still include activations, temporary gathers, and workspaces; exposed ReduceScatter and AllGather costs determine whether the capacity gain also improves throughput.</li>\n<li><strong>Tensor parallelism needs a fast domain</strong>: Splitting operations inside layers creates frequent communication on the dependency path. Place those groups on links with sufficient bandwidth and low latency; the relevant boundary is the communication domain, which may span nodes, rather than the server enclosure.</li>\n<li><strong>Pipeline parallelism trades bytes for bubbles</strong>: Layer staging reduces communication frequency, but fill and drain slots leave accelerators idle (principle \\ref{pri-distributed-step-time}). Microbatching with $\\MicrobatchCount\\gg \\PipelineParallelDegree$ is the mechanism that turns model depth into throughput rather than pipeline slack.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid layouts are joint decisions</strong>: Combine only the partitioning and sharding strategies needed to satisfy the workload's constraints (principle \\ref{pri-conservation-overhead}). Model divisibility, batch size, peak memory, communication groups, and failure domains must form one feasible configuration; improving one axis can expose a limit on another.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>The displacement of overhead (principle \\ref{pri-conservation-overhead}) makes a feasible partition the beginning of the design problem. A memory-saving split is useful only if its communication and schedule still meet the run's time-to-quality budget. Every mechanism in this chapter shrinks the per-device compute term $\\IronLawComputeTime/\\DeviceCount$ or the per-device training state, and pays for it in $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$, in $\\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ through barriers, stragglers, and pipeline bubbles, or in lost overlap. In C³ terms, no partition removes the overhead of scale; it only relocates that overhead among compute, communication, and coordination. The engineering judgment is therefore to compare complete configurations, including their exposed dependencies, rather than optimize each parallelism axis independently.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Chapter Connection</div>\n<p>The fleet now has its traffic patterns (communication) and its logic (parallelism). Every configuration in this chapter, however, assumed that every rank delivers its contribution on time and with correct data. A collective is not a barrier, but no dependent consumer can proceed until every contribution arrives, so one crashed node, failed link, or GPU that slows down without failing stalls the whole synchronous group. A contribution corrupted in transit is reduced into every replica's update. Across thousands of accelerators such events stop being rare. @Sec-fault-tolerance-reliability begins by computing how often they arrive, then builds the checkpointing, recovery, and elastic training that keep the computation making progress on imperfect hardware.</p>\n</div>", "quizzes": [{"section": "Why Distribution Is Necessary", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A research team is training a 3B-parameter transformer that fits comfortably in one H100 GPU's 80 GB of memory, but projected single-GPU training would take 18 months to reach target loss. The dataset is 3 TB and fits on local NVMe storage. Which signal makes distributed training necessary in this scenario?", "choices": ["Dataset exceeding GPU HBM capacity, which automatically mandates tensor parallelism regardless of execution time", "The presence of NVLink inside the node, which makes multi-machine scaling mandatory by default", "Unacceptable wall-clock training duration, even though single-device memory capacity and local storage are sufficient", "Fault tolerance limitations, because single-GPU training cannot survive hardware restarts"], "answer": "The correct answer is C. The deadline is the binding constraint even though the complete model fits and local storage is sufficient. Memory exhaustion can motivate sharding, offload, or other capacity remedies; dataset capacity and delivery rate can motivate distributed data services without forcing model computation across devices. NVLink enables exchanges when needed but does not mandate distribution. Single-device jobs can checkpoint and recover from interruption."}, {"section": "Why Distribution Is Necessary", "type": "SHORT", "question": "A team doubles a data-parallel cluster from 64 to 128 GPUs and observes that total step time drops by only 12 percent even though per-worker compute time halves. Using the distributed step-time equation $T_{\\text{step}}(N) = T_{\\text{compute}}/N + T_{\\text{comm}}(N) + T_{\\text{sync}}(N) - T_{\\text{overlap}}$, explain what the observation establishes and what measurement would distinguish possible causes.", "choices": [], "answer": "The observation establishes that halving per-worker compute did not halve the complete step. Exposed communication, synchronization, reduced overlap, or another critical-path delay can explain the residual; the step-time ratio alone does not identify which one binds. Compare aligned rank timelines for gradient readiness, collective transfer, barrier waiting, input delivery, and kernel execution. Collective waiting may reflect a late participant rather than limited fabric capacity, so link utilization during the critical interval is needed to support a network diagnosis."}, {"section": "The Parallelism Design Space", "type": "MCQ", "question": "Framing distributed parallelism strategies as compiler loop transformations across the training loop's iterators (batches, layers, and operations), which distributed strategy corresponds to vectorization (SIMD) of inner matrix multiplications?", "choices": ["Tensor parallelism, which partitions inner matrix-multiplication operations across devices and exchanges partial results over the interconnect", "Data parallelism, which unrolls the outer batch loop across devices so each rank processes independent data indices", "Pipeline parallelism, which stages sequential layer operations across devices analogous to CPU instruction pipelining", "Model replication, which duplicates the entire forward and backward execution redundantly across machines"], "answer": "The correct answer is A. Tensor parallelism partitions inner matrix operations, analogous to vectorizing the inner loop. The analogy describes the computational cut; NVLink still exchanges messages between devices with separate memory and does not provide a shared vector register file. Unrolling the outer batch loop describes data parallelism, while staging layer groups describes pipeline parallelism. Replication does not partition an operation."}, {"section": "The Parallelism Design Space", "type": "MCQ", "question": "In the 3D parallelism cube, where the device count equals the product of the data-, pipeline-, and tensor-parallel degrees, how do the three scaling axes interact to allocate cluster accelerators for large models?", "choices": ["The three axes are mutually exclusive; choosing data parallelism precludes using tensor or pipeline parallelism in the same cluster", "Tensor and pipeline parallelism can combine within a node, but data parallelism can only be used if model parallelism is disabled", "The total accelerator count is the sum of the three degrees, with each parallelism type requiring a disjoint node pool", "The axes compose multiplicatively, so one layout can split layers (TP), stage the layer stack (PP), and replicate the pipeline across data shards (DP), and its device count is the product of the three degrees"], "answer": "The correct answer is D. Data, pipeline, and tensor parallelism partition different workload dimensions, and the product of their degrees gives the accelerator count. The degrees are not independent knobs: tensor and pipeline degrees must divide the model, the data-parallel degree and microbatch schedule must give a viable global batch, every partition must fit memory, and each axis's traffic must map onto a tier that can carry it. Large-model configurations routinely compose all three, for example TP=8 inside an NVLink node, PP=16 across nodes, and DP=128 across the cluster. Mutual exclusivity, a ban on combining data and model parallelism, and an additive count all contradict that multiplicative composition."}, {"section": "Data Parallelism", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A 7B-parameter transformer in mixed precision requires 112 GB of static memory for standard replicated DDP (14 GB for FP16 weights, 14 GB for FP16 gradients, and 84 GB for FP32 Adam master weights, momentum, and variance). Under ZeRO Stage 3 (or FSDP Full Shard) across 64 GPUs, what is the resulting static training memory per GPU?", "choices": ["$112\\text{ GB}$, because ZeRO-3 only reduces dynamic activation memory rather than model state", "$28\\text{ GB}$, because only optimizer states are sharded while parameters and gradients remain fully replicated", "$1.75\\text{ GB}$, because weights, gradients, and optimizer states are each partitioned equally across all 64 workers", "$0.25\\text{ GB}$, because INT4 quantization eliminates floating-point representation entirely"], "answer": "The correct answer is C. ZeRO-3 / FSDP partitions all three components of static training state, namely FP16 model weights (2 bytes/param), FP16 gradients (2 bytes/param), and FP32 Adam states (12 bytes/param), across the $N=64$ ranks ($16\\text{ bytes/param} / 64 = 0.25\\text{ bytes/param}$ per device). For a 7B model ($7 \\times 10^9 \\times 0.25\\text{ bytes} = 1.75\\text{ GB}$ per GPU), this leaves substantial HBM headroom for dynamic activations. Asserting that state remains 112 GB ignores parameter and optimizer sharding. ZeRO-1 retains 28 GB of weights and gradients plus its optimizer shard, so its total is greater than 28 GB. Claiming INT4 compression confuses precision quantization with state sharding."}, {"section": "Data Parallelism", "type": "SHORT", "question": "An 8-GPU node can use four-step gradient accumulation to match the effective batch of 32 GPUs connected by 10 Gb/s Ethernet. Explain why the smaller allocation can cost less even when the larger allocation completes the fixed-sample workload sooner.", "choices": [], "answer": "Four accumulation microsteps synchronize once per optimizer update, keeping collective traffic inside the fast local interconnect while matching the effective global batch. The 32-GPU allocation adds arithmetic capacity but pays inter-node communication overhead and rents four nodes. Compare node-hours at the same sample budget and target quality: lower hourly capacity cost can outweigh longer elapsed time, so better cost efficiency does not imply higher throughput."}, {"section": "Scaling Efficiency and Convergence", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A team scales a data-parallel training run from 8 to 64 workers, increasing the effective global batch size from 512 to 4,096 samples. If the model operates comfortably below its critical batch size, which learning-rate adjustment follows the linear scaling heuristic?", "choices": ["Keep the learning rate unchanged, because large-batch variance reduction automatically stabilizes optimization", "Multiply the learning rate by 64 to match the total worker count rather than the batch expansion ratio", "Multiply the learning rate by $\\sqrt{8}$ from step zero without a warmup schedule", "Multiply the learning rate by $8\\times$ (matching the batch expansion ratio) and include a gradual linear warmup schedule"], "answer": "The correct answer is D. The batch expansion is $4096/512=8$, so the linear scaling heuristic multiplies the learning rate by eight, with warmup to limit early instability. It is a starting configuration to validate, not a universal optimizer rule. Scaling by the total worker count uses the wrong ratio; square-root scaling is a different heuristic."}, {"section": "Scaling Efficiency and Convergence", "type": "SHORT", "question": "Explain the distinction between hardware efficiency and statistical efficiency in distributed training, and describe a concrete scenario where a cluster configuration achieves high hardware efficiency but poor statistical efficiency.", "choices": [], "answer": "Hardware efficiency measures how effectively compute capacity produces step throughput after exposed execution overhead. Statistical efficiency measures how effectively processed samples advance learning toward the target quality. A global batch far beyond the critical-batch crossover can keep MFU high while added examples yield diminishing reductions in update count. This can increase the examples and allocated GPU-hours needed to reach the target. Whether wall-clock time also increases depends on achieved throughput and the measured steps to the same target; high throughput alone does not establish either a faster or slower complete run."}, {"section": "Model Parallelism", "type": "MCQ", "question": "In Megatron-LM style tensor parallelism for a transformer layer, how are the linear projections in the multi-head attention block arranged to minimize cross-device collective communication?", "choices": ["Both QKV and Output projections are column-parallel, requiring an AllGather after each projection", "Both QKV and Output projections are row-parallel, requiring an AllReduce before each projection", "The QKV projection is column-parallel and the Output projection is row-parallel, requiring only a single AllReduce at the end of the attention block", "The QKV projection is partitioned across pipeline stages while the Output projection is replicated across data-parallel ranks"], "answer": "The correct answer is C. In Megatron-LM, the first linear layer (QKV projection) is column-parallel ($W = [W_1 | W_2]$), producing partitioned intermediate activations without communication. The second linear layer (Output projection) is row-parallel ($W = [W_1 ; W_2]$), taking those partitioned activations directly as inputs and generating partial sums. Only a single AllReduce at the end of the row-parallel projection is required to sum the partial outputs. Using column-parallel for both layers would require an intermediate AllGather. Using row-parallel for both layers would require an AllReduce before the second layer. Splitting QKV and Output across pipeline stages confuses intra-layer tensor parallelism with inter-layer pipelining."}, {"section": "Model Parallelism", "type": "SHORT", "question": "In a synchronous 1F1B pipeline-parallel training schedule with $p=8$ stages and $m=32$ microbatches, calculate the theoretical pipeline bubble fraction and explain how increasing the microbatch count $m$ affects bubble overhead and activation memory.", "choices": [], "answer": "The bubble fraction is $F_{\\text{bubble}} = \\frac{p-1}{m+p-1} = \\frac{8-1}{32+8-1} = \\frac{7}{39} \\approx 17.9\\%$. Increasing the microbatch count $m$ relative to stage count $p$ drives the bubble fraction down toward zero ($F_{\\text{bubble}} \\to 0$). Under 1F1B scheduling, with equal activation sizes per microbatch, peak activation memory per stage is bounded at $\\mathcal{O}(p)$ rather than GPipe's $\\mathcal{O}(m)$, making larger microbatch counts practical."}, {"section": "Hybrid Parallelism", "type": "MCQ", "question": "In a 3D parallelism layout whose device count is the product of the data-, pipeline-, and tensor-parallel degrees, what hardware-matching principle dictates mapping tensor parallelism intra-node while pipeline parallelism spans inter-node links?", "choices": ["Pipeline parallelism requires more memory bandwidth than NVLink can provide, forcing it onto InfiniBand", "Tensor parallelism requires only point-to-point transfers, making it insensitive to link latency", "Hierarchy-aware parallelism maps frequent TP exchanges to the fastest local fabric and PP boundary transfers to slower inter-node links.", "Data parallelism cannot execute across nodes that use tensor parallelism internally"], "answer": "The correct answer is C. Tensor parallelism communicates inside each layer, so its exposed latency strongly favors the fastest local fabric. Pipeline parallelism exchanges boundary activations and gradients between stages and can tolerate slower links when enough compute separates transfers. Data parallelism can wrap both dimensions. This is bandwidth matching, not a universal minimum bandwidth or prohibition on inter-node tensor parallelism."}, {"section": "Hybrid Parallelism", "type": "SHORT", "question": "A 175B-parameter model uses mixed-precision Adam with 2-byte weights, 2-byte gradients, a 4-byte master copy, and two 4-byte moments per parameter. With tensor degree $t=8$ and pipeline degree $p=16$, calculate the ideally balanced static memory per GPU before data-parallel state sharding, and explain why tensor parallelism alone cannot fit this state on 80 GB GPUs.", "choices": [], "answer": "The 16-byte-per-parameter state totals $175\\times10^9\\times16=2{,}800$ GB. Tensor parallelism alone leaves $2{,}800/8=350$ GB per GPU, exceeding 80 GB. Adding 16 balanced pipeline stages reduces the static state to $2{,}800/(8\\times16)=21.875$ GB per GPU. Activations, temporary parameter gathers, communication buffers, and uneven layer placement require additional headroom."}]};
